\id PSA - Brenton English Septuagint 
\h Psalms 
\toc1 Psalms 
\toc2 Psalms 
\toc3 Ps. 
\mt1 PSALMS  
\c 1
\ms BOOK I
\s1 The Two Paths
\q1 \v 1 \sc Blessed\sc* is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly,
\q1 and has not stood in the way of sinners,
\q2 and has not sat in the seat of evil men.\f + \fr 1:1 \fqa Gr. \ft pestilent.\f*
\q1 \v 2 But his pleasure\f + \fr 1:2 \fqa Gr. \ft will.\f* is in the law of the Lord;
\q2 and in his law will he meditate day and night.
\q1 \v 3 And he shall be as a tree planted by the brooks of waters,
\q2 which shall yield its fruit in its season,
\q1 and its leaf shall not fall off;
\q2 and whatever he shall do shall be prospered.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Not so the ungodly;—not so:
\q2 but rather as the chaff\f + \fr 1:4 \fqa Or, \ft dust or down.\f* which the wind scatters away from the face of the earth.
\q1 \v 5 Therefore the ungodly shall not rise in judgment,
\q2 nor sinners in the counsel of the just.
\b
\q1 \v 6 For the Lord knows the way of the righteous;
\q2 but the way of the ungodly shall perish.
\c 2
\s1 The Triumphant Messiah
\q1 \v 1 \x + \xo 2:1 \xt Acts 4. 25.\x* Why did the heathen rage,
\q2 and the nations imagine vain things?
\q1 \v 2 The kings of the earth stood up,
\q2 and the rulers gathered themselves together,
\q1 against the Lord,
\q2 and against his Christ;
\q1 \v 3 \add saying\add*, “Let us break through their bonds,
\q2 and cast away their yoke from us.”
\b
\q1 \v 4 He who dwells in the heavens shall laugh them to scorn,
\q2 and the Lord shall mock them.
\q1 \v 5 Then shall he speak to them in his anger,
\q2 and trouble them in his fury.
\q1 \v 6 But “I have been made king by him on Zion his holy mountain,
\b
\q1 \v 7 declaring the ordinance of the Lord”:
\q2 the Lord said to me, \x + \xo 2:7 \xt Acts 13. 33; Heb. 1. 5.\x* “You are my Son,
\q1 today have I begotten you.
\q1 \v 8 Ask of me, and I will give you the heathen \add for\add* yours inheritance,
\q2 and the ends of the earth \add for\add* your possession.
\q1 \v 9 You shall rule them\f + \fr 2:9 \fqa Gr. \ft tend them as a shepherd. \xt Rev. 2. 27.\f* with a rod of iron;
\q2 you shall dash them in pieces as a potter's vessel.”
\b
\q1 \v 10 Now therefore understand, you kings:
\q2 be instructed, all you that judge the earth.
\q1 \v 11 Serve the Lord with fear,
\q2 and rejoice in him with trembling.
\q1 \v 12 \f + \fr 2:12 \fqa Note. \ft - This rendering or a similar one the Jews maintain.\f*Accept correction, lest at any time the Lord be angry,
\q2 and you should perish from the righteous way:
\q2 whenever his wrath shall be suddenly kindled,
\q1 blessed are all they who trust in him.
\c 3
\s1 Deliver Me, O LORD!
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David, when he fled from the presence of his son Absalom.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O Lord, why are they who afflict me multiplied?
\q2 many rise up against me.
\q1 \v 3 Many say concerning my soul,
\q2 “There is no deliverance for him in his God.” \f + \fr 3:3 \ft The word Διάψαλμα (Selah) has been rendered Pause, as most intelligible to the English reader.\f*Pause.
\q1 \v 4 But you, O Lord, art my helper:
\q2 my glory, and the one that lifts up my head.
\q1 \v 5 I cried to the Lord with my voice,
\q2 and he heard me out of his holy mountain. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 I lay down and slept;
\q2 I awaked; for the Lord will help me.
\q1 \v 7 I will not be afraid of ten thousands of people,
\q2 who beset me round about.
\b
\q1 \v 8 Arise, Lord;
\q2 deliver me, my God:
\q1 for you have smitten all who were without cause my enemies;
\q2 you have broken the teeth of sinners.
\q1 \v 9 Deliverance is the Lord's,
\q2 and your blessing is upon your people.
\c 4
\s1 Answer Me When I Call!
\d \v 1 For the end, a Song of David among \add the \add*Psalms.
\b
\q1 \v 2 When I called upon \add him\add*,
\q2 the God of my righteousness heard me:
\q1 you have made room for me in tribulation;
\q2 pity me, and listen to my prayer.
\b
\q1 \v 3 O you sons of men, how long \add will you be\add* slow of heart?\f + \fr 4:3 \fqa Lit. \ft heavy of heart.\f*
\q2 why do you love vanity, and seek falsehood? Pause.
\q1 \v 4 But know you that the Lord has done wondrous things for his holy one:
\q2 the Lord will hear me when I cry to him.
\b
\q1 \v 5 \x + \xo 4:5 \xt Eph. 4. 26.\x* Be angry, and sin not;
\q2 feel compunction upon your beds for what you say in your hearts. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 Offer the sacrifice of righteousness,
\q2 and trust in the Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Many say, “Who will show us good things?”
\q2 the light of your countenance, O Lord, has been manifested towards us.
\q1 \v 8 You have put gladness into my heart:
\q2 they have been satisfied with the fruit of their corn and wine and oil.
\q1 \v 9 I will both lie down\f + \fr 4:9 \fqa Or, \ft at the Shuham time.\f* in peace and sleep:
\q2 for you, Lord, only have caused me to dwell securely.
\c 5
\s1 Give Ear to My Words
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, concerning her that inherits.
\q1 \v 2 Listen to my words, O Lord,
\q2 attend to my cry.\f + \fr 5:2 \fqa Lit. \ft understand.\f*
\q1 \v 3 Attend to the voice of my supplication,
\q2 my King, and my God:
\q2 for to you, O Lord, will I pray.
\b
\q1 \v 4 In the morning you shall hear my voice:
\q2 in the morning will I wait upon you,
\q2 and will look up.
\b
\q1 \v 5 For you are not a God that desires iniquity;
\q2 nor shall the worker of wickedness dwell with you.
\q1 \v 6 Nor shall the transgressors continue in your sight:
\q2 you hate, O Lord, all those who work iniquity.
\q1 \v 7 You will destroy all that speak falsehood:
\q2 the Lord abhors the bloody and deceitful man.
\b
\q1 \v 8 But I will enter into yours house
\q2 in the multitude of your mercy:
\q1 I will worship in your fear
\q2 toward your holy temple.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Lead me, O Lord, in your righteousness
\q2 because of my enemies;
\q2 make my way plain before your face.
\q1 \v 10 For there is no truth in their mouth;
\q2 their heart is vain;
\q1 their \x + \xo 5:10 \xt Rom. 3. 13.\x* throat is an open sepulchre;
\q2 with their tongues they have used deceit.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Judge them, O God;
\q2 let them fail of their counsels:\f + \fr 5:11 \fqa Gr. \ft fall from, \fqa or, \ft because of.\f*
\q1 cast them out according to the abundance of their ungodliness;
\q2 for they have provoked you, O Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 12 But let all that trust on you be glad in you:
\q2 they shall exult for ever,
\q1 and you shall dwell among them;
\q2 and all that love your name shall rejoice in you.
\q1 \v 13 For you, Lord, shall bless the righteous:
\q2 you have surrounded us\f + \fr 5:13 \fqa Lit. \ft crowned.\f* as with a shield of favour.
\c 6
\s1 Do Not Rebuke Me in Your Anger
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David among the Hymns for the eighth.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O Lord, rebuke me not in your wrath,
\q2 nor chasten me in your anger.
\q1 \v 3 Pity me, O Lord; for I am weak:
\q2 heal me, O Lord; for my bones are vexed.
\q1 \v 4 My soul also is grievously vexed:
\q2 but you, O Lord, how long?
\b
\q1 \v 5 Return, O Lord, deliver my soul:
\q2 save me for your mercy's sake.
\q1 \v 6 For in death no man remembers you:
\q2 and who will give you thanks in Hades?
\b
\q1 \v 7 I am wearied\f + \fr 6:7 \fqa Gr. \ft I have laboured.\f* with my groaning;
\q2 I shall wash my bed every night;
\q2 I shall water my couch with my tears.
\q1 \v 8 My eye is troubled because of my wrath;
\q2 I am worn out because of all my enemies.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Depart from me, all you that work iniquity;
\q2 for the Lord has heard the voice of my weeping.
\q1 \v 10 The Lord has hearkened to my petition;
\q2 the Lord has accepted my prayer.
\q1 \v 11 Let all my enemies be put to shame and sore troubled:
\q2 let them be turned back and grievously put to shame quickly.
\c 7
\s1 I Take Refuge in You
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David, which he sang to the Lord because of the words of Hushai the Benjamite.\f + \fr 7:1 \fqa Lit. \ft son of Jemini.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 2 O Lord my God, in you have I trusted:
\q2 save me from all those who persecute me, and deliver me.
\q1 \v 3 Lest at any time \add the enemy\add* seize my soul as a lion,
\q2 while there is none to ransom, nor to save.
\b
\q1 \v 4 O Lord my God, if I have done this;
\q2 (if there is unrighteousness in my hands;)
\q1 \v 5 if I have requited with evil those who requited me \add with good\add*;
\q2 may I then perish empty by means of my enemies.
\q1 \v 6 Let the enemy persecute my soul, and take it;
\q2 and let him trample my life on the ground,
\q2 and lay my glory in the dust. Pause.
\q1 \v 7 Arise, O Lord, in your wrath;
\q2 be exalted in the utmost\f + \fr 7:7 \fqa Heb. \ft 'because of my enemies.'\f* boundaries of my enemies:
\q2 awake, O Lord my God, according to the decree which you did command.
\q1 \v 8 And the congregation of the nations shall compass you:
\q2 and for this cause do you return on high.
\q1 \v 9 The Lord shall judge the nations:
\q2 judge me, O Lord, according to my righteousness,
\q2 and according to my innocence that is in me.
\q1 \v 10 Oh let the wickedness of sinners come to an end; and \add then\add* you shall direct the righteous,
\q2 O God that search the hearts and reins.
\b
\q1 \v 11 My help is righteous, \add coming\add*
\q2 from God who saves the upright in heart.
\q1 \v 12 God is a righteous judge,
\q2 and strong, and patient, not inflicting vengeance every day.
\q1 \v 13 If you will not repent,
\q2 he will furbish his sword;
\q2 he has bent his bow, and made it ready.
\q1 \v 14 And on it he has fitted the instruments of death;
\q2 he has completed his arrows for the raging ones.\f + \fr 7:14 \ft See \fqa Heb. \ft perhaps 'persecuting ones.'\f*
\b
\q1 \v 15 Behold, he has travailed with unrighteousness,
\q2 he has conceived trouble, and brought forth iniquity.
\q1 \v 16 He has opened a pit, and dug it up,
\q2 and he shall fall into the ditch which he has made.
\q1 \v 17 His trouble shall return on his own head,
\q2 and his unrighteousness shall come down on his own crown.
\b
\q1 \v 18 I will give thanks to the Lord according to his righteousness;
\q2 I will sing to the name of the Lord most high.
\c 8
\s1 How Majestic Is Your Name!
\d \v 1 For the end, concerning the wine-presses, a Psalm of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O Lord, our Lord,
\q2 how wonderful is your name in all the earth!
\q1 for your magnificence is exalted
\q2 above the heavens.
\q1 \v 3 \x + \xo 8:3 \xt Mat. 21. 16.\x* Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings
\q2 have you perfected praise,
\q1 because of yours enemies;
\q2 that you might put down the enemy and avenger.
\b
\q1 \v 4 For I will regard the heavens,
\q2 the work of your fingers;
\q1 the moon and stars,
\q2 which you have established.
\b
\q1 \v 5 \x + \xo 8:5 \xt Heb. 2. 6-9.\x* What is man, that you are mindful of him?
\q2 or the son of man, that you visit him?
\q1 \v 6 You made him a little less than angels,
\q2 you have crowned him with glory and honour;
\q1 \v 7 and you have set him over the works of your hands:
\q2 you have put all things under his feet:
\q1 \v 8 sheep and all oxen,\f + \fr 8:8 \fqa Gr. \ft cows.\f*
\q2 yes, and the cattle of the field;
\q1 \v 9 the birds of the sky, and the fish of the sea, the \add creatures\add* passing through the paths of the sea.
\b
\q1 \v 10 O Lord our Lord,
\q2 how wonderful is your name in all the earth!
\c 9
\s1 I Will Give Thanks to the LORD
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, concerning the secrets of the Son.
\b
\q1 \v 2 I will give thanks to you, O Lord, with my whole heart;
\q2 I will recount all your wonderful works.
\q1 \v 3 I will be glad and exult in you:
\q2 I will sing to your name, O you Most High.
\b
\q1 \v 4 When my enemies are turned back,
\q2 they shall be feeble and perish at your presence.
\q1 \v 5 For you have maintained my cause and my right;
\q2 you satest on the throne, that judge righteousness.
\q1 \v 6 You have rebuked the nations,
\q2 and the ungodly one has perished;
\q2 you have blotted out their name for ever, even for ever and ever.
\q1 \v 7 The swords of the enemy have failed utterly;
\q2 and you have destroyed cities:
\q2 their memorial has been destroyed with a noise,
\b
\q1 \v 8 but the Lord endures for ever:
\q2 he has prepared his throne for judgment.
\q1 \v 9 And he will judge the world in righteousness,
\q2 he will judge the nations in uprightness.
\q1 \v 10 The Lord also is become a refuge for the poor,
\q2 a seasonable help, in affliction.
\q1 \v 11 And let them who know your name hope in you:
\q2 for you, O Lord, have not failed them who diligently seek you.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Sing praises to the Lord, who dwells in Zion:
\q2 declare his dealings among the nations.
\q1 \v 13 For he remembered them, \add in\add* making inquisition for blood:
\q2 he has not forgotten the supplication of the poor.
\b
\q1 \v 14 Have mercy upon me, O Lord;
\q2 look upon my affliction \add which I suffer\add* of my enemies,
\q1 you that lift me up from the gates of death:
\q2 \v 15 that I may declare all your praises
\q1 in the gates of the daughter of Zion:
\q2 I will exult in your salvation.
\b
\q1 \v 16 The heathen are caught in the destruction which they planned:
\q2 in the very snare which they hid is their foot taken.
\q1 \v 17 The Lord is known as executing judgments:
\q2 the sinner is taken in the works of his hands. A song of Pause.
\q1 \v 18 Let sinners be driven away into Hades, \add even\add*
\q2 all the nations that forget God.
\q1 \v 19 For the poor shall not be forgotten for ever:
\q2 the patience of the needy ones shall not perish for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Arise, O Lord, let not man prevail:
\q2 let the heathen be judged before you.
\q1 \v 21 Appoint, O Lord, a lawgiver over them:
\q2 let the heathen know that they are men. Pause.
\s1 The Perils of the Pilgrim
\q1 \v 22 \f + \fr 9:22 \fqa Note.\ft —Here begins a change of numbers as compared with the \fqa Heb. \ft which continues to Ps. 146, this psalm having no number, and the eleventh being called the tenth. The following verses 22-39 are numbered 1-18 in the original text.\f*Why stand you afar off, O Lord? \add why\add*
\q2 dost you overlook \add us\add* in times of need, in affliction?
\q1 \v 23 While the ungodly one acts proudly, the poor is \f + \fr 9:23 \fqa Gr. \ft inflamed, as in a fever.\f*hotly pursued: \add the wicked\add*
\q2 are taken in the crafty counsels which they imagine.
\b
\q1 \v 24 Because the sinner praises himself for the desires of his heart;
\q2 and the unjust one blesses himself.
\q1 \v 25 The sinner has provoked the Lord:
\q2 according to the abundance of his \f + \fr 9:25 \fqa Or, \ft anger.\f*pride he will not seek after \add him\add*: God is not before him.
\q1 \v 26 His ways are \f + \fr 9:26 \fqa Gr. \ft profaned.\f*profane at all times;
\q2 your judgments are removed from before him:
\q2 he will gain the mastery over all his enemies.
\q1 \v 27 For he has said in his heart, “I shall not be moved, \add continuing\add*
\q2 without evil from generation to generation.” \x + \xo 9:27 \xt Rom. 8. 14.\x*
\b
\q1 \v 28 Whose mouth is full of cursing, and bitterness, and fraud:
\q2 under his tongue are trouble and pain.
\q1 \v 29 He lies in wait with rich \add men\add* in secret places,
\q2 in order to slay the innocent:
\q2 his eyes are \f + \fr 9:29 \fqa Gr. \ft look.\f*set against the poor.
\q1 \v 30 He lies in wait in secret as a lion in his den:
\q2 he lies in wait to ravish the poor,
\q2 to ravish the poor when he draws him \add after him\add*: he will bring him down in his snare.
\q1 \v 31 He will bow down
\q2 and fall when he has mastered the poor.
\q1 \v 32 For he has said in his heart, “God has forgotten:
\q2 he has turned away his face so as never to look.”
\b
\q1 \v 33 Arise, O Lord God; let your hand be lifted up:
\q2 forget not the poor.
\q1 \v 34 Why has the wicked provoked God?
\q2 for he has said in his heart, “He will not require \add it\add*.
\q1 \v 35 You see \add it\add*;
\q2 for you dost observe trouble and wrath, to deliver them into your hands:
\q1 the poor has been left to you;
\q2 you wast a helper to the orphan.
\q1 \v 36 Break you the arm of the sinner and wicked man:
\q2 his sin shall be sought for,
\q2 and shall not be found.
\b
\q1 \v 37 The Lord shall reign for ever, even for ever and ever:
\q2 you Gentiles shall perish out of his land.
\q1 \v 38 The Lord has heard the desire of the poor:
\q2 your ear has inclined to the preparation of their heart;
\q2 \v 39 to plead for the orphan and afflicted,
\q1 that man may no more boast
\q2 upon the earth.
\c 10
\s1 In the LORD I Take Refuge
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 In the Lord I have put my trust:
\q2 how will you say to my soul,
\q1 “Flee to the mountains as a sparrow?
\q2 \v 2 For behold the sinners have bent \add their\add* bow,
\q1 they have prepared their arrows for the quiver, to shoot secretly\f + \fr 10:2 \fqa Gr. \ft in dark moon, or little moonlight, \fqa q. d. \ft luce malignâ.\f* at the upright in heart.
\q1 \v 3 For they have pulled down what you did frame,
\q2 but what has the righteous done?”
\b
\q1 \v 4 The Lord is in his holy temple,
\q2 as for the Lord, his throne is in heaven:
\q1 his eyes look upon the poor,
\q2 his eyelids try the sons of men.
\q1 \v 5 The Lord tries the righteous and the ungodly:
\q2 and He who loves unrighteousness hates his own soul.
\q1 \v 6 He shall rain upon sinners snares, fire, and brimstone,
\q2 and a stormy blast \add shall be\add* the portion of their cup.
\b
\q1 \v 7 For the Lord \add is\add* righteous, and loves righteousness;\f + \fr 10:7 \fqa Gr. \ft righteousnesses.\f*
\q2 his face beholds uprightness.
\c 11
\s1 The Godly Are No More
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, upon the eighth.
\q1 \v 2 Save me, O Lord; for the godly man has failed;
\q2 for truth is diminished\f + \fr 11:2 \fqa Gr. \ft truths are become rare.\f* from among the children of men.
\q1 \v 3 Every one has spoken vanity to his neighbour:
\q2 their lips are deceitful, they have spoken with a double heart.
\q1 \v 4 Let the Lord destroy all the deceitful lips,
\q2 and the tongue that speaks great words:
\q1 \v 5 who have said, “We will magnify our tongue;
\q2 our lips are our own: who is Lord of us?”
\b
\q1 \v 6 Because of the misery of the poor,
\q2 and because of the sighing of the needy,
\q1 “now will I arise, saith the Lord, I will set \add them\add* in safety; I will speak \add to them\add* thereof openly.”
\b
\q1 \v 7 The oracles of the Lord are pure oracles;
\q2 as silver tried in the fire, proved \add in\add* a furnace of earth,\f + \fr 11:7 \fqa Gr. \ft the earth.\f*
\q2 purified seven times.
\q1 \v 8 You, O Lord, shall keep us,
\q2 and shall preserve us, from this generation, and for ever.
\q1 \v 9 The ungodly walk around:
\q2 according to your greatness you have greatly exalted the sons of men.
\c 12
\s1 How Long, O LORD?
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\q1 \v 2 How long, O Lord, will you forget me?
\q2 for ever?
\q2 how long will you turn away your face from me?
\q1 \v 3 How long shall I take counsel in my soul, \add having\add* sorrows in my heart daily?
\q2 how long shall my enemy be exalted over me?
\b
\q1 \v 4 Look on me, listen to me, O Lord my God:
\q2 lighten my eyes, lest I sleep in death;
\q1 \v 5 lest at any time my enemy say, “I have prevailed against him”:
\q2 my persecutors will exult if ever I should be moved.
\b
\q1 \v 6 But I have hoped in your mercy;
\q2 my heart shall exult in your salvation.
\q1 I will sing to the Lord who has dealt bountifully with me,
\q2 and I will sing psalms to the name of the Lord most high.
\c 13
\s1 The Fool Says There Is No God
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 The fool has said in his heart,
\q2 “There is no God.”
\q1 They have corrupted \add themselves\add*, and become abominable in their devices;
\q2 there is none that does goodness, there is not even so much as one.
\b
\q1 \v 2 The Lord looked down from heaven
\q2 upon the sons of men,
\q1 to see if there were any that understood,
\q2 or sought after God.
\q1 \v 3 They are all gone out of the way,
\q2 they are together become good for nothing,
\q1 there is none that does good,
\q2 no not one. Their throat is an open sepulchre; with their tongues they have used deceit; the poison of asps is under their lips: whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness; their feet are swift to shed blood: destruction and misery are in their ways; and the way of peace they have not known: there is no fear of God before their eyes.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Will not all the workers of iniquity know,
\q1 who eat up my people as they would eat bread?
\q2 they have not called upon the Lord.
\q1 \v 5 There were they alarmed with fear, where there was no fear;
\q2 for God is in the righteous generation.
\q1 \v 6 You have shamed the counsel of the poor,
\q2 because the Lord is his hope.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Who will bring the salvation of Israel out of Zion?
\q1 when the Lord brings back the captivity of his people,
\q2 let Jacob exult, and Israel be glad.
\c 14
\s1 Who May Dwell on Your Holy Mountain?
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 O Lord, who shall stay temporarily in your tabernacle?
\q2 and who shall dwell in your holy mountain?
\b
\q1 \v 2 He who walks blameless,
\q2 and works righteousness,
\q2 who speaks truth in his heart.
\q1 \v 3 Who has not spoken craftily with his tongue,
\q2 neither has done evil to his neighbour,
\q2 nor taken up a reproach against them who dwelt nearest to him.
\q1 \v 4 In his sight an evil-worker is set at nothing,
\q2 but he honours them who fear the Lord.
\q2 He swears to his neighbour, and disappoints \add him\add* not.
\q1 \v 5 He has not lent\f + \fr 14:5 \fqa Gr. \ft given.\f* his money on usury,
\q2 and has not received bribes against the innocent.
\q1 He who does these things
\q2 shall never be moved.
\c 15
\s1 The Presence of the LORD
\d \v 1 A writing of David.  
\p
\q1 Keep me, O Lord;
\q2 for I have hoped in you.
\b
\q1 \v 2 I said to the Lord, “You are my Lord;
\q2 for you have no need of my goodness.\f + \fr 15:2 \fqa Gr. \ft good deeds, or things.\f*
\q1 \v 3 On behalf of the saints that are in his land,
\q2 he has magnified all his pleasure in them.
\q1 \v 4 Their weaknesses have been multiplied;
\q2 afterward they hasted.
\q1 I will by no means assemble their bloody meetings,
\q2 nor will I make mention of their names with my lips.
\b
\q1 \v 5 The Lord is the portion of my inheritance and of my cup:
\q2 you are He who restores my inheritance to me.
\q1 \v 6 The lines have fallen to me in the best places,
\q2 yes, I have a most excellent heritage.
\q1 \v 7 I will bless the Lord who has instructed me;
\q2 my reins too have chastened me even till night.\f + \fr 15:7 \fqa Possibly, \ft during the night. \fqa q. d. \ft as long as night lasted.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 8 I foresaw the Lord always before my face;
\q2 for he is on my right hand, that I should not be moved.
\q1 \v 9 Therefore my heart rejoiced and my tongue exulted;
\q2 moreover also my flesh shall rest in hope:
\q1 \v 10 because you will not leave my soul in hell,
\q2 nor will you suffer yours Holy One to see corruption.
\q1 \v 11 You have made known to me the ways of life;
\q2 you will fill me with joy with your countenance:
\q2 at your right hand \add there are\add* delights for ever.
\c 16
\s1 Hear My Righteous Plea
\d \v 1 A Prayer of David.  
\p
\q1 Listen, O Lord of my righteousness,\f + \fr 16:1 \fqa Or, \ft to my righteous plea, etc.\f*
\q2 attend to my petition;
\q1 give ear to my prayer not \add uttered\add* with deceitful lips.
\q1 \v 2 Let my judgment come forth from your presence;
\q2 let my eyes behold righteousness.
\b
\q1 \v 3 You have proved my heart;
\q2 you have visited \add me\add* by night;
\q1 you have tried me as with fire, and unrighteousness has not been found in me: \add I am purposed\add*
\q2 that my mouth shall not speak \add amiss\add*.
\q1 \v 4 As for the works of men,
\q2 by the words of your lips I have \f + \fr 16:4 \fqa Lit. \ft I have observed hard ways.\f*guarded \add myself from\add* hard ways.
\q1 \v 5 Direct my steps in your paths,
\q2 that my steps slip not.
\b
\q1 \v 6 I have cried,
\q2 for you heard me, O God:
\q1 incline your ear to me,
\q2 and listen to my words.
\q1 \v 7 Show the marvels of your mercies,
\q2 you that save them who hope in you.
\q1 \v 8 Keep me as the apple of the eye from those that resist your right hand:
\q2 you shall screen me by the covering of your wings,
\q1 \v 9 from the face of the ungodly that have afflicted me:
\q2 my enemies have surrounded about my soul.
\b
\q1 \v 10 They have enclosed \add themselves with\add* their own fat:
\q2 their mouth has spoken pride.
\q1 \v 11 They have now cast me out and surrounded me round about:
\q2 they have set their eyes \add so as\add* to bow them down to the ground.
\q1 \v 12 They laid wait for me as a lion ready for prey,
\q2 and like a lion's whelp dwelling in secret \add places\add*.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Arise, O Lord, prevent them,
\q2 and cast them down:
\q1 deliver my soul from the ungodly: \add draw\add* your sword,
\q2 \v 14 because of the enemies of yours hand: O Lord,
\q1 destroy them from the earth;
\q2 scatter them in their life,
\q1 though their belly has been filled with your hidden \add treasures\add*: they have been satisfied with uncleanness,\f + \fr 16:14 \fqa Alex. \ft children.\f*
\q2 and have left the remnant \add of their possessions\add* to their babes.
\b
\q1 \v 15 But I shall appear in righteousness before your face:
\q2 I shall be satisfied when your glory appears.
\c 17
\s1 The LORD Is My Rock
\d \v 1 For the end, \add a Psalm\add* of David the servant of the Lord; \add the words\add* which he spoke to the Lord, \add even\add* the words of this Song, in the day in which the Lord delivered him out of the hand of all his enemies, and out of the hand of Saul: and he said:
\b
\q1 \v 2 I will love you, O Lord, my strength.
\b
\q1 \v 3 The Lord is my firm support,
\q2 and my refuge, and my deliverer;
\q1 my God is my helper, I will hope in him; \add he is\add* my defender,
\q2 and the horn of my salvation, and my helper.
\q1 \v 4 I will call upon the Lord with praises,
\q2 and I shall be saved from my enemies.
\b
\q1 \v 5 The pangs of death surrounded me,
\q2 and the torrents of ungodliness troubled me exceedingly.
\q1 \v 6 The pangs of hell came round about me:
\q2 the snares of death prevented me.
\q1 \v 7 And when I was afflicted I called upon the Lord,
\q2 and cried to my God:
\q1 he heard my voice out of his holy temple,
\q2 and my cry shall enter before him, \add even\add* into his ears.
\b
\q1 \v 8 Then the earth shook and quaked,
\q2 and the foundations of the mountains were disturbed,
\q2 and were shaken, because God was angry with them.
\q1 \v 9 There went up a smoke in his wrath,
\q2 and fire burst into a flame at his presence:
\q2 coals were kindled at it.
\q1 \v 10 And he bowed the heaven, and came down:
\q2 and thick darkness was under his feet.
\q1 \v 11 And he mounted on cherubs and flew:
\q2 he flew on the wings of winds.
\q1 \v 12 And he made darkness his secret place:
\q2 round about him was his tabernacle, \add even\add* dark water in the clouds of the air.
\q1 \v 13 At the brightness before him
\q2 the clouds passed,
\q2 hail and coals of fire.
\b
\q1 \v 14 The Lord also thundered from heaven,
\q2 and the Highest uttered his voice.
\q1 \v 15 And he sent forth \add his\add* weapons, and scattered them;
\q2 and multiplied lightnings, and routed them.
\q1 \v 16 And the springs of waters appeared,
\q2 and the foundations of the world were exposed,
\q1 at your rebuke, O Lord,
\q2 at the blasting of the breath of your wrath.
\b
\q1 \v 17 He sent from on high and took me,
\q2 he drew me to himself out of many waters.
\q1 \v 18 He will deliver me from my mighty enemies,
\q2 and from them who hate me; for they are stronger than I.
\q1 \v 19 They prevented me in the day of my affliction:
\q2 but the Lord was my stay against \add them\add*.
\q1 \v 20 And he brought me out into a wide place:
\q2 he will deliver me, because he has pleasure in me.
\b
\q1 \v 21 And the Lord will recompense me according to my righteousness;
\q2 even according to the purity of my hands will he recompense me.
\q1 \v 22 For I have kept the ways of the Lord
\q2 and have not wickedly departed from my God.
\q1 \v 23 For all his judgments were before me,
\q2 and his ordinances departed not from me.
\q1 \v 24 And I shall be blameless with him,
\q2 and shall keep myself from my iniquity.
\q1 \v 25 And the Lord shall recompense me according to my righteousness,
\q2 and according to the purity of my hands before his eyes.
\b
\q1 \v 26 With the holy you will be holy;
\q2 and with the innocent man you will be innocent.
\q1 \v 27 And with the excellent \add man\add* you will be excellent;
\q2 and with the perverse you will show frowardness.
\q1 \v 28 For you will save the lowly people,
\q2 and will humble the eyes of the proud.
\b
\q1 \v 29 For you, O Lord, will light my lamp:
\q2 my God, you will lighten my darkness.
\q1 \v 30 For by you shall I be delivered from a troop;
\q2 and by my God I will pass over a wall.
\q1 \v 31 \add As for\add* my God, his way is perfect:
\q2 the oracles of the Lord are tried in the fire;
\q2 he is a protector of all those who hope in him.
\b
\q1 \v 32 For who is God but the Lord?
\q2 and who is a God except our God?
\q1 \v 33 \add It is\add* God that girds me with strength,
\q2 and has made my way blameless:
\q1 \v 34 who strengthens my feet as hart's feet,
\q2 and sets me upon high places.
\q1 \v 35 He instructs my hands for war;
\q2 and you have made my arms \add as\add* a brazen bow.
\b
\q1 \v 36 And you have made me secure\f + \fr 17:36 \fqa Gr. \ft the protection of.\f* in my salvation:
\q2 and your right hand has helped me, and your correction has upheld me to the end;
\q2 yes, your correction itself shall instruct me.
\q1 \v 37 You have made room for my goings under me,
\q2 and my footsteps did not fail.
\q1 \v 38 I will pursue my enemies, and overtake them;
\q2 and I will not turn back until they are consumed.
\q1 \v 39 I will dash them to pieces and they shall not be able to stand:
\q2 they shall fall under my feet.
\b
\q1 \v 40 For you have girded me with strength for war:
\q2 you have beaten down under me all that rose up against me.
\q1 \v 41 And you have made my enemies turn their backs before me;
\q2 and you have destroyed them who hated me.
\q1 \v 42 They cried, but there was no deliverer: \add even\add* to the Lord, but he hearkened not to them.
\q1 \v 43 I will grind them as the mud of the streets:
\q2 and I will beat them small as dust before the wind.
\b
\q1 \v 44 Deliver me from the gainsayings of the people:
\q2 you shall make me head of the Gentiles:
\q2 a people whom I knew not served me,
\q1 \v 45 at the hearing of the ear they obeyed me:
\q2 the strange children lied\f + \fr 17:45 \fqa Or, \ft feigned obedience.\f* to me.
\q1 \v 46 The strange children waxed old,
\q2 and fell away from their paths through lameness.
\b
\q1 \v 47 The Lord lives; and blessed \add be\add* my God;
\q2 and let the God of my salvation be exalted.
\q1 \v 48 \add It is\add* God that avenges me,
\q2 and has subdued the nations under me;
\q2 \v 49 my deliverer from angry enemies:
\q1 you shall set me on high above them\f + \fr 17:49 \fqa Or, \ft out of the way of.\f* that rise up against me:
\q2 you shall deliver me from the unrighteous man.
\q1 \v 50 Therefore will I confess to you, O Lord, among the Gentiles,
\q2 and sing to your name.
\b
\q1 \v 51 \add God\add* magnifies the deliverances of his king;
\q2 and deals mercifully with David his anointed,
\q2 and his seed, for ever.
\c 18
\s1 The Heavens Declare the Glory of God
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\q1 \v 2 The heavens declare the glory of God;
\q2 and the firmament proclaims the work of his hands.
\q1 \v 3 Day to day utters speech,\f + \fr 18:3 \fqa Gr. \ft eructat.\f*
\q2 and night to night proclaims knowledge.
\q1 \v 4 There are no speeches or words, in which\f + \fr 18:4 \fqa Gr. \ft of which.\f* their voices are not heard.
\q1 \v 5 \x + \xo 18:5 \xt Rom. 10. 18.\x* Their voice is gone out into all the earth,
\q2 and their words to the ends of the world.
\b
\q1 \v 6 In the sun he has set his tabernacle;
\q2 and he comes forth as a bridegroom out of his chamber:
\q1 he will exult as a giant
\q2 to run his course.
\q1 \v 7 His going forth is from the extremity of heaven, and his circuit\f + \fr 18:7 \fqa Gr. \ft meeting.\f* to the \add other\add* end of heaven:
\q2 and no one shall be hidden from his heat.
\b
\q1 \v 8 The law of the Lord is perfect,\f + \fr 18:8 \fqa Gr. \ft spotless.\f*
\q2 converting souls:
\q1 the testimony of the Lord is faithful,
\q2 instructing babes.
\q1 \v 9 The ordinances of the Lord are right,
\q2 rejoicing the heart:
\q1 the commandment of the Lord is bright,
\q2 enlightening the eyes.
\q1 \v 10 The fear of the Lord is pure,
\q2 enduring for ever and ever:
\q1 the judgments of the Lord are true, \add and\add*
\q2 justified altogether.
\q1 \v 11 To be desired more than gold,
\q2 and much precious stone:
\q1 sweeter also than honey
\q2 and the honey-comb.
\q1 \v 12 For your servant keeps to them:
\q2 in the keeping of them \add there is\add* great reward.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Who will understand \add his\add* transgressions?
\q2 purge you me from my secret \add sins\add*.
\q1 \v 14 And spare your servant \add the attack\add* of strangers:
\q2 if they do not gain the dominion over me,
\q1 then shall I be blameless,
\q2 and I shall be clear from great sin.
\q1 \v 15 So shall the sayings of my mouth,
\q2 and the meditation of my heart,
\q1 be pleasing continually before you,
\q2 O Lord my helper, and my redeemer.
\c 19
\s1 The Day of Trouble
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 The Lord hear you in the day of trouble;
\q2 the name of the God of Jacob defend you.
\q1 \v 3 Send you help from the sanctuary,
\q2 and aid you out of Zion.
\q1 \v 4 Remember all your sacrifice,\f + \fr 19:4 \fqa Or, \ft meat offering.\f*
\q2 and enrich your whole-burnt-offering. Pause.
\q1 \v 5 Grant you according to your heart,
\q2 and fulfil all your desire.
\q1 \v 6 We will exult in your salvation,
\q2 and in the name of our God shall we be magnified:
\q2 the Lord fulfil all your petitions.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Now I know that the Lord has saved his Christ:
\q2 he shall hear him from his holy heaven:
\q2 the salvation of his right hand is mighty.
\q1 \v 8 Shammah \add glory\add* in chariots, and Shammah in horses:
\q2 but we will glory in the name\f + \fr 19:8 \fqa Gr. \ft be magnified.\f* of the Lord our God.
\q1 \v 9 They are overthrown and fallen:
\q2 but we are risen, and have been set upright.
\q1 \v 10 O Lord, save the king:
\q2 and hear us in whatever day we call upon you.
\c 20
\s1 After the Battle
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O Lord, the king shall rejoice in your strength;
\q2 and in your salvation he shall greatly exult.
\q1 \v 3 You have granted him the desire of his soul,
\q2 and have not withheld from him the request of his lips. Pause.
\q1 \v 4 For you have prevented him with blessings of goodness:
\q2 you have set upon his head a crown of precious stone.
\q1 \v 5 He asked life of you, and you gave him
\q2 length of days for ever and ever.
\b
\q1 \v 6 His glory is great in your salvation:
\q2 you will crown him with glory and majesty.
\q1 \v 7 For you will give him a blessing for ever and ever:
\q2 you will gladden him with joy with your countenance.
\q1 \v 8 For the king trusts in the Lord,
\q2 and through the mercy of the Highest
\q2 he shall not be moved.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Let your hand be found by all yours enemies:
\q2 let your right hand find all that hate you.
\q1 \v 10 You shall make them as a fiery oven
\q2 at the time of your presence:
\q1 the Lord shall trouble them in his anger,
\q2 and fire shall devour them.
\q1 \v 11 You shall destroy their fruit from the earth,
\q2 and their seed from \add among\add* the sons of men.
\b
\q1 \v 12 For they intended evils against you;
\q2 they imagined a device which they shall by no means be able to perform.
\q1 \v 13 For you shall make them \add turn their\add* back in your latter end,
\q2 you will prepare their face.
\b
\q1 \v 14 Be you exalted, O Lord, in your strength:
\q2 we will sing and praise your mighty acts.
\c 21
\s1 The Psalm of the Cross
\d \v 1 For the end, concerning the morning aid, a Psalm of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 \x + \xo 21:2 \xt Mat. 27. 46.\x* O God, my God, attend to me:
\q2 why have you forsaken me?
\q1 the account of my transgressions is
\q2 far from my salvation.
\q1 \v 3 O my God, I will cry to you by day,
\q2 but you will not hear:
\q1 and by night, and \add it shall\add* not \add be accounted\add* for folly to me.
\b
\q1 \v 4 But you, the praise of Israel,
\q2 dwell in a sanctuary.
\q1 \v 5 Our fathers hoped in you;
\q2 they hoped, and you did deliver them.
\q1 \v 6 They cried to you, and were saved:
\q2 they hoped in you, and were not ashamed.
\b
\q1 \v 7 But I am a worm, and not a man;
\q2 a reproach of men, and scorn of the people.
\q1 \v 8 All that saw me mocked me:
\q2 they spoke with \add their\add* lips, they shook the head, \add saying\add*,
\q1 \v 9 “He hoped in the Lord:
\q2 let him deliver him,
\q1 let him save him,
\q2 because he takes pleasure in him.”
\b
\q1 \v 10 For you are He who drew me out of the womb;
\q2 my hope from my mother's breasts.
\q1 \v 11 I was cast on you from the womb:
\q2 you are my God from my mother's belly.
\q1 \v 12 Stand not aloof from me;
\q2 for affliction is near;
\q2 for there is no helper.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Many bullocks\f + \fr 21:13 \fqa Gr. \ft calves.\f* have surrounded me:
\q2 fat bulls have beset me round.
\q1 \v 14 They have opened their mouth against me,
\q2 as a ravening and roaring lion.
\q1 \v 15 I am poured out like water,
\q2 and all my bones are loosened:
\q1 my heart in the midst of my belly
\q2 is become like melting wax.
\q1 \v 16 My strength is dried up like a potsherd;
\q2 and my tongue is glued to my throat;
\q2 and you have brought me down to the dust of death.
\b
\q1 \v 17 For many dogs have surrounded me:
\q2 the assembly of the wicked doers has beset me round:
\q2 they pierced my hands and my feet.
\q1 \v 18 They counted all my bones;
\q2 and they observed and looked upon me.
\q1 \v 19 \x + \xo 21:19 \xt Mat. 27. 35.\x* They parted my garments \add among\add* themselves,
\q2 and cast lots upon my clothing.
\b
\q1 \v 20 But you, O Lord, remove not my help afar off:
\q2 be ready for my aid.
\q1 \v 21 Deliver my soul from the sword;
\q2 my only-begotten one from the power of the dog.
\q1 \v 22 Save me from the lion's mouth; and \add regard\add* my \f + \fr 21:22 \ft See \fqa Hebrew.\f*lowliness from the horns of the unicorns.
\b
\q1 \v 23 I will declare your name to my brothers:
\q2 in the midst of the church will I sing praise to you.
\q1 \v 24 You that fear the Lord, praise him;
\q2 all you seed of Jacob, glorify him:
\q2 let all the seed of Israel fear him.
\q1 \v 25 For he has not despised nor been angry at
\q2 the supplication of the poor;
\q1 nor turned away his face from me;
\q2 but when I cried to him, he heard me.
\b
\q1 \v 26 My praise is of you in the great congregation:
\q2 I will pay my vows before them who fear him.
\q1 \v 27 The poor shall eat and be satisfied;
\q2 and they shall praise the Lord that seek him:
\q2 their heart shall live for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 28 All the ends of the earth shall remember and turn to the Lord:
\q2 and all the kindreds of the nations shall worship before him.
\q1 \v 29 For the kingdom is the Lord's;
\q2 and he is the governor of the nations.
\q1 \v 30 All the fat ones of the earth have eaten and worshipped:
\q2 all that go down to the earth shall fall down before him:
\q2 my soul also lives to him.
\q1 \v 31 And my seed shall serve him:
\q2 the generation that is coming shall be reported to the Lord.
\q1 \v 32 And they shall report his righteousness
\q2 to the people who shall be born,
\q2 whom the Lord has made.
\c 22
\s1 The LORD Is My Shepherd
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 The Lord tends me as a shepherd, and I shall want nothing.
\q1 \v 2 In a place of green grass, there he has made me dwell:
\q2 he has nourished me by the water of rest.
\q1 \v 3 He has restored my soul:
\q2 he has guided me into the paths of righteousness, for his name's sake.
\q1 \v 4 Yes, even if I should walk in the midst of the shadow of death,
\q2 I will not be afraid of evils:
\q1 for you are with me;
\q2 your rod and your staff, these have comforted me.
\b
\q1 \v 5 You have prepared a table before me in presence of them who afflict me:
\q1 you have thoroughly anointed my head with oil;
\q2 and your cup cheers me like the best \add wine\add*.
\q1 \v 6 Your mercy also shall follow me all the days of my life:
\q1 and my dwelling \add shall be\add* in the house of the Lord for a very long time.
\c 23
\s1 The Earth Is the LORD’s
\d \v 1 A Psalm for David on the first day of the week.  
\p
\q1 The earth is the Lord's and the fulness thereof;
\q2 the world, and all that dwell in it.
\q1 \v 2 He has founded it upon the seas,
\q2 and prepared it upon the rivers.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Who shall go up to the mountain of the Lord,
\q2 and who shall stand in his holy place?
\q1 \v 4 He who is innocent in his hands and pure in his heart;
\q2 who has not lifted up his soul to vanity, nor sworn deceitfully to his neighbour.\f + \fr 23:4 \fqa Or, \ft against.\f*
\q1 \v 5 He shall receive a blessing from the Lord,
\q2 and mercy from God his Saviour.
\q1 \v 6 This is the generation of them who seek him,
\q2 that seek the face of the God of Jacob. Pause.
\q1 \v 7 Lift up your gates, you princes,
\q2 and be you lifted up, you everlasting doors;
\q2 and the king of glory shall come in.
\q1 \v 8 Who is this king of glory?
\q2 the Lord strong and mighty,
\q2 the Lord mighty in battle.
\q1 \v 9 Lift up your gates, you princes;
\q2 and be you lift up, you everlasting doors;
\q2 and the king of glory shall come in.
\q1 \v 10 Who is this king of glory?
\q2 The Lord of hosts,
\q2 he is this king of glory.
\c 24
\s1 To You I Lift Up My Soul
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 To you, O Lord, have I lifted up my soul.
\q2 \v 2 O my God, I have trusted in you:
\q1 let me not be confounded,
\q2 neither let my enemies laugh me to scorn.
\q1 \v 3 For none of them who wait on you shall in any wise be ashamed:
\q2 let them be ashamed that transgress without cause.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Show me your ways, O Lord;
\q2 and teach me your paths.
\q1 \v 5 Lead me in your truth, and teach me:
\q2 for you are God my Saviour:
\q2 and I have waited on you all the day.
\q1 \v 6 Remember your compassions, O Lord, and your mercies,
\q2 for they are from everlasting.
\q1 \v 7 Remember not the sins of my youth, nor \add my sins\add* of ignorance:
\q1 remember me according to your mercy,
\q2 for your goodness' sake, O Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 8 Good and upright is the Lord:
\q2 therefore will he instruct sinners in\f + \fr 24:8 \fqa sc. \ft as a law-giver.\f* \add the\add* way.
\q1 \v 9 The meek will he guide in judgment:
\q2 the meek will he teach his ways.
\q1 \v 10 All the ways of the Lord are mercy and truth
\q2 to them who seek his covenant and his testimonies.
\q1 \v 11 For your name's sake, O Lord,
\q2 do you also be merciful to my sin; for it is great.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Who is the man who fears the Lord?
\q2 he shall instruct him in the way which he has chosen.
\q1 \v 13 His soul shall dwell in prosperity;
\q2 and his seed shall inherit the earth.
\q1 \v 14 The Lord is the strength of them who fear him;
\q2 and his covenant is to manifest \f + \fr 24:14 \fqa Or, \ft 'it,' \fqa sc. \ft what has just been stated.\f*\add truth\add* to them.
\q1 \v 15 My eyes are continually to the Lord;
\q2 for he shall draw my feet out of the snare.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Look upon me, and have mercy upon me;
\q2 for I am an only child and poor.
\q1 \v 17 The afflictions of my heart have been multiplied;
\q2 deliver me from my distresses.
\q1 \v 18 Look upon my affliction and my trouble;
\q2 and forgive all my sins.
\q1 \v 19 Look upon my enemies; for they have been multiplied;
\q2 and they have hated me with unjust hatred.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Keep my soul, and deliver me:
\q2 let me not be ashamed;
\q2 for I have hoped in you.
\q1 \v 21 The harmless and upright joined themselves to me:
\q2 for I waited for you, O Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 22 Deliver Israel, O God,
\q2 out of all his afflictions.
\c 25
\s1 Vindicate Me, O LORD
\b
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David.  
\p
\q1 Judge me, O Lord;
\q1 for I have walked in my innocence:
\q2 and hoping in the Lord I shall not be moved.
\q1 \v 2 Prove me, O Lord, and try me;
\q2 purify as with fire my reins and my heart.
\q1 \v 3 For your mercy is before my eyes:
\q2 and I am well pleased with your truth.
\b
\q1 \v 4 I have not sat with the council of vanity,
\q2 and will not at all enter in with transgressors.
\q1 \v 5 I have hated the assembly of wicked doers;
\q2 and will not sit with ungodly \add men\add*.
\q1 \v 6 I will wash my hands in innocency,\f + \fr 25:6 \fqa Gr. \ft innocent \fqa things.\f*
\q2 and compass yours altar, O Lord:
\q1 \v 7 to hear the voice of praise,
\q2 and to declare all your wonderful works.
\b
\q1 \v 8 O Lord, I have loved the beauty of your house,
\q2 and the place of the tabernacle of your glory.
\q1 \v 9 Destroy not my soul together with the ungodly,
\q2 nor my life with bloody men:
\q1 \v 10 in whose hands \add are\add* iniquities, \add and\add*
\q2 their right hand is filled with bribes.
\b
\q1 \v 11 But I have walked in my innocence:
\q2 redeem me, and have mercy upon me.
\q1 \v 12 My foot stands in an even place:\f + \fr 25:12 \fqa Gr. \ft in evenness or uprightness.\f*
\q2 in the congregations will I bless you, O Lord.
\c 26
\s1 The LORD Is My Salvation
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David, before he was anointed.  
\p
\q1 The Lord is my light and my Saviour;
\q2 whom shall I fear?
\q1 the Lord is the defender of my life;
\q2 of whom shall I be afraid?
\q1 \v 2 When evil-doers drew near against me to eat up my flesh,
\q2 my persecutors and my enemies, they fainted and fell.
\q1 \v 3 Though an army should set itself in array against me,
\q2 my heart shall not be afraid:
\q1 though war should rise up against me,
\q2 in this am I confident.
\b
\q1 \v 4 One thing have I asked of the Lord,
\q2 this will I earnestly seek:
\q1 that I should dwell in the house of the Lord
\q2 all the days of my life,
\q1 that I should behold the fair beauty of the Lord,
\q2 and survey his temple.
\q1 \v 5 For in the day of my afflictions
\q2 he hid me in his tabernacle:
\q1 he sheltered me in the secret of his tabernacle;
\q2 he set me up on a rock.
\q1 \v 6 And now, behold, he has lifted up my head
\q2 over my enemies:
\q1 I went round and offered in his tabernacle the sacrifice of \f + \fr 26:6 \fqa Gr. \ft shouting.\f*joy;
\q2 I will sing, \f + \fr 26:6 \fqa Or, \ft and play on a psaltery.\f*even sing psalms to the Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Hear, O Lord, my voice which I have uttered aloud:
\q2 pity me, and listen to me.
\q1 \v 8 My heart said to you, “I have diligently sought your face”:
\q2 your face, O Lord, I will seek.
\q1 \v 9 Turn not your face away from me,
\q2 turn not you away from your servant in anger:
\q1 be you my helper,
\q2 forsake me not; and, O God my Saviour,
\q2 overlook me not.
\b
\q1 \v 10 For my father and my mother have forsaken me,
\q2 but the Lord has taken me to himself.
\q1 \v 11 Teach me, O Lord, in your way,
\q2 and guide me in a right path,
\q2 because of my enemies.
\q1 \v 12 Deliver me not over to the desire of them who afflict me;
\q2 for unjust witnesses have risen up against me,
\q2 and injustice has lied within herself.
\b
\q1 \v 13 I believe that I shall see
\q2 the \f + \fr 26:13 \fqa Gr. \ft good \fqa things.\f*goodness of the Lord
\q2 in the land of the living.
\q1 \v 14 Wait on the Lord:
\q2 be of good courage, and let your heart be strengthened:
\q1 yes, wait on the Lord.
\c 27
\s1 The LORD Is My Strength
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David.  
\p
\q1 To you, O Lord, have I cried;
\q2 my God, be not silent toward me:
\q1 lest you be silent toward me,
\q2 and so I should be likened to them who go down to the pit.
\q1 \v 2 Listen to the voice of my supplication,
\q2 when I pray to you,
\q1 when I lift up my hands
\q2 toward your holy temple.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Draw not away my soul with sinners,
\q2 and destroy me not with the workers of iniquity,
\q1 who speak peace with their neighbours,
\q2 but evils are in their hearts.
\q1 \v 4 Give them according to their works,
\q2 and according to the wickedness of their devices:
\q1 give them according to the works of their hands;
\q2 render their repayment to them.
\q1 \v 5 Because they have not attended to the works of the Lord,
\q2 even to the works of his hands,
\q1 you shall pull them down,
\q2 and shall not build them up.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Blessed be the Lord,
\q2 for he has hearkened to the voice of my petition.
\q1 \v 7 The Lord is my helper and my defender;
\q2 my heart has hoped in him, and I am helped:
\q1 my flesh has revived,
\q2 and willingly will I \f + \fr 27:7 \fqa Or, \ft confess to him.\f*give praise to him.
\b
\q1 \v 8 The Lord is the strength of his people,
\q2 and the \f + \fr 27:8 \fqa Gr. \ft defender of the salvation of.\f*saving defender of his anointed.
\q1 \v 9 Save your people, and bless yours inheritance:
\q2 and take care of them, and lift them up for ever.
\c 28
\s1 Ascribe Glory to the LORD
\b
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David \add on the occasion\add* of the solemn assembly of the Tabernacle.  
\p
\q1 Bring to the Lord, you sons of God, bring to the Lord young rams;
\q2 bring to the Lord glory and honour.
\q1 \v 2 Bring to the Lord glory, \add due\add* to his name;
\q2 worship the Lord in his holy court.
\b
\q1 \v 3 The voice of
\q2 the Lord is upon the waters:
\q2 the God of glory has thundered: the Lord is upon many waters.
\q1 \v 4 The voice of the Lord is mighty;
\q2 the voice of the Lord is full of majesty.
\q1 \v 5 \add There is\add* the voice of the Lord who breaks the cedars;
\q2 the Lord will break the cedars of Lebanon.
\q1 \v 6 And he will beat them small, \add even\add* Lebanon itself, like a calf;
\q2 and the beloved one is as a young unicorn.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \add There is\add* a voice of the Lord
\q2 who divides a flame of fire.
\q1 \v 8 A voice of the Lord who shakes the wilderness;
\q2 the Lord will shake the wilderness of Cades.
\q1 \v 9 The voice of the Lord strengthens the hinds,\f + \fr 28:9 \fqa Gr. \ft participle.\f*
\q2 and will uncover the thickets:
\q2 and in his temple every one speaks \add of his\add* glory.
\b
\q1 \v 10 The Lord will dwell on the waterflood:
\q2 and the Lord will sit a king for ever.
\q1 \v 11 The Lord will give strength to his people;
\q2 the Lord will bless his people with peace.
\c 29
\s1 You Turned My Mourning into Dancing
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm and Song at\f + \fr 29:1 \fqa Gr. \ft of a song.\f* the dedication of the house of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 I will exalt you, O Lord;
\q2 for you have lifted me up,
\q1 and not caused my enemies
\q2 to rejoice over me.
\q1 \v 3 O Lord my God, I cried to you,
\q2 and you did heal me.
\q1 \v 4 O Lord, you have brought up my soul from Hades,
\q2 you have delivered me from \add among\add* them who go down to the pit.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Sing to the Lord, you his saints,
\q2 and give thanks for the remembrance of his holiness.
\q1 \v 6 For anger is in his wrath,
\q2 but life in his favour:
\q1 weeping shall wait for the evening,
\q2 but joy shall be in the morning.
\b
\q1 \v 7 And I said in my prosperity,
\q2 “I shall never be moved.”
\q1 \v 8 O Lord, in your good pleasure
\q2 you did add strength to my beauty:
\q1 but you did turn away your face,
\q2 and I was troubled.
\q1 \v 9 To you, O Lord, will I cry;
\q2 and to my God will I make supplication.
\q1 \v 10 “What profit is there in my blood,
\q2 when I go down to destruction?
\q1 Shall the dust give praise to you?
\q2 or shall it declare your truth?
\q1 \v 11 The Lord heard, and had compassion upon me;
\q2 the Lord is become my helper.”
\b
\q1 \v 12 You have turned my mourning into joy for me:
\q2 you have rent off my sackcloth, and girded me with gladness;
\q1 \v 13 that my glory may sing praise to you, and I may not be pierced \add with sorrow\add*.
\q2 O Lord my God, I will give thanks to you for ever.
\c 30
\s1 Into Your Hands I Commit My Spirit
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, \add an utterance\add* of extreme fear.\f + \fr 30:1 \fqa Gr. \ft ecstasy. See title.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 2 O Lord, I have hoped in you;
\q2 let me never be ashamed:
\q2 deliver me in your righteousness and rescue me.
\q1 \v 3 Incline your ear to me;
\q2 hurry to rescue me:
\q1 be you to me for a protecting God,
\q2 and for a house of refuge to save me.
\b
\q1 \v 4 For you are my strength and my refuge;
\q2 and you shall guide me for your name's sake, and maintain me.
\q1 \v 5 You shall bring me out of the snare which they have hidden for me;
\q2 for you, O Lord, art my defender.
\q1 \v 6 Into yours hands I will commit my spirit:
\q2 you have redeemed me, O Lord God of truth.
\b
\q1 \v 7 You have hated them who idly persist in vanities:
\q2 but I have hoped in the Lord.
\q1 \v 8 I will exult and be glad in your mercy:
\q2 for you have looked upon my affliction;
\q2 you have saved my soul from distresses.
\q1 \v 9 And you have not shut me up into the hands of the enemy:
\q2 you have set my feet in a wide place.
\b
\q1 \v 10 Pity me, O Lord,
\q2 for I am afflicted:
\q1 my eye is troubled with indignation,
\q2 my soul and my belly.
\q1 \v 11 For my life is spent with grief,
\q2 and my years with groanings:
\q1 my strength has been weakened through poverty,
\q2 and my bones are troubled.
\q1 \v 12 I became a reproach among all my enemies,
\q2 but exceedingly so to my neighbours,
\q1 and a fear to my acquaintance:
\q2 they who saw me without fled from me.
\q1 \v 13 I have been forgotten as a dead man out of mind:
\q2 I am become as a broken vessel.
\q1 \v 14 For I heard the slander of many
\q2 that dwelt round about:
\q1 when they were gathered together against me,
\q2 they took counsel to take my life.
\b
\q1 \v 15 But I hoped in you, O Lord:
\q2 I said, “You are my God.”
\q1 \v 16 My lots are in your hands:
\q2 deliver me from the hand of my enemies,
\q2 \v 17 and from them who persecute me.
\q1 Make your face to shine upon your servant:
\q2 save me in your mercy.
\b
\q1 \v 18 O Lord, let me not be ashamed,
\q2 for I have called upon you:
\q1 let the ungodly be ashamed,
\q2 and brought down to Hades.
\q1 \v 19 Let the deceitful lips become dumb,
\q2 which speak iniquity against the righteous
\q2 with pride and scorn.
\b
\q1 \v 20 How abundant is the multitude of your goodness, O Lord,
\q2 which you have laid up for them who fear you!
\q1 you have worked \add it\add* out for them who hope on you,
\q2 in the presence of the sons of men.
\q1 \v 21 You will hide them in the secret of your presence
\q2 from the vexation of man:
\q1 you will screen them in a tabernacle
\q2 from the contradiction of tongues.
\b
\q1 \v 22 Blessed be the Lord:
\q2 for he has magnified his mercy
\q2 in a fortified city.
\q1 \v 23 But I said in my extreme fear,
\q2 “I am cast out from the sight of your eyes”:
\q1 therefore you did listen, O Lord, to the voice of my supplication
\q2 when I cried to you.
\b
\q1 \v 24 Love the Lord, all you his saints:
\q2 for the Lord seeks for truth, and renders \add a reward\add* to them who deal very proudly.
\q1 \v 25 Be of good courage,
\q2 and let your heart be strengthened,
\q1 all you that hope in the Lord.
\c 31
\s1 The Joy of Forgiveness
\b
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of instruction by David.\f + \fr 31:1 \fqa Gr. \ft for \fqa or, \ft of.\f*  
\p \x + \xo 31:1 \xt Rom. 4. 7,8.\x* Blessed \add are they\add* whose transgressions are forgiven,
\q2 and whose sins are covered.
\q1 \v 2 Blessed is the man to whom the Lord will not impute sin,
\q2 and in whose mouth there is no guile.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Because I kept silence, my bones waxed old,
\q2 from my crying all the day.
\q1 \v 4 For day and night
\q2 your hand was heavy upon me:
\q1 I became thoroughly miserable
\q2 while a \f + \fr 31:4 \ft See \fqa Heb.\f*thorn was fastened in \add me\add*. Pause.
\q1 \v 5 I acknowledged my sin,
\q2 and hid not my iniquity:
\q1 I said, “I will confess my iniquity to the Lord against myself”;
\q2 and you forgave the ungodliness of my heart. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 Therefore shall every holy one pray to you
\q2 in a fit time:
\q1 only in the deluge of many waters
\q2 they shall not come near to him.
\q1 \v 7 You are my refuge
\q2 from the affliction that encompasses me;
\q2 my joy, to deliver me from them who have surrounded me. Pause.
\q1 \v 8 I will instruct you and guide you in this way wherein you shall go:
\q2 I will fix my eyes upon you.
\q1 \v 9 Don't you be as horse and mule,
\q2 which have no understanding: \f + \fr 31:9 \ft See \fqa Alex. \ft ἄγξεις.\f*\add
\q1 but you must\add* constrain their jaws with bit and curb,
\q2 lest they should come near to you.
\q1 \v 10 Many are the scourges of the sinner:
\q2 but him that hopes in the Lord mercy shall compass about.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Be glad in the Lord, and exult, you righteous:
\q2 and glory, all you that are upright in heart.
\c 32
\s1 Praise to the Creator
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David.  
\p
\q1 Rejoice in the Lord, you righteous;
\q2 praise becomes the upright.
\q1 \v 2 Praise the Lord\f + \fr 32:2 \ft Rather, 'confess' \fqa or \ft give thanks to.'\f* on the harp;
\q2 play to him on a psaltery of ten strings.
\q1 \v 3 Sing to him a new song;
\q2 play skilfully with a loud noise.
\b
\q1 \v 4 For the word of the Lord is right;
\q2 and all his works are \f + \fr 32:4 \ft Rather, \fqa Gr. \ft in \fqa or \ft with faithfulness.\f*faithful.
\q1 \v 5 He loves mercy and judgment;
\q2 the earth is full of the mercy of the Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 6 By the word of the Lord the heavens were established;
\q2 and all the host of them by the breath of his \add mouth\add*.
\q1 \v 7 Who gathers the waters of the sea as \add in\add* a bottle;
\q2 who lays up the deeps in treasuries.
\q1 \v 8 Let all the earth fear the Lord;
\q2 and let all that dwell in the world be moved because of him.
\q1 \v 9 For he spoke, and they were made;
\q2 he commanded, and they were created.
\b
\q1 \v 10 The Lord frustrates the counsels of the nations;
\q2 he brings to nothing also the reasonings of the peoples, and brings to nothing the counsels of princes.
\q1 \v 11 But the counsel of the Lord endures for ever,
\q2 the thoughts of his heart from generation to generation.
\q1 \v 12 Blessed is the nation whose God is the Lord;
\q2 the people whom he has chosen for his own inheritance.
\b
\q1 \v 13 The Lord looks out of heaven;
\q2 he beholds all the sons of men.
\q1 \v 14 He looks from his prepared habitation
\q2 on all the dwellers on the earth;
\q1 \v 15 who fashioned their hearts \f + \fr 32:15 \ft Perhaps 'individually.' \fqa Vulg. \ft sigillatim.\f*alone;
\q2 who understands all their works.
\q1 \v 16 A king is not saved because of a great host;
\q2 and a giant shall not be delivered by the greatness of his strength.
\q1 \v 17 A horse is vain for safety;
\q2 nor shall he be delivered by the greatness of his power.
\b
\q1 \v 18 Behold, the eyes of the Lord are on them who fear him,
\q2 those that hope in his mercy;
\q1 \v 19 to deliver their souls from death,
\q2 and to keep them alive in famine.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Our soul waits on the Lord;
\q2 for he is our helper and defender.
\q1 \v 21 For our heart shall rejoice in him,
\q2 and we have hoped in his holy name.
\q1 \v 22 Let your mercy, O Lord, be upon us,
\q2 according as we have hoped in you.
\c 33
\s1 Taste and See That the LORD Is Good
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David, when he changed his countenance before Abimelech; and he let him go, and he departed.
\b
\q1 \v 2 I will bless the Lord at all times:
\q2 his praise shall be continually in my mouth.
\q1 \v 3 My soul shall \f + \fr 33:3 \fqa Gr. \ft be praised.\f*boast herself in the Lord:
\q2 let the meek hear, and rejoice.
\q1 \v 4 Magnify you the Lord with me,
\q2 and let us exalt his name together.
\b
\q1 \v 5 I sought the Lord diligently, and he hearkened to me,
\q2 and delivered me from all my \f + \fr 33:5 \fqa Lit. \ft neighbourhoods.\f*sojournings.
\q1 \v 6 Draw near to him, and be enlightened:
\q2 and your faces shall not \add by any means\add* be ashamed.
\q1 \v 7 This poor man cried, and the Lord hearkened to him,
\q2 and delivered him out of all his afflictions.
\q1 \v 8 The angel of the Lord will camp round about them who fear him,
\q2 and will deliver them.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Taste and see that the Lord is good:
\q2 blessed is the man who hopes in him.
\q1 \v 10 Fear the Lord, all you his saints:
\q2 for there is no want to them who fear him.
\q1 \v 11 The rich have become poor and hungry:
\q2 but they who seek the Lord diligently shall not want any good thing. Pause.
\q1 \v 12 Come, you children, hear me:
\q2 I will teach you the fear of the Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \x + \xo 33:13 \xt 1 Pet. 3. 10-13.\x* What man is there that desires life,
\q2 loving to see good days?
\q1 \v 14 Keep your tongue from evil,
\q2 and your lips from speaking guile.
\q1 \v 15 Turn away from evil, and do good;
\q2 seek peace, and pursue it.
\q1 \v 16 The eyes of the Lord are over the righteous,
\q2 and his ears \add are open\add* to their prayer:
\q1 \v 17 but the face of the Lord is against them who do evil,
\q2 to destroy their memorial from the earth.
\q1 The righteous cried, and the Lord hearkened to them,
\q2 \v 18 and delivered them out of all their afflictions.
\q1 \v 19 The Lord is near to \f + \fr 33:19 \fqa Gr. \ft the broken in heart.\f*them that are of a contrite heart;
\q2 and will save the lowly in spirit.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Many are the afflictions of the righteous:
\q2 but out of them all the Lord will deliver them.
\q1 \v 21 He keeps all their bones:
\q2 not one of them shall be broken.
\b
\q1 \v 22 The death of sinners is evil:
\q2 and they who hate righteousness will go wrong.
\q1 \v 23 The Lord will redeem the souls of his servants:
\q2 and none of those that hope in him shall go wrong.
\c 34
\s1 Contend with My Opponents, O LORD
\b
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David.  
\p
\q1 Judge you, O Lord, them who injure me,
\q2 fight against them who fight against me.
\q1 \v 2 Take hold of shield and buckler,
\q2 and arise for my help.
\q1 \v 3 Bring forth a sword, and stop \add the way\add* against them who persecute me:
\q2 say to my soul, “I am your salvation.”
\b
\q1 \v 4 Let them who seek my soul be ashamed and confounded:
\q2 let them who devise evils against me be turned back and put to shame.
\q1 \v 5 Let them be as dust before the wind,
\q2 and an angel of the Lord afflicting them.
\q1 \v 6 Let their way be dark and slippery,
\q2 and an angel of the Lord persecuting them.
\q1 \v 7 For without cause they have hid for me their destructive snare:
\q2 without a cause they have reproached my soul.
\q1 \v 8 Let a snare which they know not come upon them:
\q2 and the gin which they hid take them;
\q2 and let them fall into the very Shuham snare.
\b
\q1 \v 9 But my soul shall exult in the Lord:
\q2 it shall delight in his salvation.
\q1 \v 10 All my bones shall say, “O Lord, who is like to you?
\q2 delivering the poor out of the hand of them who are stronger than he,
\q1 yes, the poor and needy one
\q2 from them who spoil him.”
\b
\q1 \v 11 Unjust witnesses arose,
\q2 and asked me of things I knew not.
\q1 \v 12 They rewarded me evil for good,
\q2 and bereavement to my soul.
\q1 \v 13 But I, when they troubled me,
\q2 put on sackcloth,
\q1 and humbled my soul with fasting:
\q2 and my prayer shall return to my \add own\add* bosom.
\q1 \v 14 I behaved agreeably towards them as \add if it had been\add* our neighbour \add or\add* brother:
\q1 I humbled myself as one mourning and
\q2 sad of countenance.
\q1 \v 15 Yet they rejoiced against me,
\q2 and plagues were \f + \fr 34:15 \ft The \fqa Gr. \ft is repeated, by a \fqa Hebraism.\f*plentifully brought against me,
\q1 and I knew \add it\add* not:
\q2 they were scattered, but \f + \fr 34:15 \ft See \xt Acts 2. 37.\f*repented not.
\q1 \v 16 They tempted me, they sneered at me most contemptuously,
\q2 they gnashed their teeth upon me.
\b
\q1 \v 17 O Lord, when will you look upon me?
\q2 Deliver my soul from their mischief,
\q2 my only-begotten one from the lions.
\q1 \v 18 I will give thanks to you even in a great congregation:
\q2 in an abundant people I will praise you.
\q1 \v 19 Let not them who are my enemies without a cause rejoice against me;
\q2 who hate me for nothing, and wink with their eyes.
\q1 \v 20 For to me they spoke peaceably,
\q2 but imagined deceits in \add their\add* anger.
\q1 \v 21 And they opened wide their mouth upon me;
\q2 they said, “Aha, aha, our eyes have seen \add it\add*.
\b
\q1 \v 22 You have seen \add it\add*, O Lord: keep not silence:
\q2 O Lord, withdraw not \add yourself\add* from me.
\q1 \v 23 Awake, O Lord, and attend to my judgment, \add even\add* to my cause, my God and my Lord.
\q1 \v 24 Judge me, O Lord, according to your righteousness, O Lord my God;
\q2 and let them not rejoice against me.
\q1 \v 25 Let them not say in their hearts, “Aha, aha, \add it is pleasing\add* to our soul”:
\q1 neither let them say,
\q2 “We have devoured him.”
\b
\q1 \v 26 Let them
\q2 be confounded and ashamed together that rejoice at my afflictions:
\q1 let them be clothed with shame and confusion
\q2 that speak great swelling words against me.
\q1 \v 27 Let them who rejoice in my righteousness
\q2 exult and be glad:
\q1 and let them say continually, “The Lord be magnified,
\q2 who desire the peace of his servant.”
\b
\q1 \v 28 And my tongue shall meditate on your righteousness, \add and\add* on your praise all the day.
\c 35  
\d
\v 1 For the end, by David the servant of the Lord.   
\p
\v 2 The transgressor, that he may sin, says within himself, \add that\add* \x + \xo 35:2 \xt Rom. 3. 18.\x* there is no fear of God before his eyes.  
\v 3 For he has dealt craftily before him, \f + \fr 35:3 \fqa q. d. \ft with regard to the discovering.\f*to discover his iniquity and hate it.  
\v 4 The words of his mouth are transgression and deceit: he is not inclined to understand \add how\add* to do good.  
\v 5 He devises iniquity on his bed; he gives himself to every evil way; and does not abhor evil.   
\p
\v 6 O Lord, your mercy is in the heaven; and your truth \add reaches\add* to the clouds.  
\v 7 Your righteousness is as the \f + \fr 35:7 \fqa Or, \ft vast mountains.\f*mountains of God, your judgments are as a great deep: O Lord, you will preserve men and beasts.  
\v 8 How have you multiplied your mercy, O God! so the children of men shall trust in the shelter of your wings.  
\v 9 They shall be \f + \fr 35:9 \fqa Gr. \ft intoxicated.\f*fully satisfied with the fatness of yours house; and you shall cause them to drink of the full stream of your delights.  
\v 10 For with you is the fountain of life: in your light we shall see light.   
\p
\v 11 Extend your mercy to them who know you; and your righteousness to the upright in heart.  
\v 12 Let not the foot of pride come against me, and let not the hand of sinners move me.   
\p
\v 13 There have all the workers of iniquity fallen: they are cast out, and shall not be able to stand.   
\c 36
\s1 Delight Yourself in the LORD
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David.  
\p
\q1 Fret not yourself because of evil-doers,
\q2 neither be envious of them who do iniquity.
\q1 \v 2 For they shall soon be withered as the grass,
\q2 and shall soon fall away as the green herbs.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Hope in the Lord, and do good;
\q2 and dwell on the land, and you shall be fed with the wealth of it.
\q1 \v 4 Delight \add yourself\add* in the Lord;
\q2 and he shall grant you the requests of yours heart.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Disclose your way to the Lord,
\q2 and hope in him; and he shall bring \add it\add* to pass.
\q1 \v 6 And he shall bring forth your righteousness as the light,
\q2 and your judgment as the noon-day.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Submit yourself to the Lord, and supplicate him:
\q2 fret not yourself because of him that prospers in his way, at the man who does unlawful deeds.
\b
\q1 \v 8 Cease from anger, and forsake wrath:
\q2 fret not yourself so as to do evil.
\q1 \v 9 For evil-doers shall be destroyed:
\q2 but they who wait on the Lord, they shall inherit the land.
\b
\q1 \v 10 And yet a little while, and the sinner shall not be,
\q2 and you shall seek for his place, and shall not find \add it\add*.
\q1 \v 11 But the meek shall inherit the earth;
\q2 and shall delight \add themselves\add* in the abundance of peace.
\b
\q1 \v 12 The sinner will watch for the righteous,
\q2 and gnash his teeth upon him.
\q1 \v 13 But the Lord shall laugh at him:
\q2 for he foresees that his day will come.
\b
\q1 \v 14 Sinners have drawn their swords,
\q2 they have bent their bow,
\q1 to cast down the poor and needy one, \add and\add*
\q2 to slay the upright in heart.
\q1 \v 15 Let their sword enter into their \add own\add* heart,
\q2 and their bows be broken.
\b
\q1 \v 16 A little is better to the righteous
\q2 than abundant wealth of sinners.
\q1 \v 17 For the arms of sinners shall be broken;
\q2 but the Lord supports the righteous.
\b
\q1 \v 18 The Lord knows the ways of the perfect;
\q2 and their inheritance shall be for ever.
\q1 \v 19 They shall not be ashamed in an evil time;
\q2 and in days of famine they shall be satisfied.
\b
\q1 \v 20 For the sinners shall perish;
\q2 and the enemies of the Lord at the moment of their being honoured and exalted
\q1 have utterly vanished
\q2 like smoke.
\b
\q1 \v 21 The sinner borrows, and will not pay again:
\q2 but the righteous has compassion, and gives.
\q1 \v 22 For they who bless him shall inherit the earth;
\q2 and they who curse him shall be utterly destroyed.
\b
\q1 \v 23 The steps of a man are rightly ordered by the Lord:
\q2 and he will take pleasure in his way.
\q1 \v 24 When he falls, he shall not be ruined:
\q2 for the Lord supports his hand.
\b
\q1 \v 25 I was \add once\add* young, indeed I am now old;
\q2 yet I have not seen the righteous forsaken,
\q2 nor his seed seeking bread.
\q1 \v 26 He is merciful, and lends continually;
\q2 and his seed shall be blessed.\f + \fr 36:26 \fqa Gr. \ft for a blessing.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 27 Turn aside from evil, and do good;
\q2 and dwell for ever.
\q1 \v 28 For the Lord loves judgment,
\q2 and will not forsake his saints;
\q1 they shall be preserved for ever: the blameless shall be avenged,\f + \fr 36:28 \fqa Or, \ft cleared in judgement.\f*
\q2 but the seed of the ungodly shall be utterly destroyed.
\q1 \v 29 But the righteous shall inherit the earth,
\q2 and dwell upon it for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 30 The mouth of the righteous will meditate wisdom,
\q2 and his tongue will speak of judgment.
\q1 \v 31 The law of his God is in his heart;
\q2 and his steps shall not slide.\f + \fr 36:31 \fqa Gr. \ft be tripped up, or supplanted.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 32 The sinner watches the righteous,
\q2 and seeks to slay him.
\q1 \v 33 But the Lord will not leave him in his hands,
\q2 nor by any means condemn him when he is judged.
\b
\q1 \v 34 Wait on the Lord, and keep his way,
\q2 and he shall exalt you to inherit the land:
\q1 when the wicked are destroyed,
\q2 you shall see \add it\add*.
\b
\q1 \v 35 I saw the ungodly very highly exalting himself,
\q2 and lifting himself up like the cedars of Lebanon.
\q1 \v 36 Yet I passed by, and lo! he was not:
\q2 and I sought him, but his place was not found.
\b
\q1 \v 37 Maintain innocence, and behold uprightness:
\q2 for there is a remnant to the peaceable man.
\q1 \v 38 But the transgressors shall be utterly destroyed together:
\q2 the remnants of the ungodly shall be utterly destroyed.
\b
\q1 \v 39 But the salvation of the righteous is of the Lord;
\q2 and he is their defender in the time of affliction.
\q1 \v 40 And the Lord shall help them, and deliver them:
\q2 and he shall rescue them from sinners, and save them,
\q2 because they have hoped in him.
\c 37
\s1 Do Not Rebuke Me in Your Anger
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David for remembrance concerning the Sabbath-day.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O Lord, rebuke me not in your wrath,
\q2 nor chasten me in your anger.
\q1 \v 3 For your weapons are fixed in me,
\q2 and you have pressed your hand heavily upon me.
\b
\q1 \v 4 For there is no health in my flesh because of your anger;
\q2 there is no peace to my bones because of my sins.
\q1 \v 5 For my transgressions have gone over my head:
\q2 they have pressed heavily upon me like a weighty burden.
\q1 \v 6 My bruises have become noisome and corrupt,
\q2 because of my foolishness.
\q1 \v 7 I have been wretched and bowed down continually:
\q2 I went with a mourning countenance all the day.
\q1 \v 8 For my soul is filled with mockings;
\q2 and there is no health in my flesh.
\q1 \v 9 I have been afflicted and brought down exceedingly:
\q2 I have roared for the groaning of my heart.
\b
\q1 \v 10 But all my desire is before you;
\q2 and my groaning is not hidden from you.
\q1 \v 11 My heart is troubled, my strength has failed me;
\q2 and the light of my eyes is not with me.
\q1 \v 12 My friends and my neighbours drew near before me, and stood still;
\q2 and my nearest of kin stood afar off.
\q1 \v 13 While they pressed hard upon me that sought my soul:
\q2 and they who sought my hurt spoke vanities,
\q2 and devised deceits all the day.
\b
\q1 \v 14 But I, as a deaf man, heard not;
\q2 and was as a dumb man not opening his mouth.
\q1 \v 15 And I was as a man who hears not,
\q2 and who has no reproofs in his mouth.
\q1 \v 16 For I hoped in you, O Lord:
\q2 you will hear, O Lord my God.
\q1 \v 17 For I said, “Lest my enemies rejoice against me:
\q2 for when my feet were moved, they spoke boastingly against me.”
\b
\q1 \v 18 For I am ready for plagues,\f + \fr 37:18 \fqa Gr. \ft scourges.\f*
\q2 and my grief is continually before me.
\q1 \v 19 For I will declare my iniquity,
\q2 and be distressed for my sin.
\q1 \v 20 But my enemies live, and are mightier than I:
\q2 and they who hate me unjustly are multiplied.
\q1 \v 21 They who reward evil for good slandered me;
\q2 because I followed righteousness.
\b
\q1 \v 22 Forsake me not, O Lord my God:
\q2 depart not from me.
\q1 \v 23 Draw near to my help,
\q2 O Lord of my salvation.
\c 38
\s1 I Will Watch My Ways
\d \v 1 For the end, a Song of David, to Jeduthun.
\q1 \v 2 I said, “I will take care to my ways,
\q2 that I sin not with my tongue:
\q1 I set a guard on my mouth,
\q2 while the sinner stood in my presence.”
\q1 \v 3 I was dumb, and humbled myself,
\q2 and kept silence from good \add words\add*;
\q2 and my grief was renewed.
\q1 \v 4 My heart grew hot within me,
\q2 and a fire would kindle in my meditation: I spoke with my tongue,
\q1 \v 5 “O Lord, make me to know my end,
\q2 and the number of my days, what it is;
\q2 that I may know what I lack.
\q1 \v 6 Behold, you have made my days old;\f + \fr 38:6 \fqa Alex. \ft a span long.\f*
\q2 and my existence \add is\add* as nothing before you:
\q1 no, every man living \add is\add*
\q2 altogether vanity.” Pause.
\q1 \v 7 Surely man walks in a shadow;\f + \fr 38:7 \fqa Gr. \ft image.\f*
\q2 no, he is disquieted in vain:
\q1 he lays up treasures,
\q2 and knows not for whom he shall gather them.
\b
\q1 \v 8 And now what \add is\add* my expectation? \add is it\add* not the Lord? and my ground \add of hope\add* is with you. Pause.
\q1 \v 9 Deliver me from all my transgressions:
\q2 you have made me a reproach to the foolish.
\q1 \v 10 I was dumb,\f + \fr 38:10 \fqa Gr. \ft made dumb.\f*
\q2 and opened not my mouth;
\q2 for you are He who made me.
\q1 \v 11 Remove your scourges from me:
\q2 I have fainted because of the strength of yours hand.
\q1 \v 12 You chasten man with rebukes for iniquity,
\q2 and you make his life to consume away like a spider's web:
\q2 no, every man is disquieted in vain. Pause.
\q1 \v 13 O Lord, listen to my prayer
\q2 and my supplication: attend to my tears:
\q1 be not silent,
\q2 for I am a sojourner in the land,
\q2 and a stranger, as all my fathers \add were\add*.
\q1 \v 14 Spare me,
\q2 that I may be refreshed,
\q2 before I depart, and be no more.
\c 39
\s1 I Waited Patiently for the LORD
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 I waited patiently for the Lord;
\q2 and he attended to me, and hearkened to my supplication.
\q1 \v 3 And he brought me up
\q2 out of a pit of misery, and from miry clay:
\q1 and he set my feet on a rock,
\q2 and ordered my goings aright.
\q1 \v 4 And he put a new song into my mouth, \add even\add* a hymn to our God:
\q1 many shall see \add it\add*, and fear,
\q2 and shall hope in the Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Blessed \add is\add* the man
\q2 whose hope is the name of the Lord, and \add who\add* has not regarded vanities
\q2 and false frenzies.
\q1 \v 6 O Lord my God,
\q2 you have multiplied your wonderful works,
\q1 and in your thoughts there is
\q2 none who shall be likened to you:
\q1 I declared and spoke \add of them\add*:
\q2 they exceeded number.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \x + \xo 39:7 \xt Heb. 10. 5,6.\x* Sacrifice and offering you would not;
\q2 but a body have you prepared me:
\q1 whole-burnt-offering and \add sacrifice\add* for sin
\q2 you did not require.
\q1 \v 8 Then I said, “Behold, I come:\f + \fr 39:8 \fqa Or, \ft am come.\f*
\q2 in the volume of the book it is written concerning me,
\q1 \v 9 I desired to do your will, O my God,
\q2 and your law in the midst of my heart.”
\b
\q1 \v 10 I have preached righteousness in the great congregation;
\q2 lo! I will not refrain my lips;
\q2 O Lord, you know my righteousness.
\q1 \v 11 I have not hid your truth within my heart,
\q2 and I have declared your salvation; I have not hid your mercy and your truth
\q2 from the great congregation.
\b
\q1 \v 12 But you, Lord, remove not your compassion far from me;
\q2 your mercy and your truth have helped me continually.
\q1 \v 13 For innumerable evils have encompassed me;
\q2 my transgressions have taken hold of me, and I could not see;
\q1 they are multiplied more than the hairs of my head;
\q2 and my heart has failed me.
\q1 \v 14 Be pleased, O Lord, to deliver me;
\q2 O Lord, draw near to help me.
\b
\q1 \v 15 Let those that seek my soul, to destroy it,
\q2 be ashamed and confounded together;
\q1 let those that wish me evil
\q2 be turned backward and put to shame.
\q1 \v 16 Let those that say to me, “Aha, aha,”
\q2 quickly receive shame for their reward.
\q1 \v 17 Let all those that seek you, O Lord,
\q2 exult and rejoice in you;
\q1 and let them who love your salvation
\q2 say continually, “The Lord be magnified.”
\b
\q1 \v 18 But I am poor and needy;
\q2 the Lord will take care of me;
\q1 you are my helper, and my defender,
\q2 O my God, delay not.
\c 40
\s1 Victory over Betrayal
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Blessed \add is the man\add* who thinks on the poor and needy:
\q2 the Lord shall deliver him in an evil day.
\q1 \v 3 May the Lord preserve him and keep him alive,
\q2 and bless him on the earth,
\q1 and not deliver him
\q2 into the hands of his enemy.
\q1 \v 4 May the Lord help him upon the bed of his pain;
\q2 you have made all his bed in his sickness.
\b
\q1 \v 5 I said, “O Lord, have mercy upon me;
\q2 heal my soul; for I have sinned against you.”
\q1 \v 6 My enemies have spoken evil against me, \add saying\add*,
\q2 “When shall he die, and his name perish?”
\q1 \v 7 And if he came to see \add me\add*, his heart spoke vainly;
\q2 he gathered iniquity to himself;
\q2 he went forth and spoke in like manner.
\q1 \v 8 All my enemies whispered against me;
\q2 against me they devised my hurt.
\q1 \v 9 They denounced a wicked word against me, \add saying\add*, “Now that he lies,
\q2 shall he not rise up again?”
\q1 \v 10 For even the man of my peace, in whom I trusted, \x + \xo 40:10 \xt John 13. 18.\x*
\q2 who ate my bread, lifted up \add his\add* heel against me.
\b
\q1 \v 11 But you, O Lord, have compassion upon me, and raise me up,
\q2 and I shall repay them.
\q1 \v 12 By this I know that you have delighted in me,
\q2 because my enemy shall not rejoice over me.
\q1 \v 13 But you did help me because of \add mine\add* innocence,
\q2 and have established me before you for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 14 Blessed \add be\add* the Lord God of Israel
\q2 from everlasting, and to everlasting.
\q1 So be it, so be it.
\c 41
\ms BOOK II
\s1 As the Deer Pants for the Water
\d \v 1 For the end, \add a Psalm\add* for instruction, for the sons of Korah.
\b
\q1 \v 2 As the hart earnestly desires the fountains of water,
\q2 so my soul earnestly longs for you, O God.
\q1 \v 3 My soul has thirsted for the living God:
\q2 when shall I come and appear before God?
\q1 \v 4 My tears have been bread to me
\q2 day and night,
\q1 while they daily said to me,
\q2 “Where is your God?”
\q1 \v 5 I remembered these things, and poured out my soul in me,
\q2 for I will go to the place of your wondrous tabernacle, \add even\add* to the house of God,
\q2 with a voice of exultation and thanksgiving and of the sound of those who keep festival.\f + \fr 41:5 \ft See \fqa Appendix.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 6 Why are you very sad, O my soul?
\q2 and why dost you trouble me?
\q1 hope in God; for I will give thanks to him; \add he is\add* the salvation of my countenance.
\b
\q1 \v 7 O my God, my soul has been troubled within me:
\q2 therefore will I remember you
\q1 from the land of Jordan, and of the Ermonites,
\q2 from the little hill.
\q1 \v 8 Deep calls to deep
\q2 at the voice of your cataracts:
\q1 all your billows and your waves
\q2 have gone over me.
\q1 \v 9 By day the Lord will command his mercy, and \f + \fr 41:9 \fqa Heb. \ft and \fqa Alex. \ft his song shall be, etc.\f*manifest \add it\add* by night: with me \add is\add* prayer to the God of my life.
\b
\q1 \v 10 I will say to God, “You are my helper;
\q2 why have you forgotten me?
\q1 wherefore do I go sad of countenance,
\q2 while the enemy oppresses \add me\add*?
\q1 \v 11 While my bones were breaking,
\q2 they who afflicted me reproached me;
\q1 while they said to me daily,
\q2 “Where is your God?”
\b
\q1 \v 12 Why are you very sad, O my soul?
\q2 and why dost you trouble me?
\q1 hope in God; for I will give thanks to him; \add he is\add* the health of my countenance, and my God.
\c 42
\s1 Send Out Your Light
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 Judge me, O God, and plead my cause,
\q2 against an ungodly nation:
\q2 deliver me from the unjust and crafty man.
\q1 \v 2 For you, O God, art my strength:
\q2 why have you cast me off?
\q1 and why do I go sad of countenance,
\q2 while the enemy oppresses \add me\add*?
\b
\q1 \v 3 Send forth your light and your truth:
\q2 they have led me,
\q1 and brought me to your holy mountain,
\q2 and to your tabernacles.
\q1 \v 4 And I will go in to the altar of God,
\q2 to God who gladdens my youth:
\q1 I will give thanks to you on the harp,
\q2 O God, my God.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Why are you very sad, O my soul?
\q2 and why dost you trouble me?
\q1 Hope in God; for I will give thanks to him, \add who is\add* the health of my countenance, \add and\add* my God.
\c 43
\s1 Redeem Us, O God
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm for \f + \fr 43:1 \fqa Gr. \ft understanding.\f*instruction, for the sons of Korah.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O God, we have heard with our ears,
\q2 our fathers have told us,
\q1 the work which you wrought in their days,
\q2 in the days of old.
\q1 \v 3 Yours hand utterly destroyed the heathen,
\q2 and you did plant them:
\q1 you did afflict the nations,
\q2 and cast them out.
\q1 \v 4 For they inherited not the land by their \add own\add* sword,
\q2 and their \add own\add* arm did not deliver them;
\q1 but your right hand,
\q2 and yours arm, and the light of your countenance,
\q2 because you wert well pleased in them.
\b
\q1 \v 5 You are indeed my King and my God,
\q2 who command deliverances for Jacob.
\q1 \v 6 In you will we push down our enemies,
\q2 and in your name will we bring to nothing them who rise up against us.
\q1 \v 7 For I will not trust in my bow,
\q2 and my sword shall not save me.
\q1 \v 8 For you have saved us from them who afflicted us,
\q2 and have put to shame them who hated us.
\q1 \v 9 In God \f + \fr 43:9 \fqa Gr. \ft will be praised etc. See \xt Ps. 106.\f*will we make our boast all the day,
\q2 and to your name will we give thanks for ever. Pause.
\q1 \v 10 But now you have cast off, and put us to shame;
\q2 and you will not go forth with our hosts.
\q1 \v 11 You have turned us back before our enemies;
\q2 and they who hated us spoiled for themselves.
\q1 \v 12 You made us as sheep for meat;
\q2 and you scatteredst us among the nations.
\q1 \v 13 You have sold your people without price,
\q2 and there was no profit by their exchange.
\b
\q1 \v 14 You have made us a reproach to our neighbours,
\q2 a scorn and derision to them who are round about us.
\q1 \v 15 You have made us a proverb among the Gentiles,
\q2 a shaking of the head among the nations.
\q1 \v 16 All the day my shame is before me,
\q2 and the confusion of my face has covered me,
\q1 \v 17 because of the voice of the slanderer and reviler;
\q2 because of the enemy and avenger.
\b
\q1 \v 18 All these things are come upon us: but we have not forgotten you,
\q2 neither have we dealt unrighteously in your covenant.
\q1 \v 19 And our heart has not gone back;
\q2 but you have turned aside our paths from your way.
\q1 \v 20 For you have laid us low in a place of affliction,
\q2 and the shadow of death has covered us.
\b
\q1 \v 21 If we have forgotten the name of our God,
\q2 and if we have spread out our hands to a strange god; shall not God search these things out?
\q1 \v 22 for he knows the secrets of the heart.
\q1 \v 23 \x + \xo 43:23 \xt Rom. 8. 36.\x* For, for your sake we are killed all the day long;
\q2 we are counted as sheep for slaughter.
\b
\q1 \v 24 Awake, why sleep you, O Lord?
\q2 arise, and do not cast \add us\add* off for ever.
\q1 \v 25 Wherefore turn you your face away,
\q2 \add and\add* forgett our poverty and our affliction?
\q1 \v 26 For our soul has been brought down to the dust;
\q2 our belly has cleaved to the earth.
\q1 \v 27 Arise, O Lord, help us,
\q2 and redeem us for your name's sake.
\c 44
\s1 My Heart Is Stirred by a Noble Theme
\d \v 1 For the end, for alternate \add strains\add*\f + \fr 44:1 \ft See also \xt Ps. 68; 79, \ft titles; \fqa q. d. \ft repeated, see \fqa Hebrew.\f* by the sons of Korah; for instruction, a Song concerning the beloved.
\b
\q1 \v 2 My heart has uttered\f + \fr 44:2 \fqa Lit. \ft eructavit.\f* a good matter:
\q2 I declare my works to the king:
\q2 my tongue is the pen of a quick writer.
\q1 \v 3 You are more beautiful than the sons of men:
\q2 grace has been shed forth on your lips:
\q2 therefore God has blessed you for ever.
\q1 \v 4 Gird your sword upon your thigh, O Mighty One,
\q2 in your comeliness, and in your beauty;
\q1 \v 5 and bend \add your bow\add*, and prosper, and reign,
\q2 because of truth and meekness and righteousness;
\q2 and your right hand shall guide you wonderfully.
\q1 \v 6 Your weapons are sharpened, Mighty One,
\q2 (the nations shall fall under you) \add they are\add* in the heart of the king's enemies.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \x + \xo 44:7 \xt Heb. 1. 8-10.\x* Your throne, O God, is for ever and ever:
\q2 the sceptre of your kingdom is a sceptre of righteousness.
\q1 \v 8 You have loved righteousness,
\q2 and hated iniquity:
\q1 therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness beyond your fellows.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Myrrh, and stacte, and cassia \add are exhaled\add* from your garments, \add and\add*
\q2 out of the ivory palaces,
\q1 \v 10 with which kings' daughters have gladdened you for yours honour:
\q2 the queen stood by on your right hand, clothed in vesture worked with gold, \add and\add*
\q2 arrayed in divers colours.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Hear, O daughter, and see, and incline your ear;
\q2 forget also your people, and your father's house.
\q1 \v 12 Because the king has desired your beauty;
\q2 for he is your Lord.
\q1 \v 13 And the daughter of Tyre shall adore him with gifts;
\q2 the rich of the people of the land shall supplicate your favour.\f + \fr 44:13 \fqa Gr. \ft face.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 14 All her glory \add is that\add* of the daughter of the king of Esebon,\f + \fr 44:14 \fqa Alex. \ft ἔσωθεν, within, so \fqa Heb.\f*
\q2 robed \add as she is\add* in golden fringed garments,
\q1 \v 15 in embroidered \add clothing\add*:
\q2 virgins shall be brought to the king after her:
\q2 her fellows shall be brought to you.
\b
\q1 \v 16 They shall be brought with gladness and exultation:
\q2 they shall be led into the king's temple.
\b
\q1 \v 17 Instead of your fathers children are born to you:
\q2 you shall make them princes over all the earth.
\q1 \v 18 They shall make mention of your name from generation to generation:
\q2 therefore shall the nations give thanks to you for ever,\f + \fr 44:18 \fqa Gr. \ft to the age of the age.\f* even for ever and ever.
\c 45
\s1 God Is Our Refuge and Strength
\d \v 1 For the end, for the sons of Korah; a Psalm concerning secret things.
\b
\q1 \v 2 God is our refuge and strength,
\q2 a help in the afflictions that have come heavily upon us.
\q1 \v 3 Therefore will we not fear when the earth is troubled,
\q2 and the mountains are removed into the depths of the seas.
\q1 \v 4 Their waters have roared and been troubled,
\q2 the mountains have been troubled by his might. Pause.
\q1 \v 5 The flowings of the river gladden the city of God:
\q2 the Most High has sanctified his tabernacle.
\q1 \v 6 God is in the midst of her; she shall not be moved:
\q2 God shall help her with his countenance.\f + \fr 45:6 \fqa Alex. \ft and \fqa Heb. \ft before, \fqa or, \ft toward the morning.\f*
\q1 \v 7 The nations were troubled, the kingdoms tottered:
\q2 he uttered his voice, the earth shook.
\q1 \v 8 The Lord of hosts is with us;
\q2 the God of Jacob is our helper. Pause.
\q1 \v 9 Come, and behold the works of the Lord,
\q2 what wonders he has achieved on the earth.
\q1 \v 10 Putting an end to wars as for the ends of the earth;
\q2 he will crush the bow, and break in pieces the weapon,
\q2 and burn the bucklers with fire.
\b
\q1 \v 11 “Be still, and know that I am God:
\q2 I will be exalted among the nations,
\q2 I will be exalted in the earth.”
\b
\q1 \v 12 The Lord of hosts is with us;
\q2 the God of Jacob is our helper.
\c 46
\s1 Clap Your Hands, All You Peoples
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm for the sons of Korah.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Clap your hands, all you nations;
\q2 shout to God with a voice of exultation.
\b
\q1 \v 3 For the Lord most high is terrible;
\q2 \add he is\add* a great king over all the earth.
\q1 \v 4 He has subdued the peoples under us,
\q2 and the nations under our feet.
\q1 \v 5 He has chosen out his inheritance for us,
\q2 the beauty of Jacob which he loved. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 God is gone up with a shout,
\q2 the Lord with a sound of a trumpet.
\q1 \v 7 Sing praises to our God, sing praises:
\q2 sing praises to our King, sing praises.
\q1 \v 8 For God is king of all the earth:
\q2 sing praises with understanding.
\b
\q1 \v 9 God reigns over the nations:
\q2 God sits upon the throne of his holiness.
\q1 \v 10 The rulers of the people are assembled
\q2 with the God of Abraham:
\q1 for God's mighty ones of the earth
\q2 have been greatly exalted.
\c 47
\s1 Broken Bondage
\d \v 1 A Psalm of praise\f + \fr 47:1 \ft A song.\f* for the sons of Korah on the second \add day\add* of the week.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Great is the Lord,
\q2 and greatly to be praised
\q1 in the city of our God,
\q2 in his holy mountain.
\q1 \v 3 The city of the great King is well planted \add on\add* the mountains of Zion,
\q2 with the joy of the whole earth,
\q1 \add on\add* the sides of the north.
\q1 \v 4 God is known in her palaces,
\q2 when he undertakes to help her.
\b
\q1 \v 5 For, behold, the kings of the earth were assembled,
\q2 they came together.
\q1 \v 6 They saw, and so they wondered:
\q2 they were troubled, they were moved.
\q1 \v 7 Trembling took hold on them:
\q2 there were the pangs as of a woman in travail.
\q1 \v 8 You will break the ships of Tarshish
\q2 with a vehement wind.
\b
\q1 \v 9 As we have heard, so have we also seen,
\q2 in the city of the Lord of hosts,
\q1 in the city of our God:
\q2 God has founded it for ever. Pause.
\q1 \v 10 We have thought of your mercy, O God,
\q2 in the midst of your people.
\q1 \v 11 According to your name, O God,
\q2 so is also your praise to the ends of the earth:
\q2 your right hand is full of righteousness.
\q1 \v 12 Let mount Zion rejoice,
\q2 let the daughters of Judæa exult,
\q2 because of your judgments, O Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Go round about Zion, and encompass her:
\q2 tell you her towers.
\q1 \v 14 Mark you well her strength, and observe her palaces;
\q2 that you may tell the next generation.
\q1 \v 15 For this is our God for ever and ever:
\q2 he will be our guide for evermore.
\c 48
\s1 The Evanescence of Wealth
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm for the sons of Korah.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Hear these words, all you nations,
\q2 listen, all you that dwell upon the earth:
\q1 \v 3 both the sons of mean men,\f + \fr 48:3 \fqa Gr. \ft earth-born.\f* and sons of \add great\add* men; the
\q2 rich and poor \add man\add* together.
\q1 \v 4 My mouth shall speak of wisdom;
\q2 and the meditation of my heart shall bring \add forth\add* understanding.
\q1 \v 5 I will incline my ear to a parable:
\q2 I will open my riddle\f + \fr 48:5 \fqa Gr. \ft problem.\f* on the harp.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Wherefore should I fear\f + \fr 48:6 \fqa Gr. \ft shall.\f* in the evil day?
\q2 the iniquity of my heel shall compass me.
\q1 \v 7 They who trust in their strength,
\q2 and boast themselves in the multitude of their wealth—
\q1 \v 8 A brother does not redeem, shall a man redeem?
\q2 he shall not give to God a ransom for himself,
\q1 \v 9 or the price of the redemption of his soul,
\q2 though he labour for ever,
\q1 \v 10 and live to the end,
\q2 \add so\add* that he should not see corruption.
\b
\q1 \v 11 When he shall see wise men dying,
\q2 the fool and the senseless one shall perish together;
\q2 and they shall leave their wealth to strangers.
\q1 \v 12 And their sepulchres are their houses for ever,
\q2 \add even\add* their tabernacles to all generations:
\q2 they have called their lands after their own names.
\q1 \v 13 And man being in honour, understands not:
\q2 he is compared to the senseless cattle, and is like to them.
\b
\q1 \v 14 This their way is an offence to them:
\q2 yet afterwards men will commend their sayings. Pause.
\q1 \v 15 They have laid \add them\add* as sheep in Hades;
\q2 death shall feed on them;
\q1 and the upright shall have dominion over them in the morning,
\q2 and their help shall fail in Hades
\q2 from their glory.
\q1 \v 16 But God shall deliver my soul from the power of Hades,
\q2 when he shall receive me. Pause.
\q1 \v 17 Fear not when a man is enriched,
\q2 and when the glory of his house is increased.
\q1 \v 18 For he shall take nothing when he dies;
\q2 nor shall his glory descend with him.
\q1 \v 19 For his soul shall be blessed in his life:
\q2 he shall give thanks to you when you dost well to him.
\q1 \v 20 \add Yet\add* he shall go in to the generation\f + \fr 48:20 \fqa Gr. \ft as far as.\f* of his fathers;
\q2 he shall never see light.
\q1 \v 21 Man who is in honour, understands not:
\q2 he is compared to the senseless cattle, and is like them.
\c 49
\s1 The Mighty One Calls
\b
\d \v 1 A Psalm for Asaph.\f + \fr 49:1 \fqa Or, \ft of.\f*  
\p
\q1 The God of gods, the Lord, has spoken,
\q2 and called the earth
\q2 from the rising of the sun to the going down \add thereof\add*.
\q1 \v 2 Out of Zion \add comes\add*
\q2 the excellence of his beauty.
\q1 \v 3 God, our God, shall come manifestly, and shall not keep silence:
\q2 a fire shall be kindled before him,
\q2 and round about him there shall be a very great tempest.
\q1 \v 4 He shall summon the heaven above,
\q2 and the earth, that he may \x + \xo 49:4 \xt Heb. 10. 30.\x* judge his people.
\q1 \v 5 “Assemble you his saints to him,
\q2 those that have engaged in a covenant with him upon sacrifices.
\q1 \v 6 And the heavens shall declare his righteousness:
\q2 for God is judge. Pause.
\q1 \v 7 Hear, my people, and I will speak to you,
\q2 O Israel: and I will testify to you:
\q2 I am God, your God.
\q1 \v 8 I will not reprove you on account of your sacrifices;
\q2 for your whole-burnt-offerings are before me continually.
\q1 \v 9 I will take no bullocks out of yours house,
\q2 nor he-goats out of your flocks.
\q1 \v 10 For all the wild beasts of the thicket are mine,
\q2 the cattle on the mountains, and oxen.
\q1 \v 11 I know all the birds of the sky;
\q2 and the beauty of the field is mine.
\q1 \v 12 If I should be hungry, I will not tell you:
\q2 for the world is mine, and the fulness of it.
\q1 \v 13 Will I eat the flesh of bulls,
\q2 or drink the blood of goats?
\q1 \v 14 Offer to God the sacrifice of praise;
\q2 and pay your vows to the Most High.
\q1 \v 15 And call upon me in the day of affliction; and
\q2 I will deliver you, and you shall glorify me.” Pause.
\b
\q1 \v 16 But to the sinner God has said,
\q2 “Why do you do declare my ordinances,
\q2 and take up my covenant in your mouth?
\q1 \v 17 Whereas you have hated instruction,
\q2 and have cast my words behind \add you\add*.
\q1 \v 18 If you saw a thief, you rann along with him,\f + \fr 49:18 \ft See 1 Pe 4. 4.\f*
\q2 and have cast in your lot with adulterers.
\q1 \v 19 Your mouth has multiplied wickedness,
\q2 and your tongue has framed deceit.\f + \fr 49:19 \ft From these words in \fqa Alex. \ft to \xt Ps. 79. 11, \ft thirty psalms are wanting.\f*
\q1 \v 20 You did sit and speak against your brother,
\q2 and did scandalise your mother's son.
\q1 \v 21 These things you did, and I kept silence:
\q2 you thought wickedly that I should be like you;
\q1 \add but\add* I will reprove you,
\q2 and set \add yours offences\add* before you.
\b
\q1 \v 22 Now consider these things, you that forget God,
\q2 lest he rend \add you\add*,
\q2 and there be no deliverer.
\q1 \v 23 The sacrifice of praise will glorify me: and that is the way\f + \fr 49:23 \fqa Gr. \ft there.\f*
\q2 wherein I will show to him the salvation of God.”
\c 50
\s1 Create in Me a Clean Heart, O God
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David,
\q1 \v 2 when Nathan the prophet came to him, when he had gone in to Bathsheba.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Have mercy upon me, O God,
\q2 according to your great mercy;
\q1 and according to the multitude of your compassions
\q2 blot out my transgression.
\q1 \v 4 Wash me thoroughly from my iniquity,
\q2 and cleanse me from my sin.
\q1 \v 5 For I am conscious of my iniquity;
\q2 and my sin is continually before me.
\q1 \v 6 Against you only have I sinned,
\q2 and done evil before you:
\q1 that you might be justified in your sayings,
\q2 and might overcome when you are judged.
\q1 \v 7 For, behold, I was conceived in iniquities,
\q2 and in sins did my mother conceive me.
\b
\q1 \v 8 For, behold, you love truth:
\q2 you have manifested to me the secret and hidden things of your wisdom.
\q1 \v 9 You shall sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be purified:
\q2 you shall wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow.
\q1 \v 10 You shall cause me to hear gladness and joy:
\q2 the afflicted bones shall rejoice.
\q1 \v 11 Turn away your face from my sins,
\q2 and blot out all my iniquities.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Create in me a clean heart, O God;
\q2 and renew a right spirit in my inward parts.
\q1 \v 13 Cast me not away from your presence;
\q2 and remove not your Holy Spirit from me.
\q1 \v 14 Restore to me the joy of your salvation:
\q2 establish me with your directing Spirit.\f + \fr 50:14 \fqa Gr. \ft governing.\f*
\q1 \v 15 \add Then\add* will I teach transgressors your ways;
\q2 and ungodly men shall turn to you.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Deliver me from blood-guiltiness, O God,
\q2 the God of my salvation:
\q2 \add and\add* my tongue shall joyfully declare your righteousness.
\q1 \v 17 O Lord, you shall open my lips;
\q2 and my mouth shall declare your praise.
\q1 \v 18 For if you desiredst sacrifice, I would have given \add it\add*:
\q2 you will not take pleasure in whole-burnt-offerings.
\q1 \v 19 Sacrifice to God is a broken spirit:
\q2 a broken and humbled heart
\q2 God will not despise.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Do good, O Lord, to Zion in your good pleasure;
\q2 and let the walls of Jerusalem be built.
\q1 \v 21 Then shall you be pleased with a sacrifice of righteousness, offering,\f + \fr 50:21 \ft Properly, a heave or wave-offfering.\f* and whole-burnt-sacrifices:
\q2 then shall they offer calves upon yours altar.
\c 51
\s1 Why Do You Boast of Evil?
\d \v 1 For the end, \add a Psalm\add* of instruction\f + \fr 51:1 \fqa Gr. \ft governing.\f* by David,
\q1 \v 2 when Doec the Idumean came and told Saul, and said to him, David is gone to the house of Abimelech.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Why do you do, O mighty man, boast of iniquity in \add your\add* mischief?
\q2 All the day
\q1 \v 4 your tongue has devised unrighteousness;
\q2 like a sharpened razor
\q2 you have worked deceit.
\q1 \v 5 You have loved wickedness more than goodness;
\q2 unrighteousness better than to speak righteousness. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 You have loved all words of destruction,
\q2 \add and\add* a deceitful tongue.
\q1 \v 7 Therefore may God destroy you for ever,
\q2 may he pluck you up and utterly remove you from \add your\add* dwelling,
\q2 and your root from the land of the living. Pause.
\q1 \v 8 And the righteous shall see, and fear,
\q2 and shall laugh at him, and say,
\q1 \v 9 “Behold the man
\q2 who made not God his help;
\q1 but trusted in the abundance of his wealth,
\q2 and strengthened himself in his vanity.”
\b
\q1 \v 10 But I am as a fruitful olive in the house of God:
\q1 I have trusted in the mercy of God
\q2 for ever, even for evermore.
\q1 \v 11 I will give thanks to you for ever,
\q2 for you have done \add it\add*:
\q1 and I will wait on your name;
\q2 for \add it is\add* good
\q2 before your saints.
\c 52
\s1 The Fool Says There Is No God
\d \v 1 For the end, \add a Psalm\add* of David upon Maels, of instruction.
\b
\q1 \v 2 The fool has said in his heart,
\q2 “There is no God.”
\q1 They have corrupted \add themselves\add*, and become abominable in iniquities:
\q2 there is none that does good.
\b
\q1 \v 3 God looked down from heaven
\q2 upon the sons of men,
\q1 to see if there were any that understood,
\q2 or sought after God.
\q1 \v 4 \x + \xo 52:4 \xt Rom. 3. 10-12.\x* They have all gone out of the way,
\q2 they are together become unprofitable;
\q1 there is none that does good,
\q2 there is not even one.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Will none of the workers of iniquity know,
\q1 who devour my people as they would eat bread?
\q2 they have not called upon God.
\q1 There were they greatly afraid,
\q2 where there was no fear:
\q1 \v 6 for God has scattered the bones
\q2 of the men-pleasers;
\q1 they were ashamed,
\q2 for God despised them.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Who will bring the salvation of Israel out of Zion?
\q1 When the Lord turns the captivity of his people,
\q2 Jacob shall exult, and Israel shall be glad.
\c 53
\s1 Save Me by Your Name
\d \v 1 For the end, among the Hymns of instruction by David,
\q1 \v 2 when the Ziphites came and said to Saul, Lo, is not David hid with us?
\b
\q1 \v 3 Save me, O God, by your name,
\q2 and judge me by your might.
\q1 \v 4 O God, hear my prayer;
\q2 listen to the words of my mouth.
\q1 \v 5 For strangers have risen up against me,
\q2 and mighty men have sought my life:
\q2 they have not set God before them. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 For lo! God assists me;
\q2 and the Lord is the helper of my soul.
\q1 \v 7 He shall return evil to my enemies;
\q2 utterly destroy them in your truth.
\q1 \v 8 I will willingly sacrifice to you:
\q2 I will give thanks to your name, O Lord; for \add it is\add* good.
\q1 \v 9 For you have delivered me out of all affliction,
\q2 and my eye has seen \add my desire\add* upon my enemies.
\c 54
\s1 Cast Your Burden upon the LORD
\d \v 1 For the end, among Hymns of instruction by David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Listen, O God, to my prayer;
\q2 and disregard not my supplication.
\q1 \v 3 Attend to me, and listen to me:
\q2 I was grieved in my meditation, and troubled;
\q1 \v 4 because of the voice of the enemy,
\q2 and because of the oppression of the sinner:
\q1 for they brought iniquity against me,
\q2 and were wrathfully angry with me.
\b
\q1 \v 5 My heart was troubled within me;
\q2 and the fear of death fell upon me.
\q1 \v 6 Fear and trembling came upon me,
\q2 and darkness covered me.
\q1 \v 7 And I said, “O that I had wings as \add those\add* of a dove!
\q2 then would I flee away, and be at rest.
\q1 \v 8 Lo! I have fled afar off,
\q2 and lodged in the wilderness. Pause.
\q1 \v 9 I waited for him that should deliver me
\q2 from distress of spirit and tempest.”
\b
\q1 \v 10 Destroy, O Lord, and divide their tongues:
\q2 for I have seen iniquity and gainsaying in the city.
\q1 \v 11 Day and night he shall go\f + \fr 54:11 \fqa Or, \ft it, \fqa sc. \ft iniquity.\f* round about it upon its walls:
\q2 iniquity and sorrow and unrighteousness \add are\add* in the midst of it;
\q1 \v 12 and usury and craft have not failed from its streets.
\b
\q1 \v 13 For if an enemy had reproached me,
\q2 I would have endured it;
\q1 and if one who hated \add me\add* had spoken vauntingly against me,
\q2 I would have hid myself from him.
\q1 \v 14 But you, O man likeminded,
\q2 my guide, and my acquaintance,
\q1 \v 15 who in companionship with me sweetened \add our\add* food:
\q2 we walked in the house of God in concord.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Let death come upon them,
\q2 and let them go down alive into Hades,
\q2 for iniquity is in their dwellings, in the midst of them.
\q1 \v 17 I cried to God,
\q2 and the Lord hearkened to me.
\q1 \v 18 Evening, and morning, and at noon I will declare and make known \add my wants\add*:
\q2 and he shall hear my voice.
\q1 \v 19 He shall deliver my soul in peace
\q2 from them who draw near to me:
\q2 for they were with me in many \add cases\add*.
\q1 \v 20 God shall hear, and bring them low,
\q2 \add even\add* He who has existed from eternity. Pause.  
\p For they suffer\f + \fr 54:20 \fqa Or, \ft they have nothing to give in exchange, \fqa q. d. \ft to redeem their souls. Compare \xt Ps. 48. 7,8; Mark 8. 37.\f* no reverse,
\q2 and \add therefore\add* they have not feared God.
\q1 \v 21 He has reached forth his hand for retribution;
\q2 they have profaned his covenant.
\q1 \v 22 They were scattered at the anger of his countenance,
\q2 and his heart drew near them.\f + \fr 54:22 \ft Compare \fqa Heb. \ft and \fqa A. V.\f*
\q1 His words were smoother than oil,
\q2 yet are they darts.
\b
\q1 \v 23 \x + \xo 54:23 \xt 1 Pet. 5. 7.\x* Cast your care upon the Lord, and he shall sustain you;\f + \fr 54:23 \fqa Or, \ft nourish.\f*
\q2 he shall never suffer the righteous to be moved.
\q1 \v 24 But you, O God, shall bring them down
\q2 to the pit of destruction;
\q1 bloody and crafty men shall not live out half their days;
\q1 but I will hope in you, O Lord.
\c 55
\s1 Be Merciful to Me, O God
\d \v 1 For the end, concerning the people who were removed from the sanctuary,\f + \fr 55:1 \fqa Or, \ft holy things.\f* by David for a memorial, when the Philistines caught him in Geth.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Have mercy upon me, O God;
\q2 for man has trodden me down;
\q2 all the day long he warring has afflicted me.
\q1 \v 3 My enemies have trodden me down all the day from the dawning of the day;
\q2 for there are many warring against me.
\b
\q1 \v 4 They shall be afraid,
\q2 but I will trust in you.
\q1 \v 5 In God I will praise my words;
\q2 all the day have I hoped in God;
\q1 I will not fear
\q2 what flesh shall do to me.
\b
\q1 \v 6 All the day long they have abominated my words;
\q2 all their devices \add are\add* against me for evil.
\q1 \v 7 They will dwell near and hide \add themselves\add*;
\q2 they will watch my steps,
\q2 accordingly as I have waited patiently in my soul.
\q1 \v 8 You will on no account save them;
\q2 you will bring down the people in wrath.
\b
\q1 \v 9 O God, I have declared my life to you;
\q2 you have set my tears before you,
\q2 even according to your promise.
\q1 \v 10 My enemies shall be turned back,
\q2 in the day wherein I shall call upon you;
\q2 behold, I know that you are my God.
\b
\q1 \v 11 In God will I praise \add his\add* word;
\q2 in the Lord will I praise \add his\add* saying.
\q1 \v 12 I have hoped in God; I will not be afraid of
\q2 what man shall do to me.
\b
\q1 \v 13 The vows of your praise, O God,
\q2 which I will pay, are upon me.
\q1 \v 14 For you have delivered my soul from death,
\q2 and my feet from sliding,
\q1 that I should be well-pleasing before God
\q2 in the land of the living.
\c 56
\s1 In You My Soul Takes Refuge
\d \v 1 For the end. Destroy not: by David, for a memorial, when he fled from the presence of Saul to the cave.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Have mercy, upon me, O God, have mercy upon me:
\q2 for my soul has trusted in you:
\q1 and in the shadow of your wings will I hope,
\q2 until the iniquity have passed away.
\q1 \v 3 I will cry to God most high;
\q2 the God who has benefited me. Pause.
\q1 \v 4 He sent from heaven and saved me;
\q2 he gave to reproach them who trampled on me:
\q1 God has sent forth his mercy
\q2 and his truth;
\q1 \v 5 and he has delivered my soul from the midst of \add lions'\add* whelps:
\q2 I lay down to sleep, \add though\add* troubled.
\q1 \add As for\add* the sons of men, their teeth are arms and \add missile\add* weapons,
\q2 and their tongue a sharp sword.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Be you exalted, O God, above the heavens;
\q2 and your glory above all the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 7 They have prepared snares for my feet,
\q2 and have bowed down my soul:
\q1 they have dug a pit before my face,
\q2 and fallen into it \add themselves\add*. Pause.
\q1 \v 8 My heart, O God, \add is\add* ready, my heart \add is\add* ready:
\q2 I will sing, yes will sing psalms.
\q1 \v 9 Awake, my glory;
\q2 awake, psaltery and harp:
\q2 I will awake early.
\b
\q1 \v 10 O Lord, I will give thanks to you among the nations:
\q2 I will sing to you among the Gentiles.
\q1 \v 11 For your mercy has been magnified even to the heavens,
\q2 and your truth to the clouds.
\q1 \v 12 Be you exalted, O God, above the heavens;
\q2 and your glory above all the earth.
\c 57
\s1 God Judges the Earth
\d \v 1 For the end. Destroy not: by David, for a memorial.
\b
\q1 \v 2 If you do indeed speak righteousness, \add then\add* do you judge rightly,
\q2 you sons of men.
\q1 \v 3 For you work iniquities in \add your\add* hearts in the earth:
\q2 your hands plot unrighteousness.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Sinners have gone astray from the womb:
\q2 they go astray from the belly: they speak lies.
\q1 \v 5 Their venom is like \add that\add* of a serpent; as \add that\add* of a deaf asp,
\q2 and that stops her ears;
\q1 \v 6 which will not hear the voice of charmers, nor \add heed\add* the charm prepared skilfully\f + \fr 57:6 \fqa Gr. \ft poison.\f* by the wise.
\b
\q1 \v 7 God has crushed their teeth in their mouth:
\q2 God has broken the cheek-teeth of the lions.
\q1 \v 8 They shall utterly pass away like water running through:
\q2 he shall bend his bow till they shall fail.
\q1 \v 9 They shall be destroyed as melted wax:
\q2 the fire has fallen
\q2 and they have not seen the sun.
\b
\q1 \v 10 Before your thorns feel the white thorn,
\q2 he shall swallow you up as living, as in his wrath.
\q1 \v 11 The righteous shall rejoice
\q2 when he sees the vengeance of the ungodly:
\q1 he shall wash his hands
\q2 in the blood of the sinner.
\q1 \v 12 And a man shall say,
\q2 “Truly then there is a reward for\f + \fr 57:12 \fqa Gr. \ft fruit.\f* the righteous:
\q2 truly there is a God that judges them in the earth.”
\c 58
\s1 Deliver Me from My Enemies
\d \v 1 For the end. Destroy not: by David for a memorial, when Saul sent, and watched his house to kill him.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Deliver me from my enemies, O God;
\q2 and ransom me from those that rise up against me.
\q1 \v 3 Deliver me from the workers of iniquity,
\q2 and save me from bloody men.
\q1 \v 4 For, behold, they have hunted after my soul;
\q2 violent men have set upon me:
\q2 neither \add is it\add* my iniquity, nor my sin, O Lord.
\q1 \v 5 Without iniquity I ran and
\q2 directed \add my course aright\add*:
\q2 awake to help me, and behold.
\q1 \v 6 And you, Lord God of hosts, the God of Israel,
\q2 draw near to visit all the heathen;
\q2 pity not any that work iniquity. Pause.
\q1 \v 7 They shall return at evening,
\q2 and hunger like a dog, and go round about the city.
\q1 \v 8 Behold, they shall utter a voice with their mouth,
\q2 and a sword is in their lips;
\q2 “for who, \add say they\add*, has heard?”
\q1 \v 9 But you, Lord, will laugh them to scorn;
\q2 you will utterly set at nothing all the heathen.
\b
\q1 \v 10 I will keep my strength, \add looking\add* to you;
\q2 for you, O God, art my helper.
\q1 \v 11 \add As for\add* my God, his mercy shall go before me:
\q2 my God will show me \add vengeance\add* on my enemies.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Slay them not,
\q2 lest they forget your law;\f + \fr 58:12 \ft Shammah read λαοῦ 'people'.\f*
\q1 scatter them by your power;
\q2 and bring them down,
\q2 O Lord, my defender.
\q1 \v 13 \add For\add* the sin of their mouth,
\q2 \add and\add* the word of their lips,
\q1 let them be even taken
\q2 in their pride.
\q1 \v 14 And for \add their\add* cursing and falsehood shall utter destruction be denounced:
\q2 \add they shall fall\add* by the wrath of utter destruction, and shall not be;
\q1 so shall they know that the God of Jacob
\q2 is Lord of the ends of the earth. Pause.
\q1 \v 15 They shall return at evening,
\q2 and be hungry as a dog,
\q2 and go round about the city.
\q1 \v 16 They shall be scattered here and there for meat;
\q2 and if they be not satisfied, they shall even murmur.
\b
\q1 \v 17 But I will sing to your strength,
\q2 and in the morning will I exult \add in\add* your mercy;
\q1 for you have been my supporter,
\q2 and my refuge in the day of my affliction.
\q1 \v 18 \add You are\add* my helper;
\q2 to you, my God, will I sing;
\q2 you are my supporter, O my God, \add and\add* my mercy.
\c 59
\s1 Victory with God
\d \v 1 For the end, for them who shall yet be changed; for an inscription by David for instruction,
\q1 \v 2 when he \add had\add* burned Mesopotamia of Syria, and Syria Shobal, and Joab \add had\add* returned and smitten \add in\add* the valley of salt twelve thousand.
\b
\q1 \v 3 O God, you have rejected
\q2 and destroyed us;
\q1 you have been angry,
\q2 yet have pitied us.
\q1 \v 4 You have shaken the earth,
\q2 and troubled it;
\q1 heal its breaches,
\q2 for it has been shaken.
\q1 \v 5 You have shewn your people hard things:
\q2 you have made us drink the wine of astonishment.
\b
\q1 \v 6 You have given a token to them who fear you,
\q2 that they might flee from the bow. Pause.
\b
\q1 \v 7 That your beloved ones may be delivered;
\q2 save with your right hand, and hear me.
\b
\q1 \v 8 God has spoken in his holiness;
\q2 “I will rejoice,
\q1 and divide Sicima,
\q2 and measure out the valley of tents.
\q1 \v 9 Gilead is mine, and Manasseh is mine;
\q2 and Ephraim is the \f + \fr 59:9 \fqa Or, \ft strengthening.\f*strength of my head;
\q1 \v 10 Judas is my king; Moab is the caldron of my hope;
\q2 over Idumea will I stretch out my shoe;
\q2 the Philistines have been subjected to me.”
\b
\q1 \v 11 Who will lead me into the fortified city?
\q2 who will guide me as far as Idumea?
\q1 \v 12 Will not you, O God, who have cast us off?
\q2 and will not you, O God, go forth with our forces?
\q1 \v 13 Give us help from trouble:
\q2 for vain is the deliverance of man.
\q1 \v 14 In God will we do valiantly;\f + \fr 59:14 \fqa Gr. \ft work power.\f*
\q2 and he shall bring to nothing them who harass us.
\c 60
\s1 You Have Heard My Vows
\d \v 1 For the end, among the Hymns of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O God, listen to my petition;
\q2 attend to my prayer.
\q1 \v 3 From the ends of the earth have I cried to you,
\q2 when my heart was in trouble:
\q1 you liftedst me up on a rock,
\q2 you did guide me:
\q1 \v 4 because you wert my hope,
\q2 a tower of strength from the face of the enemy.
\q1 \v 5 I will dwell in your tabernacle for ever;
\q2 I will shelter myself under the shadow of your wings. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 For you, O God, have heard my prayers;
\q2 you have given an inheritance
\q2 to them who fear your name.
\b
\q1 \v 7 You shall add days to the days of the king;
\q2 \add you shall lengthen\add* his years to all generations.
\q1 \v 8 He shall endure for ever before God:
\q2 which of them will
\q2 seek out his mercy and truth?
\q1 \v 9 So will I sing to your name for ever and ever,
\q2 that I may daily perform my vows.
\c 61
\s1 Waiting on God
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David for Jeduthun.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Shall not my soul be subjected to God?
\q2 for of him is my salvation.
\q1 \v 3 For he is my God and my Saviour;
\q2 my helper, I shall not
\q2 be moved very much.\f + \fr 61:3 \fqa Or, \ft any more.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 4 How long will you assault a man?
\q2 you are all slaughtering
\q1 as with a bowed wall
\q2 and a broken hedge.
\q1 \v 5 They only took counsel to set at nothing my honour:
\q2 I ran in thirst:
\q1 with their mouth they blessed,
\q2 but with their heart they cursed. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 Nevertheless do you, my soul, be subjected to God;
\q2 for of him \add is\add* my patient hope.
\q1 \v 7 For he \add is\add* my God and my Saviour;
\q2 my helper, I shall not be moved.
\q1 \v 8 In God\f + \fr 61:8 \fqa Gr. \ft on.\f* \add is\add* my salvation and my glory:
\q2 \add he is\add* the God of my help, and my hope is in God.
\q1 \v 9 Hope in him, all you congregation of the people;
\q2 pour out your hearts before him,
\q2 for God is our helper. Pause.
\q1 \v 10 But the sons of men are vain;
\q2 the sons of men are false,
\q1 so as to be deceitful in the balances; they are all alike\f + \fr 61:10 \fqa Gr. \ft altogether.\f* \add formed\add* out of vanity.
\q1 \v 11 Trust not in unrighteousness,
\q2 and lust not after robberies:
\q1 if wealth should flow in,
\q2 set not your heart upon it.
\b
\q1 \v 12 God has spoken once,
\q2 \add and\add* I have heard these two things,
\q1 that power is of God;
\q2 \v 13 and mercy is yours, O Lord;
\q1 for you will recompense every one
\q2 according to his works.
\c 62
\s1 Thirsting for God
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David, when he was in the wilderness of Idumea.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O God, my God, I cry to you early;
\q2 my soul has thirsted for you:
\q2 how often has my flesh \add longed\add* after you,
\q1 in a barren and trackless
\q2 and dry land!
\b
\q1 \v 3 Thus have I appeared before you in the sanctuary,
\q2 that I might see your power and your glory.
\q1 \v 4 For your mercy is better than life:\f + \fr 62:4 \fqa Gr. \ft lives.\f*
\q2 my lips shall praise you.
\q1 \v 5 Thus will I bless you during my life:
\q2 I will lift up my hands in your name.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Let my soul be filled as with marrow and fatness;
\q2 and \add my\add* joyful lips shall praise your name.
\q1 \v 7 Forasmuch as\f + \fr 62:7 \fqa Gr. \ft if. See \xt Acts 26. 23.\f* I have remembered you on my bed:
\q2 in the early seasons I have meditated on you.
\q1 \v 8 For you have been my helper,
\q2 and in the shelter of your wings will I rejoice.
\q1 \v 9 My soul has kept very close\f + \fr 62:9 \fqa Gr. \ft been glued.\f* behind you:
\q2 your right hand has upheld me.
\b
\q1 \v 10 But they vainly sought after my soul;
\q2 they shall go into the lowest parts of the earth.
\q1 \v 11 They shall be delivered up to the power of the sword;
\q2 they shall be portions for foxes.
\q1 \v 12 But the king shall rejoice in God;
\q2 every one that swears by him shall be praised;
\q2 for the mouth of them who speak unjust things has been stopped.
\c 63
\s1 The Hurtful Tongue
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Hear my prayer, O God, when I make my petition to you;
\q2 deliver my soul from fear of the enemy.
\q1 \v 3 You have sheltered me from the conspiracy of them who do wickedly;
\q2 from the multitude of them who work iniquity;
\q1 \v 4 who have sharpened their tongues as a sword;
\q2 they have bent their bow maliciously;
\q1 \v 5 to shoot in secret at the blameless;
\q2 they will shoot him suddenly, and will not fear.
\b
\q1 \v 6 They have set up for themselves an evil matter,
\q2 they have given counsel to hide snares; they have said,
\q2 “Who shall see them?”
\q1 \v 7 They have searched out iniquity; they have wearied themselves with searching diligently,
\q2 a man shall approach”
\q2 and the heart is deep,
\b
\q1 \v 8 and God shall be exalted,
\q2 their wounds were \add caused by\add* the weapon of the foolish children,
\q1 \v 9 and their tongues have set him at nothing,
\q2 all that saw them
\q2 were troubled;
\q1 \v 10 and every man was alarmed,
\q2 and they related the works of God,
\q2 and understood his deeds.
\b
\q1 \v 11 The righteous shall rejoice in the Lord,
\q2 and hope on him,
\q2 and all the upright in heart shall be praised.
\c 64
\s1 Praise Awaits God in Zion
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm \add and\add* Song of David.
\q1 \v 2 Praise becomes you, O God, in Zion;
\q2 and to you shall the vow be performed.
\q1 \v 3 Hear my prayer;
\q2 to you all flesh shall come.
\v 4 The words of transgressors have overpowered us;
\q2 but do you pardon our sins.
\q1 \v 5 Blessed \add is he\add* whom you have chosen and adopted;
\q2 he shall dwell in your courts;
\q1 we shall be filled with the good things of your house;
\q2 your temple is holy.
\b
\q1 \v 6 \add You are\add* wonderful in righteousness.
\q2 Listen to us, O God our Saviour;
\q1 the hope of all the ends of the earth,
\q2 and of them \add that are\add* on the sea afar off:
\q1 \v 7 who dost establish\f + \fr 64:7 \fqa Gr. \ft prepare.\f* the mountains in your strength,
\q2 being girded about with power;
\q1 \v 8 who trouble the depth of the sea,
\q2 the sounds of its waves.
\q1 \v 9 The nations shall be troubled, and they who inhabit the ends \add of the earth\add* shall be afraid of your signs;
\q2 you will cause the outgoings of morning and evening to rejoice.
\b
\q1 \v 10 You have visited the earth, and saturated it;
\q2 you have abundantly enriched it.
\q1 The river of God is filled with water;
\q2 you have prepared their food, for thus is the preparation \add of it\add*.
\q1 \v 11 Saturate her furrows, multiply her fruits; \add the crop\add* springing up shall rejoice in its drops.
\q1 \v 12 You will bless the crown of the year \add because\add* of your goodness;
\q2 and your plains shall be filled with fatness.
\q1 \v 13 The mountains of the wilderness shall be enriched;
\q2 and the hills shall gird themselves with joy.
\q1 \v 14 The rams of the flock are clothed \add with wool\add*,
\q2 and the valleys shall abound in corn;
\q1 they shall cry aloud,
\q2 yes they shall sing hymns.
\c 65
\s1 Make a Joyful Noise
\d \v 1 For the end, a Song of a Psalm of resurrection.  
\p
\q1 Shout to God,
\q2 all the earth.
\q1 \v 2 O sing praises to his name;
\q2 give glory to his praise.
\q1 \v 3 Say to God, “How awful are your works!
\q2 through the greatness of your power yours enemies shall lie to you.
\q1 \v 4 Let all the earth worship you,
\q2 and sing to you;
\q2 let them sing to your name.” Pause.
\q1 \v 5 Come and behold the works of God; \add he is\add* terrible in \add his\add* counsels beyond the children of men.
\q1 \v 6 Who turns the sea into dry land;
\q2 they shall go through the river on foot;
\q2 there shall we rejoice in him,
\q1 \v 7 who by his power is Lord\f + \fr 65:7 \fqa Or, \ft has dominion for ever.\f* over the age,
\q2 his eyes look upon the nations;
\q2 let not them who provoke \add him\add* be exalted in themselves. Pause.
\q1 \v 8 Bless our God, you Gentiles,
\q2 and make the voice of his praise to be heard;
\q1 \v 9 who quickens my soul in life,
\q2 and does not suffer my feet to be moved.
\b
\q1 \v 10 For you, O God, have proved us;
\q2 you have tried us with fire as silver is tried.
\q1 \v 11 You brought us into the snare;
\q2 you laid afflictions on our back.
\q1 \v 12 You did mount men upon our heads;
\q2 we went through fire and water;
\q2 but you brought us out into \add a place of\add* refreshment.
\b
\q1 \v 13 I will go into yours house with whole-burnt-offerings;
\q2 I will pay you my vows,
\q1 \v 14 which my lips framed,
\q2 and my mouth uttered in my affliction.
\q1 \v 15 I will offer to you whole-burnt-sacrifices full of marrow,
\q2 with incense and rams;
\q2 I will sacrifice to you oxen with goats. Pause.
\q1 \v 16 Come, hear,
\q2 and I will tell, all you that fear God, how great things he has done for my soul.
\q1 \v 17 I cried to him with my mouth,
\q2 and exalted him with my tongue.
\q1 \v 18 If I have regarded iniquity in my heart,
\q2 let not the Lord listen \add to me\add*.
\q1 \v 19 Therefore God has hearkened to me;
\q2 he has attended to the voice of my prayer.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Blessed be God, who has not turned away my prayer,
\q2 nor his mercy from me.
\c 66
\s1 May God Cause His Face to Shine upon Us
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David among the Hymns.
\q1 \v 2 God be merciful to us, and bless us; \add and\add* cause his face to shine upon us. Pause.
\q1 \v 3 That \add men\add* may know your way on the earth,
\q2 your salvation among all nations.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Let the nations, O God, give thanks to you;
\q2 let all the nations give thanks to you.
\q1 \v 5 Let the nations rejoice and exult,
\q2 for you shall judge the peoples in equity,
\q2 and shall guide the nations on the earth. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 Let the peoples, O God, give thanks to you;
\q2 let all the peoples give thanks to you.
\b
\q1 \v 7 The earth has yielded her fruit;
\q2 let God, our God, bless us.
\q1 \v 8 Let God bless us;
\q2 and let all the ends of the earth fear him.
\c 67
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of a Song by David.
\s1 God’s Enemies Are Scattered
\q1 \v 2 Let God arise, and let his enemies be scattered;
\q2 and let them who hate him flee from before him.
\q1 \v 3 As smoke vanishes, let them vanish:
\q2 as wax melts before the fire,
\q1 so let the sinners
\q2 perish from before God.
\q1 \v 4 But let the righteous rejoice;
\q2 let them exult before God:
\q2 let them be delighted with joy.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Sing to God,
\q2 sing praises to his name:
\q1 make a way for him that rides upon the west
\q2 (the Lord is his name)
\q2 and exult before him. They shall be troubled before the face of him,
\q1 \v 6 \add who is\add* the father of the orphans,
\q2 and judge of the widows: \add such is\add*
\q2 God in his holy place.
\q1 \v 7 God settles the solitary in a house;
\q2 leading forth prisoners mightily,
\q2 also them who act provokingly, \add even\add* them who dwell in tombs.
\b
\q1 \v 8 O God, when you went forth before your people,
\q2 when you went through the wilderness; Pause:
\q1 \v 9 the earth quaked, yes, the heavens dropped \add water\add*
\q2 at the presence of the God of Sinai,
\q2 at the presence of the God of Israel.
\q1 \v 10 O God, you will grant to yours inheritance a gracious rain;
\q2 for it was weary, but you did refresh it.
\q1 \v 11 Your creatures\f + \fr 67:11 \ft See the \fqa Hebrew.\f* dwell in it:
\q2 you have in your goodness prepared for the poor.
\b
\q1 \v 12 The Lord God will give a word to them who preach \add it\add* in
\q2 a great company.
\q1 \v 13 “The king of the forces of the beloved, of the beloved, \add will\add* even \add grant them\add*
\q2 for the beauty of the house to divide the spoils.
\q1 \v 14 Even if you should lie among the lots, \add you shall have\add*
\q2 the wings of a dove covered with silver,
\q2 and her breast with yellow gold.”\f + \fr 67:14 \fqa Lit. \ft greenness of gold.\f*
\q1 \v 15 When the heavenly One scatters kings upon it,
\q2 they shall be made snow-white in Selmon.
\b
\q1 \v 16 The mountain of God is a rich mountain;
\q2 a swelling mountain,\f + \fr 67:16 \fqa Gr. \ft curdled like cheese.\f* a rich mountain.
\q1 \v 17 Wherefore do you conceive \add evil\add*, you swelling mountains? \add this is\add* the mountain which God has delighted to dwell in;
\q2 yes, the Lord will dwell \add in it\add* for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 18 The chariots of God are ten thousand fold,
\q2 thousands of rejoicing ones:
\q1 the Lord is among them,
\q2 in Sinai, in the holy place.
\q1 \v 19 \f + \fr 67:19 \fqa Gr. \ft having gone up.\f*Thou art gone up on high,
\q2 you have led captivity captive,
\q1 you have received gifts for man, yes, for \add they were\add* rebellious,
\q2 that you might dwell among them.
\q1 \v 20 Blessed be the Lord God, blessed be the Lord daily; and
\q2 the God of our salvation shall prosper us. Pause.
\q1 \v 21 Our God is the God of salvation;
\q2 and to the Lord belong the issues from death.
\q1 \v 22 But God shall crush the heads of his enemies;
\q2 the hairy crown of them who go on in their trespasses.
\q1 \v 23 The Lord said, “I will bring again from Bashan, I will bring \add my people\add* again through the depths of the sea.
\q1 \v 24 That your foot may be dipped in blood, \add and\add* the tongue of your dogs \add be stained\add* with that of \add yours\add* enemies.”
\b
\q1 \v 25 Your goings, O God, have been seen;
\q2 the goings of my God, the king, in the sanctuary.
\q1 \v 26 The princes went first,
\q2 next before the players on instruments,
\q2 in the midst of damsels playing on timbrels.
\q1 \v 27 Praise God in the congregations,
\q2 the Lord from the fountains of Israel.
\q1 \v 28 There is Benjamin the younger \add one\add* in ecstasy,
\q2 the princes of Judah their rulers,
\q2 the princes of Zebulun, the princes of Naphtali.
\b
\q1 \v 29 O God, command you your strength:
\q2 strengthen, O God,
\q2 this which you have worked in us.
\q1 \v 30 Because of your temple at Jerusalem
\q2 shall kings bring presents to you.
\q1 \v 31 Rebuke the wild beasts of the reed:
\q2 let the crowd of bulls with the heifers of the nations \add be rebuked\add*,
\q1 so that those who have been proved with silver may not be shut out:
\q2 scatter you the nations that wish for wars.
\q1 \v 32 Ambassadors shall arrive out of Egypt;
\q2 Ethiopia shall hurry \add to stretch out\add* her hand readily to God.
\b
\q1 \v 33 Sing to God, you kingdoms of the earth;
\q2 sing psalms to the Lord. Pause.
\q1 \v 34 Sing to God that \f + \fr 67:34 \fqa Gr. \ft mounts, \fqa or, \ft has mounted.\f*rides on the heaven of heaven, eastward:
\q2 lo, he will utter a mighty sound with his voice.
\q1 \v 35 Give you glory to God:
\q2 his excellency is over Israel,
\q2 and his power is in the clouds.
\q1 \v 36 God is wonderful \f + \fr 67:36 \fqa Or, \ft among his holy ones.\f*in his holy \add places\add*,
\q2 the God of Israel:
\q2 he will give power and strength to his people:
\q1 blessed be God.
\c 68
\d \v 1 For the end, \add a Psalm\add* of David, for \f + \fr 68:1 \ft See \xt Ps. 44. \ft title.\f*alternate \add strains\add*.
\s1 The Waters Are up to My Neck
\q1 \v 2 Save me, O God;
\q2 for the waters have come in to my soul.
\q1 \v 3 I am stuck fast in deep mire, and there is no standing:
\q1 I am come in to the depths of the sea,
\q2 and a storm has overwhelmed me.
\q1 \v 4 I am weary \add of\add* crying,
\q2 my throat has become hoarse;
\q1 my eyes have failed
\q2 by my waiting on my God.
\q1 \v 5 They who hate me without a cause are more than the hairs of my head:
\q1 my enemies that persecute me unrighteously
\q2 are strengthened:
\q1 then I \f + \fr 68:5 \fqa Or, \ft paid for, \fqa or, \ft made up for.\f*restored
\q2 that which I took not away.
\b
\q1 \v 6 O God, you know my foolishness;
\q2 and my transgressions are not hidden from you.
\q1 \v 7 Let not them who wait on you, O Lord of hosts,
\q2 be ashamed on my account:
\q1 let not them who seek you,
\q2 be ashamed on my account, O God of Israel.
\q1 \v 8 For I have suffered reproach for your sake;
\q2 shame has covered my face.
\q1 \v 9 I became strange to my brothers,
\q2 and a stranger to my mother's children.
\q1 \v 10 \x + \xo 68:10 \xt John 2. 17; Rom. 15. 3.\x* For the zeal of yours house has eaten me up;
\q2 and the reproaches of them who reproached you are fallen upon me.
\q1 \v 11 And I bowed down my soul with fasting,
\q2 and that was made my reproach.
\q1 \v 12 And I put on sackcloth for my covering;
\q2 and I became a proverb to them.
\q1 \v 13 They who sit in the gate talked against me,
\q2 and they who drank wine sang against me.
\b
\q1 \v 14 But I \add will cry\add* to you, O Lord, in my prayer; O God, it is a propitious time:
\q1 in the multitude of your mercy hear me,
\q2 in the truth of your salvation.
\q1 \v 15 Save me from the mire, that I stick not \add in it\add*:
\q1 let me be delivered from them who hate me,
\q2 and from the deep waters.
\q1 \v 16 Let not the waterflood drown me,
\q2 nor let the deep swallow me up;
\q2 neither let the well shut its mouth upon me.
\q1 \v 17 Hear me, O Lord;
\q2 for your mercy is good:
\q2 according to the multitude of your compassions look upon me.
\q1 \v 18 And turn not away your face from your \f + \fr 68:18 \fqa Or, \ft son.\f*servant;
\q2 for I am afflicted:
\q2 hear me quickly.
\q1 \v 19 Draw near to my soul and redeem it:
\q2 deliver me because of my enemies.
\b
\q1 \v 20 For you know my reproach, and my shame, and my confusion;
\q2 all that afflict me are before you.
\q1 \v 21 My soul has waited for reproach and misery;
\q2 and I waited for one to grieve with me, but there was none; and for one to comfort me,
\q2 but I found none.
\q1 \v 22 They gave \add me\add* also gall for my food,
\q2 and made me drink vinegar for my thirst.
\b
\q1 \v 23 \x + \xo 68:23 \xt Rom. 11. 9,10.\x* Let their table before them be for a snare,
\q2 and for a repayment, and for a stumbling-block.
\q1 \v 24 Let their eyes be darkened that they should not see;
\q2 and bow down their back continually.
\q1 \v 25 Pour out your wrath upon them,
\q2 and let the fury of your anger take hold on them.
\q1 \v 26 \x + \xo 68:26 \xt Acts 1. 20.\x* Let their habitation be made desolate;
\q2 and let there be no inhabitant in their tents:
\q1 \v 27 because they persecuted him whom you have smitten;
\q2 and they have added to the grief of my wounds.
\q1 \v 28 Add iniquity to their iniquity;
\q2 and let them not come into your righteousness.
\q1 \v 29 Let them be blotted out of the book of the living,
\q2 and let them not be written with the righteous.
\b
\q1 \v 30 I am poor and sorrowful;
\q2 but the salvation of your countenance has helped me.
\q1 \v 31 I will praise the name of my God with a song,
\q2 I will magnify him with praise;
\q1 \v 32 and \add this\add* shall please God
\q2 more than a young calf having horns and hoofs.
\q1 \v 33 Let the poor see and rejoice;
\q2 seek the Lord diligently, and you shall live.
\q1 \v 34 For the Lord hears the poor,
\q2 and does not set at nothing his fettered ones.
\b
\q1 \v 35 Let the heavens and the earth praise him,
\q2 the sea, and all things moving in them.
\q1 \v 36 For God will save Zion,
\q2 and the cities of Judea shall be built; and \add men\add* shall dwell there, and inherit it.
\q1 \v 37 And the seed of his servants shall possess it,
\q2 and they who love his name shall dwell therein.
\c 69
\d \v 1 For the end, by David for a remembrance, that the Lord may save me.
\s1 Hurry, O LORD, to Help Me!
\q1 \v 2 Draw near, O God,
\q2 to my help.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Let them be ashamed and confounded that seek my soul:
\q1 let them be turned backward and put to shame,
\q2 that wish me evil.
\q1 \v 4 Let them who say to me, “Aha, aha,”
\q2 be turned back and put to shame immediately.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Let all that seek you exult and be glad in you:
\q1 and let those that love your salvation say continually, “Let God be magnified.”
\q1 \v 6 But I am poor and needy;
\q2 O God, help me:
\q1 you are my helper and deliverer,
\q2 O Lord, delay not.
\c 70
\s1 Be My Rock of Refuge
\d \v 1 By David, \add a Psalm sung by\add* the sons of Jonadab, and the first that were taken captive.  
\p
\q1 O Lord, I have hoped in you:
\q2 let me never be put to shame.
\q1 \v 2 In your righteousness deliver me and rescue me:
\q2 incline your ear to me, and save me.
\q1 \v 3 Be to me a protecting God,
\q2 and a strong hold to save me:
\q1 for you are my fortress
\q2 and my refuge.
\q1 \v 4 Deliver me, O my God, from the hand of the sinner,
\q2 from the hand of the transgressor and unjust man.
\b
\q1 \v 5 For you are my support, O Lord; O Lord, \add you are\add* my hope from my youth.
\q1 \v 6 On you have I been stayed from the womb:
\q2 from the belly of my mother you are my protector:
\q2 of you is my praise continually.\f + \fr 70:6 \fqa Gr. \ft hymn singing.\f*
\q1 \v 7 I am become as it were a wonder to many; but you are \add my\add* strong helper.
\q1 \v 8 Let my mouth be filled with praise, that I may hymn your glory, \add and\add* your majesty all the day.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Cast me not off at the time of old age;
\q2 forsake me not when my strength fails.
\q1 \v 10 For my enemies have spoken against me;
\q2 and they who lay wait for my soul have taken counsel together,
\q1 \v 11 saying, “God has forsaken him:
\q2 persecute you and take him; for there is none to deliver \add him\add*.
\b
\q1 \v 12 O God, go not far from me,
\q2 O my God, draw near to my help.
\q1 \v 13 Let those that plot against my soul
\q2 be ashamed and utterly fail:
\q1 let those that seek my hurt
\q2 be clothed with shame and dishonour.
\b
\q1 \v 14 But I will hope continually,
\q2 and will praise you more and more.
\q1 \v 15 My mouth shall declare your righteousness openly, \add and\add* your salvation all the day;
\q2 for I am not acquainted with the affairs \add of men\add*.
\q1 \v 16 I will go on in the might of the Lord:
\q2 O Lord, I will make mention of your righteousness only.
\b
\q1 \v 17 O God, you have taught me from my youth,
\q2 and until now will I declare your wonders;
\q1 \v 18 even until I am old and advanced in years.
\q2 O God, forsake me not;
\q1 until I shall have declared yours arm
\q2 to all the generation that is to come:
\q1 \v 19 even your power and your righteousness, O God, up to the highest \add heavens, even\add*
\q2 the mighty works which you have done:
\q2 O God, who is like to you?
\b
\q1 \v 20 What afflictions many and sore have you showed me!
\q2 yet you did turn and quicken me,
\q1 and brought me again
\q2 from the depths of the earth.
\q1 \v 21 You did multiply your righteousness, and did turn and comfort me, and brought me again out of the depths of the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 22 I will also therefore give thanks to you, O God, \add because of\add* your truth, on an instrument of psalmody:
\q1 I will sing psalms to you on the harp,
\q2 O Holy One of Israel.
\q1 \v 23 My lips shall rejoice
\q2 when I sing to you;
\q1 and my soul,
\q2 which you have redeemed.
\q1 \v 24 Moreover also my tongue shall dwell all the day\f + \fr 70:24 \fqa Lit. \ft meditate.\f*
\q2 upon your righteousness;
\q1 when they shall be ashamed and confounded
\q2 that seek my hurt.
\c 71
\s1 Endow the King with Your Justice
\d \v 1 For Solomon.  
\p
\q1 O God, give your judgment to the king,
\q2 and your righteousness to the king's son;
\q1 \v 2 \add that he may\add* judge your people with righteousness,
\q2 and your poor with judgment.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Let the mountains
\q2 and the hills raise peace to your people:
\q1 \v 4 he shall judge the poor of the people in righteousness,
\q2 and save the children of the needy;
\q2 and shall bring low the false accuser.
\q1 \v 5 And he shall continue as long as the sun,
\q2 and before the moon
\q2 for ever.
\q1 \v 6 He shall come down as rain upon a fleece;
\q2 and as drops falling upon the earth.
\q1 \v 7 In his days shall righteousness spring up;
\q2 and abundance of peace
\q2 till the moon be removed.
\b
\q1 \v 8 And he shall have dominion from sea to sea,
\q2 and from the river to the ends of the earth.
\q1 \v 9 The Ethiopians shall fall down before him;
\q2 and his enemies shall lick the dust.
\q1 \v 10 The kings of Tarshish, and the isles, shall bring presents:
\q2 the kings of the Arabians and Saba shall offer gifts.
\q1 \v 11 And all kings shall worship him;
\q2 all the Gentiles shall serve him.
\b
\q1 \v 12 For he has delivered the poor from the oppressor;
\q2 and the needy who had no helper.
\q1 \v 13 He shall spare the poor and needy,
\q2 and shall deliver the souls of the needy.
\q1 \v 14 He shall redeem their souls from usury and injustice: and their name \add shall be\add* precious before him.
\b
\q1 \v 15 And he shall live,
\q2 and there shall be given him of the gold of Arabia: and \add men\add* shall pray for him continually; \add and\add* all the day shall they praise him.
\q1 \v 16 There shall be an establishment on the earth
\q2 on the tops of the mountains:
\q1 the fruit thereof shall be exalted above Lebanon,
\q2 and they of the city shall flourish as grass of the earth.
\q1 \v 17 Let his name be blessed for ever:
\q2 his name shall endure longer than the sun:\f + \fr 71:17 \fqa Gr. \ft before.\f*
\q1 and all the tribes of the earth shall be blessed in him:
\q2 all nations shall call him blessed.
\b
\q1 \v 18 Blessed is the Lord God of Israel,
\q2 who alone does wonders.
\q1 \v 19 And blessed is his glorious name for ever, even for ever and ever:
\q2 and all the earth shall be filled with his glory.
\q1 So be it, so be it.
\d \v 20 The hymns of David the son of Jesse are ended.
\c 72
\ms BOOK III
\s1 Surely God Is Good to Israel
\d \v 1 A Psalm for Asaph.  
\p
\q1 How good is God to Israel,
\q2 to the upright in heart!
\q1 \v 2 But my feet were almost overthrown;
\q2 my goings very nearly slipped.
\q1 \v 3 For I was jealous of the transgressors,
\q2 beholding the tranquillity of sinners.
\b
\q1 \v 4 For there is no sign of reluctance in their death: and \add they have\add* firmness under their affliction.
\q1 \v 5 They are not in the troubles of \add other\add* men;
\q2 and they shall not be scourged with \add other\add* men.
\q1 \v 6 Therefore pride has possessed them;
\q2 they have clothed themselves with their injustice and ungodliness.
\q1 \v 7 Their injustice shall go forth as out of fatness:
\q2 they have fulfilled their intention.
\q1 \v 8 They have taken counsel and spoken in wickedness:
\q2 they have uttered unrighteousness loftily.
\q1 \v 9 They have set their mouth against heaven,
\q2 and their tongue has gone through upon the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 10 Therefore shall my people return here:
\q2 and full days shall be found with them.\f + \fr 72:10 \fqa Gr. \ft in.\f*
\q1 \v 11 And they said, “How does God know?
\q2 and is there knowledge\f + \fr 72:11 \fqa Gr. \ft if there is.\f* in the Most High?”
\q1 \v 12 Behold, these \add are\add* the sinners, and they who prosper
\q2 always: they have possessed wealth.
\b
\q1 \v 13 And I said, Truly in vain have I justified my heart,
\q2 and washed my hands in innocency.
\q1 \v 14 For I was plagued all the day, and my reproof \add was\add* every morning.
\q1 \v 15 If I said, “I will speak thus”; behold, I \add should\add* have broken covenant with the generation of your children.
\q1 \v 16 And I undertook to understand this, \add but\add* it is too hard for me,
\q1 \v 17 until I go into the sanctuary of God; \add and so\add* understand the latter end.
\b
\q1 \v 18 Surely you have appointed \add judgments\add* to them because of their crafty dealings:
\q2 you have cast them down when they were lifted up.
\q1 \v 19 How have they become desolate!
\q2 suddenly they have failed: they have perished because of their iniquity.
\q1 \v 20 As the dream of one awakening, O Lord, in your city you will despise their image.
\b
\q1 \v 21 For my heart has rejoiced,
\q2 and my reins have been gladdened.\f + \fr 72:21 \fqa Gr. \ft changed.\f*
\q1 \v 22 But I \add was\add* vile and knew not:
\q2 I became brutish before you.
\b
\q1 \v 23 Yet I am continually with you:
\q2 you have holden my right hand.
\q1 \v 24 You have guided me by your counsel,
\q2 and you have taken me to yourself with glory.
\q1 \v 25 For what have I in heaven \add but you\add*?
\q2 and what have I desired upon the earth beside you?
\q1 \v 26 My heart and my flesh have failed: \add but\add* God \add is the strength\add* of my heart,
\q2 and God is my portion for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 27 For, behold, they who remove themselves far from you shall perish:
\q2 you have destroyed every one that goes a whoring from you.
\q1 \v 28 But it is good for me to cleave close to God,
\q2 to put my trust in the Lord;
\q2 that I may proclaim all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion.
\c 73
\s1 Why Have You Rejected Us Forever?
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of \f + \fr 73:1 \fqa Gr. \ft understanding.\f*instruction for Asaph.  
\p
\q1 Wherefore have you rejected \add us\add*, O God, for ever? \add wherefore\add* is your wrath kindled
\q2 against the sheep of your pasture?
\q1 \v 2 Remember your congregation
\q2 which you have purchased from the beginning;
\q1 you did ransom the rod of yours inheritance;
\q2 this mount Zion wherein you have dwelt.
\q1 \v 3 Lift up yours hands against their pride continually; \add because of\add* all that the enemy has done wickedly in your holy places.
\b
\q1 \v 4 And they who hate you have boasted in the midst of your feast;
\q2 they have set up their standards for signs,
\q1 \v 5 ignorantly as it were in the entrance above;
\q2 \v 6 they cut down its doors at once with axes as in a wood of trees; they have broken it down with hatchet and stone cutter.
\q1 \v 7 They have burnt your sanctuary with fire to the ground;
\q2 they have profaned the \f + \fr 73:7 \fqa Gr. \ft tabernacle.\f*habitation of your name.
\b
\q1 \v 8 They have said in their heart, \add even\add* all their kindred together,
\q2 “Come, let us abolish the feasts of the Lord from the earth.”
\q1 \v 9 We have not seen our signs;
\q2 there is no longer a prophet; and \add God\add* will not know us any more.
\q1 \v 10 How long, O God, shall the enemy reproach?
\q2 shall the enemy provoke your name for ever?
\q1 \v 11 Wherefore turn you away yours hand, and your right hand
\q2 from the midst of your bosom for ever?
\b
\q1 \v 12 But God is our King \f + \fr 73:12 \fqa Gr. \ft before the age.\f*of old;
\q2 he has worked salvation in the midst of the earth.
\q1 \v 13 You did establish the sea, in your might,
\q2 you did break to pieces the heads of the \f + \fr 73:13 \fqa Or, \ft serpents.\f*dragons in the water.
\q1 \v 14 You did break to pieces the heads of the dragon;
\q2 you did give him \add for\add* meat to the Ethiopian nations.
\q1 \v 15 You did cleave fountains and torrents;
\q2 you driedst up \f + \fr 73:15 \fqa Heb. \ft rivers of Etham.\f*mighty rivers.
\q1 \v 16 The day is yours, and the night is yours;
\q2 you have prepared the sun and the moon.
\q1 \v 17 You have made all the borders of the earth;
\q2 you have made summer and spring.
\b
\q1 \v 18 Remember this your creation: an enemy has reproached the Lord,
\q2 and a foolish people has provoked your name.
\q1 \v 19 Deliver not to the wild beasts a soul that gives praise to you:
\q2 forget not for ever the souls of your poor.
\q1 \v 20 Look upon your covenant: for the dark \add places\add* of the earth are filled with the habitations of iniquity.
\q1 \v 21 Let not the afflicted and shamed one be rejected:
\q2 the poor and needy shall praise your name.
\b
\q1 \v 22 Arise, O God, plead your cause:
\q2 remember your reproaches that come from the foolish one all the day.
\q1 \v 23 Forget not the voice of your suppliants:
\q2 let the pride of them who hate you continually ascend before you.
\c 74
\d \v 1 For the end, Destroy not, a Psalm of a Song for Asaph.
\s1 God’s Righteous Judgment
\q1 \v 2 We will give thanks to you, O God,
\q2 we will give thanks, and call upon your name:
\q2 I will declare all your wonderful works.
\b
\q1 \v 3 “When I shall take a set time,
\q2 I will judge righteously.
\q1 \v 4 The earth is dissolved, and all that dwell in it:
\q2 I have strengthened its pillars. Pause.
\q1 \v 5 I said to the transgressors, ‘Do not transgress’;
\q2 and to the sinners, ‘Lift not up the horn.
\q1 \v 6 Lift not up your horn on high;
\q2 speak not unrighteousness against God.’”
\b
\q1 \v 7 For \add good comes\add* neither from the east, nor from the west,
\q2 nor from the desert mountains.
\q1 \v 8 For God is the judge;
\q2 he puts down one, and raises up another.
\q1 \v 9 For \add there is\add* a cup in the hand of the Lord,
\q2 full of unmingled wine; and he has \f + \fr 74:9 \fqa Gr. \ft from this into that.\f*turned \add it\add* from side to side,
\q2 but its dregs have not been wholly poured out;
\q2 all the sinners of the earth shall drink \add them\add*.
\b
\q1 \v 10 But I will exult for ever:
\q2 I will sing praises to the God of Jacob.
\b
\q1 \v 11 “And I will break all the horns of sinners;
\q2 but the horns of the righteous one shall be exalted.”
\c 75
\d \v 1 For the end, among the Hymns, a Psalm for Asaph; a Song for the Assyrian.
\s1 God’s Name Is Great in Israel
\q1 \v 2 God is known in Judea;
\q2 his name is great in Israel.
\q1 \v 3 And his place has been in peace,\f + \fr 75:3 \fqa Or, \ft Salem, see the Hebrew.\f*
\q2 and his dwelling-place in Zion.
\q1 \v 4 There he broke the power of the bows,
\q2 the shield, and the sword, and the battle. Pause.
\q1 \v 5 You dost wonderfully shine forth from the everlasting mountains.
\q1 \v 6 All the simple ones in heart were troubled; all the men of wealth have slept their sleep, and
\q2 have found nothing in their hands.
\q1 \v 7 At your rebuke, O God of Jacob,
\q2 the riders on horses slumbered.
\b
\q1 \v 8 You are terrible; and who shall withstand you,
\q2 because of your anger?
\q1 \v 9 You did cause judgment to be heard from heaven;
\q2 the earth feared, and was still,
\q1 \v 10 when God arose to judgment,
\q2 to save all the meek in heart. Pause.
\q1 \v 11 For the inward thought of man shall give thanks to you:
\q2 and the memorial of his inward thought shall keep a feast to you.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Vow, and pay \add your vows\add* to the Lord our God;
\q2 all that are round about him shall bring gifts, \add even\add* to him that is terrible,
\q1 \v 13 and that takes away the spirits of princes;
\q2 to him that is terrible among the kings of the earth.
\c 76
\d \v 1 For the end, for Jeduthun, a Psalm of Asaph.
\s1 In the Day of Trouble I Sought the Lord
\q1 \v 2 I cried to the Lord with my voice, yes, my voice \add was addressed\add* to God; and he gave heed to me.
\q1 \v 3 In the day of my affliction I earnestly sought the Lord; \add even\add* with my hands by night before him, and I was not deceived;
\q2 my soul refused to be comforted.
\q1 \v 4 I remembered God, and rejoiced;
\q2 I poured out my complaint, and my soul fainted. Pause.
\q1 \v 5 All my enemies set a watch \add against me\add*:
\q2 I was troubled, and spoke not.
\q1 \v 6 I considered the days of old,
\q2 and remembered ancient years.
\q1 \v 7 And I meditated;
\q2 I communed with my heart by night, and diligently searched my spirit, \add saying\add*,
\b
\q1 \v 8 “Will the Lord cast off for ever?
\q2 and will he be well pleased no more?
\q1 \v 9 Will he cut off his mercy for ever,
\q2 even for ever and ever?
\q1 \v 10 Will God forget to pity?
\q2 or will he shut up his compassions in his wrath?” Pause.
\q1 \v 11 And I said, “Now I have begun”;
\q2 this is the change of the right hand of the Most High.
\b
\q1 \v 12 I remembered the works of the Lord;
\q2 for I will remember your wonders from the beginning.
\q1 \v 13 And I will meditate on all your works,
\q2 and will consider your doings.
\q1 \v 14 O God, your way is in the sanctuary;
\q2 who is a great God as our God?
\q1 \v 15 You are the God that do wonders;
\q2 you have made known your power among the nations.
\q1 \v 16 You have with yours arm redeemed your people,
\q2 the sons of Jacob and Joseph. Pause.
\q1 \v 17 The waters saw you, O God,
\q2 the waters saw you, and feared;
\q2 and the depths were troubled.
\q1 \v 18 \add There was\add* an abundant sound of waters:
\q2 the clouds uttered a voice;
\q2 for yours arrows went abroad.
\q1 \v 19 The voice of your thunder was abroad,
\q2 and around your lightnings appeared to the world;
\q2 the earth trembled and quaked.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Your way is in the sea,
\q2 and your paths in many waters,
\q2 and your footsteps cannot be known.
\q1 \v 21 You did guide your people as sheep
\q2 by the hand of Moses and Aaron.
\c 77
\s1 I Will Open My Mouth in Parables
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of \f + \fr 77:1 \fqa Gr. \ft understanding.\f*instruction for Asaph.  
\p
\q1 Pay attention, O my people, to my law:
\q2 incline your ear to the words of my mouth.
\b
\q1 \v 2 \x + \xo 77:2 \xt Mat. 13. 35.\x* I will open my mouth in parables:
\q2 I will utter dark sayings \add which have been\add* from the beginning.
\q1 \v 3 All which we have heard and known,
\q2 and our fathers have declared to us.
\q1 \v 4 They were not hid from their children to a second generation; \add the fathers\add*
\q2 declaring the praises of the Lord,
\q1 and his mighty acts,
\q2 and his wonders which he worked.
\b
\q1 \v 5 And he raised up a testimony in Jacob,
\q2 and appointed a law in Israel,
\q1 which he commanded our fathers,
\q2 to make it known to their children:
\q1 \v 6 that another generation might know,
\q2 even the sons which should be born;
\q2 and they should arise and declare them to their children.
\q1 \v 7 That they might set their hope on God,
\q2 and not forget the works of God,
\q2 but diligently seek his commandments.
\q1 \v 8 That they should not be as their fathers,
\q2 a perverse and provoking generation;
\q1 a generation which set not its heart aright,
\q2 and its spirit was not \f + \fr 77:8 \fqa Or, \ft faithfully secured.\f*steadfast with God.
\b
\q1 \v 9 The children of Ephraim, bending and shooting \add with\add* the bow, turned \add back\add* in the day of battle.
\q1 \v 10 They kept not the covenant of God,
\q2 and would not walk in his law.
\q1 \v 11 And they forgot his benefits,
\q2 and his miracles which he \add had\add* showed them;
\q1 \v 12 the miracles which he worked before their fathers,
\q2 in the land of Egypt, in the plain of Tanes.
\q1 \v 13 He clave the sea, and led them through:
\q2 he made the waters to stand as \add in\add* a bottle.
\q1 \v 14 And he guided them with a cloud by day,
\q2 and all the night with a light of fire.
\q1 \v 15 He clave a rock in the wilderness,
\q2 and made them drink as in a great deep.
\q1 \v 16 And he brought water out of the rock,
\q2 and caused waters to flow down as rivers.
\b
\q1 \v 17 And they sinned yet more against him;
\q2 they provoked the Most High in the wilderness.
\q1 \v 18 And they tempted God in their hearts,
\q2 in asking meat for \add the desire of\add* their souls.
\q1 \v 19 They spoke also against God, and said,
\q2 “Will God be able to prepare a table in the wilderness?
\q1 \v 20 Forasmuch as he struck the rock, and the waters flowed,
\q2 and the torrents ran abundantly;
\q1 will he be able also to give bread,
\q2 or prepare a table for his people?”
\b
\q1 \v 21 Therefore the Lord heard, and was provoked:
\q2 and fire was kindled in Jacob,
\q1 and wrath went up
\q2 against Israel.
\q1 \v 22 Because they believed not in God,
\q2 and trusted not in his salvation.
\b
\q1 \v 23 Yet he commanded the clouds from above,
\q2 and opened the doors of heaven,
\q1 \v 24 and rained upon them manna to eat,
\q2 and gave them the bread of heaven.
\q1 \v 25 Man ate angels' bread; \x + \xo 77:25 \xt Mat. 6. 31.\x*
\q2 he sent them provision to the full.
\q1 \v 26 He removed the south wind from heaven;
\q2 and by his might he brought in the south-west wind.
\q1 \v 27 And he rained upon them flesh like dust,
\q2 and feathered birds like the sand of the seas.
\q1 \v 28 And they fell into the midst of their camp,
\q2 round about their tents.
\q1 \v 29 So they ate, and were completely filled;
\q2 and he gave them their desire.
\b
\q1 \v 30 They were not disappointed of their desire: \add but\add*
\q2 when their food was yet in their mouth,
\q1 \v 31 then the indignation of God rose up against them,
\q2 and slew the fattest of them,
\q2 and overthrew the choice men of Israel.
\b
\q1 \v 32 In the midst of all this they sinned yet more,
\q2 and believed not his miracles.
\q1 \v 33 And their days were consumed in vanity,
\q2 and their years with anxiety.
\q1 \v 34 When he slew them, they sought him:
\q2 and they returned and called betimes upon God.
\q1 \v 35 And they remembered that God was their helper,
\q2 and the most high God was their redeemer.
\b
\q1 \v 36 Yet they loved him \add only\add* with their mouth,
\q2 and lied to him with their tongue.
\q1 \v 37 For their heart \add was\add* not right with him,
\q2 nor were they steadfast in his covenant.
\b
\q1 \v 38 But he is compassionate, and will \f + \fr 77:38 \fqa Gr. \ft be propitious, \fqa or, \ft merciful to.\f*forgive their sins, and will not destroy \add them\add*:
\q1 yes, he will frequently turn away his wrath,
\q2 and will not kindle all his anger.
\q1 \v 39 And he remembered that they are flesh;
\q2 a wind that passes away, and returns not.
\b
\q1 \v 40 How often did they provoke him in the wilderness, \add and\add* anger him in a dry land!
\q1 \v 41 Yes, they turned back, and tempted God,
\q2 and provoked the Holy One of Israel.
\q1 \v 42 They remembered not his hand,
\q2 the day in which he delivered them from the hand of the oppressor.
\q1 \v 43 How he had worked his signs in Egypt,
\q2 and his wonders in the field of Tanes:
\b
\q1 \v 44 and had changed their rivers into blood;
\q2 and their \f + \fr 77:44 \fqa Gr. \ft rains, \fqa or, \ft showers.\f*streams, that they should not drink.
\q1 \v 45 He sent against them the dog-fly, and it devoured them;
\q2 and the frog, and it spoiled them.
\q1 \v 46 And he gave their fruit to the canker worm,
\q2 and their labours to the locust.
\q1 \v 47 He killed their vines with hail,
\q2 and their sycamores with frost.
\q1 \v 48 And he gave up their cattle to hail,
\q2 and their substance to the fire.
\b
\q1 \v 49 He sent out against them the fury of his anger,
\q2 wrath, and indignation, and affliction,
\q2 a message by evil angels.
\q1 \v 50 He made a way for his wrath;
\q2 he spared not their souls from death,
\q2 but consigned their cattle to death;
\q1 \v 51 and struck every first-born in the land of Egypt;
\q2 the first-fruits of their labours in the tents of Ham.
\b
\q1 \v 52 And he removed his people like sheep;
\q2 he led them as a flock in the wilderness.
\q1 \v 53 And he guided them with hope, and they feared not:
\q2 but the sea covered their enemies.
\q1 \v 54 And he brought them in to the mountain of his sanctuary,
\q2 this mountain which his right hand had purchased.
\q1 \v 55 And he cast out the nations from before them,
\q2 and made them to inherit by a line of inheritance,
\q2 and made the tribes of Israel to dwell in their tents.
\b
\q1 \v 56 Yet they tempted and provoked the most high God,
\q2 and kept not his testimonies.
\q1 \v 57 And they turned back, and broke covenant, even as also their fathers:
\q2 they became like a crooked bow.
\q1 \v 58 And they provoked him with their high places,
\q2 and moved him to jealousy with their carved images.
\b
\q1 \v 59 God heard and \f + \fr 77:59 \fqa Lit. \ft overlooked them. See \xt Acts 17. 30.\f*lightly regarded \add them\add*,
\q2 and greatly despised Israel.
\q1 \v 60 And he rejected the tabernacle of Shelah,
\q2 his tent where he dwelt among men.
\q1 \v 61 And he gave their strength into captivity,
\q2 and their beauty into the enemy's hand.
\q1 \v 62 And he \f + \fr 77:62 \fqa Gr. \ft shut up.\f*gave his people to the sword;
\q2 and disdained his inheritance.
\q1 \v 63 Fire devoured their young men;
\q2 and their virgins mourned not.
\q1 \v 64 Their priests fell by the sword;
\q2 and their widows shall not be wept for.
\b
\q1 \v 65 So the Lord awaked as one out of sleep, \add and\add*
\q2 as a mighty man who has been heated with wine.
\q1 \v 66 And he struck his enemies in the hinder parts:
\q2 he brought on them a perpetual reproach.
\b
\q1 \v 67 And he rejected the tabernacle of Joseph,
\q2 and chose not the tribe of Ephraim;
\q1 \v 68 but chose the tribe of Judah,
\q2 the mount Zion which he loved.
\q1 \v 69 And he built his sanctuary as \add the place\add* of unicorns;
\q2 he founded it for ever on the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 70 He chose David also his servant,
\q2 and took him up from the flocks of sheep.
\q1 \v 71 He took him from following the ewes great with young,
\q2 to be the shepherd of Jacob his servant,
\q2 and Israel his inheritance.
\q1 \v 72 So he tended them in the innocency of his heart;
\q2 and guided them by the skilfulness of his hands.
\c 78
\s1 A Prayer for Deliverance
\d \v 1 A Psalm for Asaph.  
\p
\q1 O God, the heathen are come into yours inheritance;
\q2 they have polluted your holy temple;
\q2 they have made Jerusalem a storehouse of fruits.
\q1 \v 2 They have given the dead bodies of your servants \add to be\add*
\q2 food for the birds of the sky,
\q2 the flesh of your holy ones for the wild beasts of the earth.
\q1 \v 3 They have shed their blood as water,
\q2 round about Jerusalem; and there was none to bury \add them\add*.
\q1 \v 4 We are become a reproach to our neighbours,
\q2 a scorn and derision to them \add that are\add* round about us.
\b
\q1 \v 5 How long, O Lord?
\q2 will you be angry for ever?
\q2 shall your jealousy burn like fire?
\q1 \v 6 Pour out your wrath upon the heathen
\q2 that have not known you,
\q1 and upon the kingdoms
\q2 which have not called upon your name.
\q1 \v 7 For they have devoured Jacob,
\q2 and laid his place waste.
\b
\q1 \v 8 Remember not our old transgressions;
\q2 let your tender mercies quickly prevent us;
\q2 for we are greatly impoverished.
\q1 \v 9 Help us, O God our Saviour;
\q2 for the glory of your name, O Lord,
\q1 deliver us;
\q2 and be merciful to our sins, for your name's sake.
\q1 \v 10 Lest perhaps they should say among the heathen,
\q2 “Where is their God?”
\q1 and let the avenging of your servants' blood
\q2 that has been shed be known among the heathen before our eyes.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Let the groaning of the prisoners come in before you;
\q2 according to the greatness of yours arm preserve the \f + \fr 78:11 \fqa i. e. \ft men appointed to die.\f*sons of the slain ones.
\q1 \v 12 Repay to our neighbours
\q2 sevenfold into their bosom their reproach, with which they have reproached you, O Lord.
\q1 \v 13 For we are your people and the sheep of your pasture;
\q2 we will give you thanks for ever;
\q1 we will declare your praise
\q2 throughout all generations.
\c 79
\d \v 1 For the \f + \fr 79:1 \ft See \xt Ps. 44, \ft and \xt 68, \ft titles.\f*end, for alternate \add strains\add*, a testimony for Asaph, a Psalm concerning the Assyrian.
\s1 Hear Us, O Shepherd of Israel
\q1 \v 2 Attend, O Shepherd of Israel,
\q2 who guide Joseph like a flock;
\q1 you who sitt upon the cherubs,
\q2 manifest yourself;
\q1 \v 3 before Ephraim and Benjamin and Manasseh,
\q2 stir up your power, and come to deliver us.
\q1 \v 4 Turn us, O God,
\q2 and cause your face to shine;
\q2 and we shall be delivered.
\b
\q1 \v 5 O Lord God of hosts,
\q2 how long are you angry
\q2 with the prayer of your servant?
\q1 \v 6 You will feed us with bread of tears;
\q2 and will cause us to drink tears by measure.
\q1 \v 7 You have made us a strife to our neighbours;
\q2 and our enemies have mocked at us.
\q1 \v 8 Turn us, O Lord God of hosts,
\q2 and cause your face to shine;
\q2 and we shall be saved. Pause.
\b
\q1 \v 9 You have transplanted a vine out of Egypt:
\q2 you have cast out the heathen, and planted it.
\q1 \v 10 You made a way before it,
\q2 and did cause its roots to strike, and the land was filled \add with it\add*.
\q1 \v 11 Its shadow covered the mountains, and its shoots \add equalled\add* the goodly cedars.\f + \fr 79:11 \fqa Gr. \ft Cedars of God, see \xt Gen. 30. 8; Jon. 3. 3; Acts 7. 20.\f*
\q1 \v 12 It sent forth its branches to the sea,
\q2 and its shoots to the river.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Wherefore have you broken down its hedge,
\q2 while all that pass by the way pluck it?
\q1 \v 14 The boar out of the wood has laid it waste,
\q2 and the wild beast has devoured it.
\q1 \v 15 O God of hosts, turn, we pray you: look on \add us\add* from heaven, and behold
\q1 and visit this vine;
\q2 \v 16 and restore that which your right hand has planted:
\q2 and look on the son of man whom you did strengthen for yourself.
\b
\q1 \v 17 \add It is\add* burnt with fire and dug up:
\q2 they shall perish at the rebuke of your presence.\f + \fr 79:17 \fqa Gr. \ft See \xt Ps. 20. 9.\f*
\q1 \v 18 Let your hand be upon the man of your right hand,
\q2 and upon the son of man whom you did strengthen for yourself.
\q1 \v 19 So will we not depart from you:
\q2 you shall quicken us, and we will call upon your name.
\q1 \v 20 Turn us, O Lord God of hosts,
\q2 and make your face to shine;
\q2 and we shall be saved.
\c 80
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm for Asaph,\f + \fr 80:1 \fqa Alex. \ft David.\f* concerning the wine-presses.
\s1 Sing for Joy to God Our Strength
\q1 \v 2 Rejoice you in God our helper;
\q2 shout aloud to the God of Jacob.
\q1 \v 3 Take a psalm, and produce the timbrel,
\q2 the pleasant psaltery with the harp.
\q1 \v 4 Blow the trumpet at the new moon,
\q2 in the glorious day of your feast.\f + \fr 80:4 \fqa Alex. \ft our.\f*
\q1 \v 5 For \add this\add* is an ordinance for Israel,
\q2 and a statute of the God of Jacob.
\q1 \v 6 He made it\f + \fr 80:6 \fqa Gr. \ft him, \fqa sc. \ft Israel.\f* \add to be\add* a testimony in Joseph,
\q2 when he came forth out of the land of Egypt:
\q2 he heard a language which he understood not.
\b
\q1 \v 7 “He removed his back from burdens:
\q2 his hands slaved in making the baskets.
\q1 \v 8 You did call upon me in trouble, and I delivered you;
\q2 I heard you in the secret place of the storm:
\q2 I proved you at the water of Strife.\f + \fr 80:8 \fqa Lit. \ft contradiction.\f* Pause.
\q1 \v 9 Hear, my people, and I will speak to you, O Israel; and I will testify to you: if you will listen to me;
\q1 \v 10 there shall be no new god in you;
\q2 nor shall you worship a strange god.
\q1 \v 11 For I am the Lord your God,
\q2 that brought you out of the land of Egypt:
\q1 open your mouth wide,
\q2 and I will fill it.
\b
\q1 \v 12 But my people hearkened not to my voice;
\q2 and Israel gave no heed to me.
\q1 \v 13 So I let them go after the ways of their own hearts:
\q2 they will go on in their own ways.
\q1 \v 14 If my people had hearkened to me,
\q2 if Israel had walked in my ways,
\q1 \v 15 I should have put down their enemies very quickly,
\q2 and should have laid my hand upon those that afflicted them.
\q1 \v 16 The Lord's enemies \add should have\add* lied to him: but their time\f + \fr 80:16 \fqa sc. \ft of the others.\f* shall be for ever.
\q1 \v 17 And he fed them with the fat of wheat;
\q2 and satisfied them with honey out of the rock.”
\c 81
\s1 God Presides in the Divine Assembly
\d \v 1 A Psalm for Asaph.  
\p
\q1 God stands in the assembly of gods; and in the midst \add of them\add* will judge gods.
\b
\q1 \v 2 “How long will you judge unrighteously,
\q2 and accept the persons of sinners? Pause.
\q1 \v 3 Judge the orphan and poor: do justice\f + \fr 81:3 \fqa Gr. \ft justify.\f* to the low and needy.
\q1 \v 4 Rescue the needy,
\q2 and deliver the poor out of the hand of the sinner.
\q1 \v 5 They know not, nor understand;
\q2 they walk on in darkness:
\q2 all the foundations of the earth shall be shaken.
\q1 \v 6 I have said, ‘You are gods; and all \add of you\add* children of the Most High.
\q1 \v 7 But you die as men,
\q2 and fall as one of the princes.’”
\b
\q1 \v 8 Arise, O God, judge the earth:
\q2 for you shall inherit all nations.
\c 82
\d \v 1 A Song of a Psalm for Asaph.
\s1 O God, Be Not Silent
\q1 \v 2 O God, who shall be compared to you? be not silent,
\q2 neither be still, O God.
\q1 \v 3 For behold, yours enemies have made a noise; and they who hate you
\q2 have lifted up the head.
\q1 \v 4 Against your people they have craftily imagined a device,
\q2 and have taken counsel against your saints.
\q1 \v 5 They have said, “Come, and let us utterly destroy them out of the nation;
\q2 and let the name of Israel be remembered no more at all.”
\b
\q1 \v 6 For they have taken counsel together with one consent:
\q2 they have made a confederacy against you;
\q1 \v 7 even the tents of the Idumeans, and the Ismaelites;
\q2 Moab, and the Hagrites;
\q1 \v 8 Gebal, and Ammon, and Amalek;
\q2 the Philistines also, with them who dwell at Tyre.
\q1 \v 9 Yes, Asshur too is come with them:
\q2 they have become a help to the children of Lot. Pause.
\q1 \v 10 Do you to them as to Midian, and to Sisera; as to Jabin at the brook of Kison.
\q1 \v 11 They were utterly destroyed at Aendor:
\q2 they became as dung for the earth.
\q1 \v 12 Make their princes as Oreb and Zeb,
\q2 and Zebee and Salmana; \add even\add* all their princes:
\q1 \v 13 who said, “Let us take to ourselves
\q2 the \f + \fr 82:13 \fqa Alex. \ft sanctuary.\f*altar of God as an inheritance.”
\b
\q1 \v 14 O my God, make them as a wheel;
\q2 as stubble before the face of the wind.
\q1 \v 15 As fire which shall burn up a wood,
\q2 as the flame may consume the mountains;
\q1 \v 16 so shall you persecute them with your tempest,
\q2 and trouble them in your anger.
\q1 \v 17 Fill their faces with dishonour;
\q2 so shall they seek your name, O Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 18 Let them be ashamed and troubled for evermore;
\q2 yes, let them be confounded and destroyed.
\q1 \v 19 And let them know that your name is Lord;
\q2 that you alone art Most High over all the earth.
\c 83
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm for the sons of Korah, concerning the wine-presses.
\s1 Better Is One Day in Your Courts
\q1 \v 2 How amiable are your tabernacles,
\q2 O Lord of hosts!
\q1 \v 3 My soul longs, and faints
\q2 for the courts of the Lord:
\q1 my heart and my flesh have exulted
\q2 in the living God.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Yes, the sparrow has found himself a home,
\q2 and the turtle-dove a nest for herself,
\q1 where she may lay her young, \add even\add* yours altars, O Lord of hosts, my King, and my God.
\q1 \v 5 Blessed are they who dwell in your house:
\q2 they will praise you evermore. Pause.
\q1 \v 6 Blessed is the man whose help is of you, O Lord;
\q2 in his heart he has purposed to go up
\q1 \v 7 the valley of weeping,
\q2 to the place which he has appointed, for \add there\add* the law-giver will grant blessings.
\q1 \v 8 They shall go from strength to strength:
\q2 the God of gods shall be seen in Zion.
\b
\q1 \v 9 O Lord God of hosts, hear my prayer:
\q2 listen, O God of Jacob. Pause.
\q1 \v 10 Behold, O God our defender,
\q2 and look upon the face of yours anointed.
\q1 \v 11 For one day in your courts is better
\q2 than thousands.
\q1 I would rather be an abject in the house of God,
\q2 than dwell in the tents of sinners.
\q1 \v 12 For the Lord loves mercy and truth:
\q2 God will give grace and glory:
\q1 the Lord will not withhold good things
\q2 from them who walk in innocence.
\b
\q1 \v 13 O Lord of hosts,
\q2 blessed is the man who trusts in you.
\c 84
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm for the sons of Korah.
\s1 You Showed Favor to Your Land
\q1 \v 2 O Lord, you have taken pleasure in your land:
\q2 you have turned back the captivity of Jacob.
\q1 \v 3 You have forgiven your people their transgressions;
\q2 you have covered all their sins. Pause.
\q1 \v 4 You have caused all your wrath to cease:
\q2 you have turned from your fierce anger.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Turn us, O God of our salvation,
\q2 and turn your anger away from us.
\q1 \v 6 Would you be angry with us for ever?
\q2 or will you continue your wrath from generation to generation?
\q1 \v 7 O God, you will turn and quicken us;
\q2 and your people shall rejoice in you.
\q1 \v 8 Show us your mercy, O Lord,
\q2 and grant us your salvation.
\b
\q1 \v 9 I will hear what the Lord God will say concerning me:
\q2 for he shall speak peace to his people, and to his saints,
\q2 and to those that turn their heart toward him.
\q1 \v 10 Moreover his salvation is near them who fear him;
\q2 that glory may dwell in our land.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Mercy and truth are met together:
\q2 righteousness and peace have kissed \add each other\add*.
\q1 \v 12 Truth has sprung out of the earth;
\q2 and righteousness has looked down from heaven.
\q1 \v 13 For the Lord will give goodness;
\q2 and our land shall yield her fruit.
\q1 \v 14 Righteousness shall go before him;
\q2 and shall set his steps in the way.
\c 85
\s1 Tried but Trusting
\d \v 1 A Prayer of David.  
\p
\q1 O Lord, incline your ear, and listen to me;
\q2 for I am poor and needy.
\q1 \v 2 Preserve my soul, for I am holy;
\q2 save your servant, O God, who hopes in you.
\q1 \v 3 Pity me, O Lord:
\q2 for to you will I cry all the day.
\q1 \v 4 Rejoice the soul of your servant:
\q2 for to you, O Lord, have I lifted up my soul.
\b
\q1 \v 5 For you, O Lord, art kind, and gentle;
\q2 and plenteous in mercy to all that call upon you.
\q1 \v 6 Give ear to my prayer, O Lord;
\q2 and attend to the voice of my supplication.
\q1 \v 7 In the day of my trouble I cried to you:
\q2 for you did hear me.
\b
\q1 \v 8 There is none like to you, O Lord, among the gods; and there are no \add works\add* like to your works.
\q1 \v 9 All nations whom you have made shall come,
\q2 and shall worship before you, O Lord;
\q2 and shall glorify your name.
\q1 \v 10 For you are great, and do wonders: you are the only \add and\add* the great God.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Guide me, O Lord, in your way,
\q2 and I will walk in your truth:
\q1 let my heart rejoice,
\q2 that I may fear your name.
\q1 \v 12 I will give you thanks, O Lord my God, with all my heart;
\q2 and I will glorify your name for ever.
\q1 \v 13 For your mercy is great toward me;
\q2 and you have delivered my soul from the lowest hell.
\b
\q1 \v 14 O God, transgressors have risen up against me, and an assembly of violent \add men\add* have sought my life;
\q2 and have not set you before them.
\q1 \v 15 But you, O Lord God, art compassionate and merciful,
\q2 long-suffering, and abundant in mercy and true.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Look you upon me, and have mercy upon me:
\q2 give your strength to your servant,
\q2 and save the son of yours handmaid.
\q1 \v 17 Establish with me a token for good;
\q2 and let them who hate me see \add it\add* and be ashamed;
\q2 because you, O Lord, have helped me, and comforted me.
\c 86
\s1 The LORD Loves the Gates of Zion
\d \v 1 A Psalm of a Song for the sons of Korah.  
\p
\q1 His foundations
\q2 are in the holy mountains.
\q1 \v 2 The Lord loves the gates of Zion,
\q2 more than all the tabernacles of Jacob.
\q1 \v 3 Glorious things have been spoken of you,
\q2 O city of God. Pause.
\q1 \v 4 “I will make mention of Raab and Babylon
\q2 to them who know me:
\q1 behold also the Philistines, and Tyre, and the people of the Ethiopians:
\q2 these were born there.”
\b
\q1 \v 5 A man shall say, Zion \add is my\add* mother; and \add such\add* a man was born in her”;
\q2 and the Highest himself has founded her.
\b
\q1 \v 6 The Lord shall recount \add it\add* in the writing of the people,
\q2 and of these princes that \f + \fr 86:6 \fqa Gr. \ft have been in her.\f*were born in her. Pause.
\q1 \v 7 The dwelling of all within you is as \add the dwelling\add* of those that rejoice.”
\c 87
\d \v 1 A song of a Psalm for the sons of Korah for the end, upon Maels for responsive \add strains\add*, of instruction for Heman\f + \fr 87:1 \fqa Gr. \ft understanding.\f* the Israelite.
\s1 I Cry Out before You
\q1 \v 2 O Lord God of my salvation,
\q2 I have cried by day and in the night before you.
\q1 \v 3 Let my prayer come in before you;
\q2 incline your ear to my supplication, O Lord.
\q1 \v 4 For my soul is filled with troubles,
\q2 and my life has drawn near to Hades.
\q1 \v 5 I have been reckoned with them who go down to the pit;
\q2 I became as a man without help;
\b
\q1 \v 6 free among the dead,
\q2 as the slain ones cast out,\f + \fr 87:6 \fqa Alex. \ft om. ἐῤῥιμμένοι, cast out.\f* who sleep in the tomb;
\q1 whom you remember no more;
\q2 and they are rejected from your hand.
\b
\q1 \v 7 They laid me in the lowest pit, in dark \add places\add*, and in the shadow of death.
\q1 \v 8 Your wrath has pressed heavily upon me,
\q2 and you have brought upon me all your billows. Pause.
\q1 \v 9 You have removed my acquaintance far from me;
\q2 they have made me an abomination to themselves;
\q2 I have been delivered up, and have not gone forth.
\q1 \v 10 My eyes are dimmed from poverty;
\q2 but I cried to you, O Lord, all the day;
\q2 I spread forth my hands to you.
\q1 \v 11 Will you work wonders for the dead? or shall physicians\f + \fr 87:11 \ft See \fqa Heb.\f* raise \add them\add* up, that they shall praise you?
\q1 \v 12 Shall anyone declare your mercy in the tomb?
\q2 and your truth in destruction?
\q1 \v 13 Shall your wonders be known in darkness?
\q2 and your righteousness in a forgotten land?
\b
\q1 \v 14 But I cried to you, O Lord;
\q2 and in the morning shall my prayer prevent you.
\q1 \v 15 Wherefore, O Lord, dost you reject my prayer,\f + \fr 87:15 \fqa Heb. \ft and \fqa Alex. \ft soul.\f* \add and\add* turn your face away from me?
\b
\q1 \v 16 I am poor and in troubles from my youth; and having been exalted,
\q2 I was brought low and into despair.\f + \fr 87:16 \ft See \xt 2 Cor. 4. 8.\f*
\q1 \v 17 Your wrath has passed over me;
\q2 and your terrors have greatly disquieted me.
\q1 \v 18 They surrounded me like water;
\q2 all the day they beset me together.
\q1 \v 19 You have put far from me \f + \fr 87:19 \fqa Alex. \ft friend and neighbour.\f*\add every\add* friend,
\q2 and my acquaintances because of \add my\add* wretchedness.
\c 88
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of instruction for Ethan the Israelite.
\s1 I Will Sing of His Love Forever
\q1 \v 2 I will sing of your mercies, O Lord, for ever:
\q2 I will declare your truth with my mouth to all generations.
\q1 \v 3 For you have said, “Mercy shall be built up for ever:
\q2 your truth shall be established in the heavens.”
\b
\q1 \v 4 “I made a covenant with my chosen ones,
\q2 I swore to David my servant.
\q1 \v 5 I will \f + \fr 88:5 \fqa Gr. \ft prepare.\f*establish your seed for ever,
\q2 and build up your throne to all generations.” Pause.
\q1 \v 6 The heavens shall declare your wonders, O Lord;
\q2 and your truth
\q2 in the assembly of the saints.
\q1 \v 7 For who in the \f + \fr 88:7 \fqa Gr. \ft clouds.\f*heavens shall be compared to the Lord?
\q2 and who shall be likened to the Lord among the sons of God?
\q1 \v 8 God is glorified in the council of the saints;
\q2 great and terrible toward all that are round about him.
\b
\q1 \v 9 O Lord God of hosts, who is like to you?
\q2 you are mighty, O Lord, and your truth is round about you.
\q1 \v 10 You rule the power of the sea;
\q2 and you calm the tumult of its waves.
\q1 \v 11 You have brought down the proud as one that is slain;
\q2 and with the arm of your power you have scattered yours enemies.
\q1 \v 12 The heavens are yours, and the earth is yours:
\q2 you have founded the world, and the fulness of it.
\q1 \v 13 You have created the north and the \f + \fr 88:13 \fqa Gr. \ft sea.\f*west:
\q2 Tabor and Hermon shall rejoice in your name.
\b
\q1 \v 14 Yours is the mighty arm: let your hand be strengthened,
\q2 let your right hand be exalted.
\q1 \v 15 Justice and judgment are the establishment of your throne:
\q2 mercy and truth shall go before your face.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Blessed is the people who knows the joyful sound:
\q2 they shall walk, O Lord, in the light of your countenance.
\q1 \v 17 And in your name shall they rejoice all the day:
\q2 and in your righteousness shall they be exalted.
\q1 \v 18 For you are the boast of their strength;
\q2 and in your good pleasure shall our horn be exalted,
\q1 \v 19 for \add our\add* help is of the Lord;
\q2 and of the Holy One of Israel, our king.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Then you spoke in vision to your children,
\q2 and said,
\q1 “I have laid help on a mighty one;
\q2 I have exalted one chosen out of my people.
\q1 \v 21 \x + \xo 88:21 \xt Acts 13. 22.\x* I have found David my servant;
\q2 I have anointed him by \add my\add* holy \f + \fr 88:21 \fqa Heb. \ft and \fqa Alex. \ft oil, see \xt Ps. 91. 10.\f*mercy.
\q1 \v 22 For my hand shall support him;
\q2 and my arm shall strengthen him.
\b
\q1 \v 23 The enemy shall have no advantage against him;
\q2 and the son of transgression shall not hurt him again.
\q1 \v 24 And I will hew down his foes before him,
\q2 and put to flight those that hate him.
\q1 \v 25 But my truth and my mercy shall be with him;
\q2 and in my name shall his horn be exalted.
\q1 \v 26 And I will set his hand in the sea,
\q2 and his right hand in the rivers.
\q1 \v 27 He shall call upon me, \add saying\add*, ‘You are my Father,
\q2 my God, and the helper of my salvation.’
\b
\q1 \v 28 And I will make him \add my\add* first-born,
\q2 higher than the kings of the earth.
\q1 \v 29 I will keep my mercy for him for ever, and my covenant \add shall be\add* firm with him.
\q1 \v 30 And I will establish his seed for ever and ever,
\q2 and his throne as the days of heaven.
\b
\q1 \v 31 If his children should forsake my law,
\q2 and walk not in my judgments;
\q1 \v 32 if they should profane my ordinances,
\q2 and not keep my commandments;
\q1 \v 33 I will visit their transgressions with a rod,
\q2 and their sins with scourges.
\b
\q1 \v 34 But my mercy I will not utterly remove from him,
\q2 nor wrong my truth.
\q1 \v 35 Nor will I by any means profane my covenant;
\q2 and I will not make void the things that proceed out of my lips.
\q1 \v 36 Once have I sworn by my holiness,
\q2 that I will not lie to David.
\q1 \v 37 His seed shall endure for ever,
\q2 and his throne as the sun before me;
\q1 \v 38 and as the moon \add that is\add* established for ever,
\q2 and as the faithful witness in heaven.” Pause.
\q1 \v 39 But you have cast off and set at nothing,
\q2 you have rejected yours anointed.
\q1 \v 40 You have overthrown the covenant of your servant;
\q2 you have profaned his sanctuary, \add casting it\add* to the ground.
\q1 \v 41 You have broken down all his hedges;
\q2 you have made his strong holds a terror.
\q1 \v 42 All that go by the way have spoiled him:
\q2 he is become a reproach to his neighbours.
\b
\q1 \v 43 You have exalted the right hand of his enemies;
\q2 you have made all his enemies to rejoice.
\q1 \v 44 You have turned back the help of his sword,
\q2 and have not helped him in the battle.
\q1 \v 45 You have deprived him of glory:\f + \fr 88:45 \fqa Gr. \ft purification.\f*
\q2 you have broken down his throne to the ground.
\q1 \v 46 You have shortened the days of his throne:\f + \fr 88:46 \fqa Alex. \ft time.\f*
\q2 you have poured shame upon him. Pause.
\q1 \v 47 How long, O Lord, will you turn away,
\q2 for ever?
\q2 shall your anger flame out as fire?
\q1 \v 48 Remember what my being is:
\q2 for have you created all the sons of men in vain?
\q1 \v 49 What man is there who shall live, and not see death? shall \add anyone\add* deliver his soul from the hand of Hades? Pause.
\b
\q1 \v 50 Where are yours ancient mercies, O Lord,
\q2 which you swore to David in your truth?
\q1 \v 51 Remember, O Lord, the reproach of your servants,
\q2 which I have borne in my bosom, \add even the reproach\add* of many nations;
\q1 \v 52 wherewith yours enemies have reviled, O Lord:
\q2 wherewith they have reviled the repayment of yours anointed.
\b
\q1 \v 53 Blessed be the Lord for ever. So be it, so be it.
\c 89
\ms BOOK IV
\s1 From Everlasting to Everlasting
\d \v 1 A Prayer of Moses the man of God.  
\p
\q1 Lord, you have been our refuge
\q2 in all generations.
\q1 \v 2 Before the mountains existed, and \add before\add* the earth and the world were formed,
\q1 even from age to age,
\q2 You are.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Turn not man back to \add his\add* low place,
\q2 whereas you said, “Return, you sons of men?”
\q1 \v 4 For a thousand years in your sight
\q2 are as the yesterday which is past,
\q2 and as a watch in the night.
\q1 \v 5 Years shall be vanity\f + \fr 89:5 \fqa Gr. \ft their vanities or rejections.\f* to them:
\q2 let the morning pass away as grass.
\q1 \v 6 In the morning let it flower, and pass away:
\q2 in the evening let it droop, let it be withered and dried up.
\b
\q1 \v 7 For we have perished in your anger,
\q2 and in your wrath we have been troubled.
\q1 \v 8 You have set our transgressions before you:
\q2 our age is in the light of your countenance.
\q1 \v 9 For all our days are gone, and we have passed away in your wrath:
\q2 our years have spun out\f + \fr 89:9 \fqa Gr. \ft mediated.\f* their tale as a spider.
\q1 \v 10 \add As for\add* the days of our years, in them are seventy years; and if \add men should be\add* in strength, eighty years: and the greater part\f + \fr 89:10 \fqa Or, \ft what is more than these.\f* of them would be labour and trouble;
\q2 for weakness overtakes us, and we shall be chastened.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Who knows the power of your wrath?
\q1 \v 12 and \add who knows how\add* to number \add his days\add* because of the fear of your wrath?
\q2 So manifest your right hand, and those that are instructed in wisdom in the heart.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Return, O Lord, how long?
\q2 and be intreated concerning your servants.
\q1 \v 14 We have been satisfied in the morning with your mercy;
\q2 and we did exult and rejoice:
\q1 \v 15 let us rejoice in all our days, in return for the days wherein you did afflict us,
\q2 the years wherein we saw evil.
\b
\q1 \v 16 And look upon your servants, and upon your works;
\q2 and guide their children.
\q1 \v 17 And let the brightness of the Lord our God be upon us:
\q2 and do you direct\f + \fr 89:17 \fqa Or, \ft prosper.\f* for us the works of our hands.\f + \fr 89:17 \fqa Alex. \ft + yes, prosper you the work of our hands.\f*
\c 90
\s1 You Are My Refuge and My Fortress
\d \v 1 Praise of a Song, by David.  
\p
\q1 He who dwells in the help of the Highest,
\q2 shall stay temporarily under the shelter of the God of heaven.
\q1 \v 2 He shall say to the Lord, “You are my helper and my refuge:
\q2 my God; I will hope in him.”
\b
\q1 \v 3 For he shall deliver you from the snare of the hunters, from \add every\add* troublesome matter.
\q1 \v 4 He shall overshadow you with his shoulders,
\q2 and you shall trust under his wings:
\q2 his truth shall cover you with a shield.
\q1 \v 5 You shall not be afraid of terror by night;
\q2 nor of the arrow flying by day;
\q1 \v 6 \add nor\add* of the \add evil\add* thing that walks\f + \fr 90:6 \ft See ver 3.\f* in darkness; \add nor\add* of calamity, and the evil spirit at noon-day.
\q1 \v 7 A thousand shall fall at your side,
\q2 and ten thousand at your right hand;
\q2 but it shall not come near you.
\q1 \v 8 Only with your eyes shall you observe
\q2 and see the reward of sinners.
\b
\q1 \v 9 For you, O Lord, art my hope:
\q2 you, my soul, have made the Most High your refuge.
\q1 \v 10 No evils shall come upon you,
\q2 and no scourge shall draw near to your dwelling.
\q1 \v 11 For he shall give his angels charge concerning you,
\q2 to keep you in all your ways.
\q1 \v 12 \x + \xo 90:12 \xt Mat. 4. 6.\x* They shall bear you up on their hands,
\q2 lest at any time you dash your foot against a stone.
\q1 \v 13 You shall tread on the asp and basilisk:
\q2 and you shall trample on the lion and dragon.
\b
\q1 \v 14 For he has hoped in me, and I will deliver him:
\q2 I will protect him, because he has known my name.
\q1 \v 15 He shall call upon me,
\q2 and I will listen to him:
\q1 I am with him in affliction; and
\q2 I will deliver him, and glorify him.
\q1 \v 16 I will satisfy him with length of days,
\q2 and show him my salvation.”
\c 91
\d \v 1 A Psalm of a Song for the Sabbath-day.
\s1 How Great Are Your Works!
\q1 \v 2 It is a good thing to give thanks to the Lord,
\q2 and to sing praises to your name, O you Most High;
\q1 \v 3 to proclaim your mercy in the morning,
\q2 and your truth by night,
\q1 \v 4 on a psaltery of ten strings,
\q2 with a song on the harp.
\b
\q1 \v 5 For you, O Lord, have made me glad with your work:
\q2 and in the operations of your hands will I exult.
\q1 \v 6 How have your works been magnified, O Lord!
\q2 your thoughts are very deep.
\q1 \v 7 A foolish man will not know,
\q2 and a senseless man will not understand this.
\q1 \v 8 When the sinners spring up as the grass,
\q2 and all the workers of iniquity have watched;\f + \fr 91:8 \ft Perhaps, 'come to light'.\f* \add it is\add*
\q2 that they may be utterly destroyed for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 9 But you, O Lord, art most high for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 10 For, behold,\f + \fr 91:10 \fqa Alex. \ft + for behold yours enemies, O Lord, as in \fqa Heb.\f* yours enemies shall perish;
\q2 and all the workers of iniquity shall be scattered.
\q1 \v 11 But my horn shall be exalted as \add the horn\add* of a unicorn;
\q2 and my old age with rich mercy.\f + \fr 91:11 \fqa Alex. \ft oil. See \xt Ps. 88. 20.\f*
\q1 \v 12 And my eye has seen my enemies,
\q2 and my ear shall hear the wicked that rise up against me.
\b
\q1 \v 13 The righteous shall flourish as a palm-tree:
\q2 he shall be increased as the cedar in Lebanon.
\q1 \v 14 They who are planted in the house of the Lord
\q2 shall flourish in the courts of our God.
\q1 \v 15 Then shall they be increased in a fine old age;\f + \fr 91:15 \fqa Gr. \ft fat.\f*
\q2 and they shall be prosperous; that they may declare
\q1 \v 16 “that the Lord my God is righteous,
\q2 and there is no iniquity in him.”
\c 92
\s1 The LORD Reigns!
\d \v 1 For the day before the Sabbath,\f + \fr 92:1 \fqa Alex. \ft the sabbath-day.\f* when the land was \add first\add* inhabited, the praise of a Song by David.  
\p
\q1 The Lord reigns; he has clothed himself with honour:
\q2 the Lord has clothed and girded himself with strength;
\q1 for he has established the world,
\q2 which shall not be moved.
\q1 \v 2 Your throne is prepared of old:
\q2 you are from everlasting.
\b
\q1 \v 3 The rivers have lifted up, O Lord,
\q2 the rivers have lifted up their voices,\f + \fr 92:3 \fqa Alex. \ft + the floods will lift up their waves. See \fqa Heb.\f*
\q1 \v 4 at the voices\f + \fr 92:4 \fqa q. d. \ft in answer to.\f* of many waters:
\q2 the billows of the sea are wonderful:
\q2 the Lord is wonderful in high places.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Your testimonies are made very sure:
\q2 holiness becomes yours house, O Lord,
\q2 for ever.\f + \fr 92:5 \fqa Gr. \ft to length of days.\f*
\c 93
\s1 The LORD Will Not Forget His People
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David for the fourth \add day\add* of the week.  
\p
\q1 The Lord is a God of vengeance;
\q2 the God of vengeance has declared himself.\f + \fr 93:1 \fqa Gr. \ft plural, \fqa bis.\f*
\q1 \v 2 Be you exalted, you that judge the earth:
\q2 render a reward to the proud.
\q1 \v 3 How long shall sinners, O Lord,
\q2 how long shall sinners boast?
\b
\q1 \v 4 They will utter and speak unrighteousness;
\q2 all the workers of iniquity will speak \add so\add*.
\q1 \v 5 They have afflicted your people, O Lord,
\q2 and hurt yours heritage.
\q1 \v 6 They have slain the widow and fatherless,
\q2 and murdered the stranger.
\q1 \v 7 And they said, “The Lord shall not see,
\q2 nor shall the God of Jacob understand.”
\b
\q1 \v 8 Understand now, you simple among the people;
\q2 and you fools, at length be wise.
\q1 \v 9 He who planted the ear, does he not hear?
\q2 or He who formed the eye, does not he perceive?
\q1 \v 10 He who chastises the heathen, shall not he punish, \add even\add* He who teaches man knowledge?
\q1 \v 11 \x + \xo 93:11 \xt 1 Cor. 3. 20.\x* The Lord knows the thoughts of men,
\q2 that they are vain.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Blessed is the man whomsoever you shall chasten, O Lord,
\q2 and shall teach him out of your law;
\q1 \v 13 to give him rest from evil days,
\q2 until a pit be digged for the sinful one.
\q1 \v 14 For the Lord will not cast off his people,
\q2 nor will he forsake his inheritance;
\q1 \v 15 until righteousness return to judgment,
\q2 and all the upright in heart shall follow it. Pause.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Who will rise up for me against the transgressors?
\q2 or who will stand up with me against the workers of iniquity?
\q1 \v 17 If the Lord had not helped me,
\q2 my soul had almost sojourned in Hades.
\q1 \v 18 If I said, “My foot has been moved”;
\q2 \v 19 your mercy, O Lord, helped me. O Lord, according to the multitude of my griefs within my heart,
\q2 your consolations have soothed my soul.\f + \fr 93:19 \fqa Lit. \ft 'have loved'. \fqa Alex. \ft ἠὺφπαναν, 'have gladdened'.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 20 Shall the throne of iniquity have fellowship with you,
\q2 which frames mischief by an ordinance?
\q1 \v 21 They will hunt for the soul of the righteous,
\q2 and condemn innocent blood.
\q1 \v 22 But the Lord was my refuge;
\q2 and my God the helper of my hope.
\q1 \v 23 And he will recompense to them their iniquity
\q2 and their wickedness:
\q2 the Lord our God shall utterly destroy them.
\c 94
\d \v 1 The praise of a Song by David.  
\p
\q1 Come, let us exult
\q2 in the Lord;\f + \fr 94:1 \fqa Gr. \ft to.\f* let us make a joyful noise to God our Saviour.
\q1 \v 2 Let us come before his presence with thanksgiving,
\q2 and make a joyful noise to him with psalms.
\b
\q1 \v 3 For the Lord is a great God, and
\q2 a great king over all gods: for the Lord will not cast off his people.\f + \fr 94:3 \fqa Heb. \ft and \fqa Alex. \ft omit this clause. See \xt Ps. 93. 14.\f*
\q1 \v 4 For the ends of the earth are in his hands;
\q2 and the heights of the mountains are his.
\q1 \v 5 For the sea is his, and he made it:
\q2 and his hands formed the dry land.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Come, let us worship and fall down before him;
\q2 and weep before the Lord that made us.
\q1 \v 7 For he is our God;
\q2 and we are the people of his pasture,
\q2 and the sheep of his hand.
\b
\q1 \v 8 \x + \xo 94:8 \xt Heb. 3. 7-12.\x* Today, if you will hear his voice,
\q2 harden not your hearts,
\q1 as in the provocation,
\q2 according to the day of irritation in the wilderness:\f + \fr 94:8 \ft Note, πειρασμοῦ is the reading of several editions.\f*
\q1 \v 9 where your fathers tempted me,
\q2 proved me, and saw my works.
\q1 \v 10 Forty years was I grieved with this generation,
\q2 and said, “They do always err in their heart,
\q2 and they have not known my ways.
\q1 \v 11 So I swore in my wrath,
\q2 “They shall not enter into my rest.”
\c 95
\d \v 1 When the house was built after the Captivity, a Song by David.  
\p
\q1 Sing to the Lord a new song;
\q2 sing to the Lord, all the earth.
\q1 \v 2 Sing to the Lord, bless his name:
\q2 proclaim his salvation from day to day.
\q1 \v 3 Publish his glory among the Gentiles,
\q2 his wonderful works among all people.
\b
\q1 \v 4 For the Lord is great, and greatly to be praised:
\q2 he is terrible above all gods.
\q1 \v 5 For all the gods of the heathen are devils:
\q2 but the Lord made the heavens.
\q1 \v 6 Thanksgiving and beauty are before him:
\q2 holiness and majesty are in his sanctuary.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Bring to the Lord, you families of the Gentiles,
\q2 bring to the Lord glory and honour.
\q1 \v 8 Bring to the Lord the glory \add becoming\add* his name:
\q2 take offerings, and go into his courts.
\q1 \v 9 Worship the Lord in his holy court:
\q2 let all the earth tremble before him.
\b
\q1 \v 10 Say among the heathen, “The Lord reigns”:
\q2 for he has established the world so that it shall not be moved:
\q2 he shall judge the people in righteousness.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Let the heavens rejoice,
\q2 and the earth exult;
\q1 let the sea be moved,
\q2 and the fulness of it.
\q1 \v 12 The plains shall rejoice,
\q2 and all things in them:
\q1 then shall all the trees of the wood
\q2 exult before the presence of the Lord:
\q1 \v 13 for he comes,
\q2 for he comes to judge the earth;
\q1 he shall judge the world in righteousness,
\q2 and the people with his truth.
\c 96
\d \v 1 For David, when his land is established.  
\p
\q1 The Lord reigns, let the earth exult,
\q2 let many islands rejoice.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Cloud and darkness are round about him;
\q2 righteousness and judgment are the establishment of his throne.
\q1 \v 3 Fire shall go before him,
\q2 and burn up his enemies round about.
\q1 \v 4 His lightnings appeared to the world;
\q2 the earth saw, and trembled.
\q1 \v 5 The mountains melted like wax
\q2 at the presence of the Lord,
\q2 at the presence of the Lord of the whole earth.
\q1 \v 6 The heavens have declared his righteousness,
\q2 and all the people have seen his glory.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Let all that worship carved images be ashamed,
\q2 who boast of their idols; \x + \xo 96:7 \xt Heb. 1. 6.\x*
\q2 worship him, all you his angels.
\q1 \v 8 Zion heard and rejoiced;
\q2 and the daughters of Judea exulted,
\q2 because of your judgments, O Lord.
\q1 \v 9 For you are Lord most high over all the earth;
\q2 you are greatly exalted above all gods.
\b
\q1 \v 10 You that love the Lord, hate evil;
\q2 the Lord preserves the souls of his saints;
\q2 he shall deliver them from the hand of sinners.
\q1 \v 11 Light is sprung up for the righteous,
\q2 and gladness for the upright in heart.
\q1 \v 12 Rejoice in the Lord, you righteous;
\q2 and give thanks for a remembrance of his holiness.
\c 97
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 Sing to the Lord a new song;
\q2 for the Lord has worked wonderful works,
\q1 his right hand, and his holy arm,
\q2 have worked salvation for him.
\q1 \v 2 The Lord has made known his salvation,
\q2 he has revealed his righteousness in the sight of the nations.
\q1 \v 3 He has remembered his mercy to Jacob,
\q2 and his truth to the house of Israel;
\q1 all the ends of the earth
\q2 have seen the salvation of our God.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Shout to God, all the earth;
\q2 sing, and exult, and sing psalms.
\q1 \v 5 Sing to the Lord with a harp,
\q2 with a harp, and the voice of a psalm.
\q1 \v 6 With trumpets of metal, and the sound of a trumpet of horn
\q2 make a joyful noise to the Lord before the king.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Let the sea be moved, and the fulness of it;
\q2 the world, and they who dwell in it.
\q1 \v 8 The rivers shall clap their hands together;
\q2 the mountains shall exult.\f + \fr 97:8 \fqa Alex. \ft + before the Lord, for he comes.\f*
\q1 \v 9 For he is come to judge the earth;
\q1 he shall judge the world in righteousness,
\q2 and the nations in uprightness.
\c 98  
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p The Lord reigns;—let the people rage; \add it is he\add* that sits upon the cherubs, let the earth be moved.  
\v 2 The Lord is great in Zion, and is high over all the people.  
\v 3 Let them give thanks to your great name; for it is terrible and holy.  
\v 4 And the king's honour loves judgment; you have prepared \f + \fr 98:4 \fqa Gr. \ft equities.\f*equity, you have worked judgment and justice in Jacob.  
\v 5 Exalt you the Lord our God, and worship \add at\add* his footstool; for he is holy.   
\p
\v 6 Moses and Aaron among his priests, and Samuel among them who call upon his name; they called upon the Lord, and he heard them.  
\v 7 He spoke to them in a pillar of cloud; they kept his testimonies, and the ordinances which he gave them.  
\v 8 O Lord our God, you heard them; O God, you became propitious to them, though you did take vengeance on all their devices.  
\v 9 Exalt you the Lord our God, and worship at his holy mountain; for the Lord our God is holy.   
\c 99
\d \v 1 A Psalm for Thanksgiving.  
\p
\q1 Make a joyful noise to the Lord,
\q2 all the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Serve the Lord with gladness;
\q2 come before his presence with exultation.
\q1 \v 3 Know that the Lord he is God;
\q2 he made us, and not we ourselves; \add we are\add* his people, and the sheep of his pasture.
\q1 \v 4 Enter into his gates with thanksgiving,
\q2 and his courts with hymns;
\q2 give thanks to him, praise his name.
\b
\q1 \v 5 For the Lord is good,
\q2 his mercy is for ever; and his truth \add endures\add* to generation and generation.
\c 100  
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p I will sing to you, O Lord, of mercy and judgment; I will sing a psalm,  
\v 2 and I will be wise in a blameless way. When will you come to me? I walked in the innocence of my heart, in the midst of my house.  
\v 3 I have not set before my eyes any unlawful thing; I have hated transgressors.  
\v 4 A perverse heart has not cleaved to me; I have not known an evil man, forasmuch as he turns away from me.  
\v 5 Him that secretly speaks against his neighbour, him have I driven from \add me\add*: He who is proud in look and insatiable in heart,—with him I have not eaten.  
\v 6 My eyes \add shall be\add* upon the faithful of the land, that they may dwell with me: He who walked in a perfect way, the Shuham ministered to me.  
\v 7 The proud doer dwelt not in the midst of my house; the unjust speaker prospered not in my sight.  
\v 8 Early did I slay all the sinners of the land, that I might destroy out of the city of the Lord all that work iniquity.   
\c 101
\d \v 1 A Prayer for the Poor; when he is deeply afflicted, and pours out his supplication before the Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 2 Hear my prayer, O Lord,
\q2 and let my cry come to you.
\q1 \v 3 Turn not away your face from me: in the day \add when\add* I am afflicted, incline your ear to me: in the day \add when\add* I shall call upon you,
\q2 quickly hear me.
\b
\q1 \v 4 For my days have vanished like smoke,
\q2 and my bones have been parched like a stick.
\q1 \v 5 I am blighted like grass, and my heart is dried up;
\q2 for I have forgotten to eat my bread.
\q1 \v 6 Because of the voice of my groaning,
\q2 my bone has cleaved to my flesh.
\q1 \v 7 I have become like a pelican
\q2 of the wilderness;
\q1 \v 8 I have become like an owl in a ruined house. I have watched, and am become
\q2 as a sparrow dwelling alone on a roof.
\b
\q1 \v 9 All the day long my enemies have reproached me;
\q2 and they who praised me have sworn against me.
\q1 \v 10 For I have eaten ashes as it were bread,
\q2 and mingled my drink with weeping;
\q1 \v 11 because of your anger and your wrath:
\q2 for you have lifted me up, and dashed me down.
\q1 \v 12 My days have declined like a shadow;
\q2 and I am withered like grass.
\b
\q1 \v 13 But you, Lord, endure for ever,
\q2 and your memorial to generation and generation.
\q1 \v 14 You shall arise, and have mercy upon Zion: for \add it is\add* time to have mercy upon her,
\q2 for the set time is come.
\q1 \v 15 For your servants have taken pleasure in her stones,
\q2 and they shall pity her dust.
\b
\q1 \v 16 So the nations shall fear your name, O Lord,
\q2 and all kings your glory.
\q1 \v 17 For the Lord shall build up Zion,
\q2 and shall appear in his glory.\f + \fr 101:17 \fqa Or, \ft then shall be.\f*
\q1 \v 18 He has had regard to the prayer of the lowly,
\q2 and has not despised their petition.
\b
\q1 \v 19 Let this be written for another generation;
\q2 and the people who shall be created shall praise the Lord.
\q1 \v 20 For he has looked out from the height of his sanctuary;
\q2 the Lord looked upon the earth from heaven;
\q1 \v 21 to hear the groaning of the fettered ones,
\q2 to loosen the sons of the slain;
\q1 \v 22 to proclaim the name of the Lord in Zion,
\q2 and his praise in Jerusalem;
\q1 \v 23 when the people are gathered together, and the kings,
\q2 to serve the Lord.
\b
\q1 \v 24 He answered him in the way of his strength:
\q2 tell me the fewness of my days.
\q1 \v 25 “Take me not away in the midst of my days: your years \add are\add* \x + \xo 101:25 \xt Heb. 1. 11,13.\x* through all generations.
\q1 \v 26 In the beginning you, O Lord, did lay\f + \fr 101:26 \fqa Gr. \ft plural.\f* the foundation of the earth;
\q2 and the heavens are the works of yours hands.
\q1 \v 27 They shall perish, but you remain: and \add they all\add* shall wax old as a garment;
\q1 and as a vesture shall you fold them,
\q2 and they shall be changed.
\q1 \v 28 But you are the Shuham,
\q2 and your years shall not fail.
\q1 \v 29 The children of your servants shall dwell \add securely\add*,
\q2 and their seed shall prosper for ever.\f + \fr 101:29 \fqa Or, \ft be directed aright.\f*
\c 102
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David.  
\p
\q1 Bless the Lord, O my soul; and all \add that is\add* within me, \add bless\add* his holy name.
\q1 \v 2 Bless the Lord, O my soul,
\q2 and forget not all his praises:
\q1 \v 3 who forgives all your transgressions,
\q2 who heals all your diseases;
\q1 \v 4 who redeems your life from corruption;
\q2 who crowns you with mercy and compassion;
\q1 \v 5 who satisfies your desire with good things: \add so that\add* your youth shall be renewed like \add that\add* of the eagle.
\b
\q1 \v 6 The Lord executes mercy and judgment
\q2 for all that are injured.
\q1 \v 7 He made known his ways to Moses,
\q2 his will to the children of Israel.
\q1 \v 8 The Lord is compassionate and pitiful,
\q2 long-suffering, and full of mercy.
\q1 \v 9 He will not be always angry;
\q2 nor will he be wrathful for ever.
\q1 \v 10 He has not dealt with us according to our sins,
\q2 nor recompensed us according to our iniquities.
\b
\q1 \v 11 For as the heaven is high above the earth, the Lord has \add so\add* increased his mercy toward them who fear him.
\q1 \v 12 As far as the east is from the west, \add so far\add* has he removed our transgressions from us.
\q1 \v 13 As a father pities \add his\add* children,
\q2 the Lord pities them who fear him.
\q1 \v 14 For he knows our frame:
\q2 remember that we are dust.
\b
\q1 \v 15 \add As for\add* man, his days are as grass;
\q2 as a flower of the field, so shall he flourish.
\q1 \v 16 For the wind passes over it, and it shall not be;
\q2 and it shall know its place no more.
\q1 \v 17 But the mercy of the Lord is from generation to generation
\q2 upon them who fear him,
\q1 and his righteousness to children's children;
\q2 \v 18 to them who keep his covenant,
\q2 and remember his commandments to do them.
\q1 \v 19 The Lord has prepared his throne in the heaven;
\q2 and his kingdom rules over all.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Bless the Lord, all you his angels, mighty in strength,
\q2 who perform his bidding, \add ready\add* to listen to the voice of his words.
\q1 \v 21 Bless the Lord, all you his hosts; \add you\add* ministers of his that do his will.
\q1 \v 22 Bless the Lord, all his works,
\q2 in every place of his dominion:
\q1 bless the Lord, O my soul.
\c 103
\d \v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David.  
\p
\q1 Bless the Lord, O my soul.
\q1 O Lord my God, you are very great;
\q2 you have clothed yourself with praise and honour:
\q1 \v 2 who dost robe yourself with light as with a garment;
\q2 spreading out the heaven as a curtain.
\q1 \v 3 Who covers his chambers
\q2 with waters;
\q1 who makes the clouds his chariot;
\q2 who walks on the wings of the wind.
\q1 \v 4 \x + \xo 103:4 \xt Heb. 1. 7.\x* Who makes his angels spirits,
\q2 and his ministers a flaming fire.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Who establishes the earth on her sure foundation:
\q2 it shall not be moved for ever.
\q1 \v 6 The deep, as it were a garment, is his covering:
\q2 the waters shall stand \f + \fr 103:6 \fqa Or, \ft above.\f*on the hills.
\q1 \v 7 At your rebuke they shall flee;
\q2 at the voice of your thunder they shall be alarmed.
\q1 \v 8 They go up to the mountains, and down to the plains,
\q2 to the place which you have founded for them.
\q1 \v 9 You have set a bound which they shall not pass,
\q2 nor shall they turn again to cover the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 10 He sends forth his fountains among the valleys:
\q2 the waters shall run between the mountains.
\q1 \v 11 They shall give drink to all the wild beasts of the field:
\q2 the wild asses shall take \add of them\add* to \add quench\add* their thirst.
\q1 \v 12 By them shall the birds of the sky lodge:
\q2 they shall utter a voice out of the midst of the rocks.
\q1 \v 13 He waters the mountains from his chambers:
\q2 the earth shall be satisfied with the fruit of your works.
\b
\q1 \v 14 He makes grass to grow for the cattle,
\q2 and green herb for the service of men,
\q2 to bring bread out of the earth;
\q1 \v 15 and wine makes glad the heart of man,
\q2 to make his face cheerful with oil:
\q2 and bread strengthens man's heart.
\q1 \v 16 The trees of the plain shall be full \add of sap; even\add* the cedars of Lebanon which he has planted.
\q1 \v 17 There the sparrows will build their nests;
\q2 and the house of the heron takes the lead among them.
\q1 \v 18 The high mountains are a refuge for the stags, \add and\add* the rock for the rabbits.
\b
\q1 \v 19 He appointed the moon for seasons:
\q2 the sun knows his going down.
\q1 \v 20 You did make darkness, and it was night;
\q2 in it all the wild beasts of the forest will be abroad:
\q1 \v 21 \add even\add* young lions roaring for prey,
\q2 and to seek meat for themselves from God.
\q1 \v 22 The sun arises, and they shall be gathered together,
\q2 and shall lie down in their dens.
\q1 \v 23 Man shall go forth to his work,
\q2 and to his labour till evening.
\b
\q1 \v 24 How great are your works, O Lord!
\q2 in wisdom have you worked them all:
\q2 the earth is filled with your creation.
\q1 \v 25 \add So is\add* this great and wide sea:
\q2 there are things creeping innumerable,
\q2 small animals and great.
\q1 \v 26 There go the ships; \add and\add* this dragon whom you have made to play in it.
\b
\q1 \v 27 All wait upon you, to give them \add their\add* food in due season.
\q1 \v 28 When you have given \add it\add* them, they will gather \add it\add*;
\q1 and when you have opened yours hand,
\q2 they shall all be filled with good.
\q1 \v 29 But when you have turned away your face,
\q2 they shall be troubled:
\q1 you will take away their breath, and they shall fail,
\q2 and return to their dust.
\q1 \v 30 You shall send forth your Spirit,
\q2 and they shall be created;
\q1 and you shall renew
\q2 the face of the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 31 Let the glory of the Lord be for ever:
\q2 the Lord shall rejoice in his works;
\q1 \v 32 who looks upon the earth,
\q2 and makes it tremble;
\q1 who touches the mountains,
\q2 and they smoke.
\q1 \v 33 I will sing to the Lord while I live;
\q2 I will sing praise to my God while I exist.
\q1 \v 34 Let my meditation be sweet to him:
\q2 and I will rejoice in the Lord.
\q1 \v 35 Let the sinners fail from off the earth,
\q2 and transgressors, so that they shall be no more.
\q1 Bless the Lord, O my soul.
\c 104
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p Give thanks to the Lord, and call upon his name;
\q2 declare his works among the heathen.
\q1 \v 2 Sing to him, yes, sing praises to him:
\q2 tell forth all his wonderful works.
\q1 \v 3 Glory in his holy name:
\q2 let the heart of them who seek the Lord rejoice.
\q1 \v 4 Seek you the Lord, and be strengthened;
\q2 seek his face continually.
\q1 \v 5 Remember his wonderful works that he has done;
\q2 his wonders, and the judgments of his mouth;
\q1 \v 6 \add you\add* seed of Abraham, his servants, \add you\add* children of Jacob, his chosen ones.
\b
\q1 \v 7 He is the Lord our God;
\q2 his judgments are in all the earth.
\q1 \v 8 He has remembered his covenant for ever,
\q2 the word which he commanded for a thousand generations:
\q1 \v 9 which he established as a covenant to Abraham, and \add he remembered\add* his oath to Isaac.
\q1 \v 10 And he established it to Jacob for an ordinance,
\q2 and to Israel for an everlasting covenant;
\q1 \v 11 saying, “To you will I give the land of Canaan,
\q2 the line of your inheritance”:
\b
\q1 \v 12 when they were few in number,
\q2 very few, and sojourners in it.
\q1 \v 13 And they went from nation to nation, and from \add one\add* kingdom to another people.
\q1 \v 14 He suffered no man to wrong them;
\q2 and he rebuked kings for their sakes:
\q1 \v 15 \add saying\add*, “Touch not my anointed ones;
\q2 and do my prophets no harm.”
\b
\q1 \v 16 Moreover he called for a famine upon the land;
\q2 he broke the whole support of bread.
\q1 \v 17 He sent a man before them;
\q2 Joseph was sold for a slave.
\q1 \v 18 They hurt his feet with fetters;\f + \fr 104:18 \fqa Gr. \ft humbled.\f*
\q2 his soul passed into iron,\f + \fr 104:18 \fqa Or, \ft his body, see \fqa Heb.\f*
\q1 \v 19 until the time that his cause came on;
\q2 the word of the Lord tried him as fire.
\q1 \v 20 The king sent and loosed him; \add even\add* the prince of the people, and let him go free.
\q1 \v 21 He made him lord over his house,
\q2 and ruler of all his substance;
\q1 \v 22 to chastise his rulers at his pleasure,
\q2 and to teach his elders wisdom.
\b
\q1 \v 23 Israel also came into Egypt,
\q2 and Jacob sojourned in the land of Ham.
\q1 \v 24 And he increased his people greatly,
\q2 and made them stronger than their enemies.
\q1 \v 25 And he turned their heart to hate his people,
\q2 to deal craftily with his servants.
\q1 \v 26 He sent forth Moses his servant, \add and\add* Aaron whom he had chosen.
\q1 \v 27 He established among them his signs, and \add his\add* wonders in the land of Ham.
\q1 \v 28 He sent forth darkness, and made it dark;
\q2 yet they rebelled against his words.\f + \fr 104:28 \fqa Gr. \ft embittered.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 29 He turned their waters into blood,
\q2 and slew their fish.
\q1 \v 30 Their land produced frogs abundantly,
\q2 in the chambers of their kings.
\q1 \v 31 He spoke, and the dog-fly came,
\q2 and lice in all their coasts.
\q1 \v 32 He turned their rain into hail, \add and sent\add* flaming fire in their land.
\q1 \v 33 And he struck their vines and their fig-trees;
\q2 and broke every tree of their coast.
\q1 \v 34 He spoke, and the locust came,
\q2 and caterpillars innumerable,
\q1 \v 35 and devoured all the grass in their land,
\q2 and devoured the fruit of their ground.
\q1 \v 36 He struck also every first-born of their land,
\q2 the first-fruits of all their labour.
\b
\q1 \v 37 And he brought them out with silver and gold;
\q2 and there was not a feeble one among their tribes.
\q1 \v 38 Egypt rejoiced at their departing;
\q2 for the fear of them fell upon them.
\q1 \v 39 He spread out a cloud for a covering to them,
\q2 and fire to give them light by night.
\q1 \v 40 They asked, and the quail came,
\q2 and he satisfied them with the bread of heaven.
\q1 \v 41 He clave the rock, and the waters flowed;
\q2 rivers ran in dry places.
\q1 \v 42 For he remembered his holy word, which \add he promised\add* to Abraham his servant.
\q1 \v 43 And he brought out his people with exultation,
\q2 and his chosen with joy;
\q1 \v 44 and gave them the lands of the heathen;
\q2 and they inherited the labours of the people;
\q1 \v 45 that they might keep his ordinances,
\q2 and diligently seek his law.
\c 105
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 Give thanks to the Lord; for he is good: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.
\q1 \v 2 Who shall tell the mighty acts of the Lord? \add who\add* shall cause all his praises to be heard?
\q1 \v 3 Blessed are they who keep judgment,
\q2 and do righteousness at all times.
\b
\q1 \v 4 Remember us, O Lord, with the favour \add you have\add* to your people:
\q2 visit us with your salvation;
\q1 \v 5 that we may behold the good of yours elect,
\q2 that we may rejoice in the gladness of your nation,
\q2 that we may glory with yours inheritance.
\b
\q1 \v 6 We have sinned with our fathers,
\q2 we have transgressed, we have done unrighteously.
\q1 \v 7 Our fathers in Egypt understood not your wonders,
\q2 and remembered not the multitude of your mercy; but provoked \add him\add* as they went up
\q2 by the Red Sea.
\q1 \v 8 Yet he saved them for his name's sake,
\q2 that he might cause his mighty power to be known.
\q1 \v 9 And he rebuked the Red Sea, and it was dried up:
\q2 so he led them through the deep as through the wilderness.
\q1 \v 10 And he saved them out of the hand of them who hated \add them\add*,
\q2 and redeemed them out of the hand of the enemy.
\q1 \v 11 The water covered those that oppressed them:
\q2 there was not one of them left.
\q1 \v 12 Then they believed his words,
\q2 and celebrated his praise.
\b
\q1 \v 13 They made haste, they forgot his works;
\q2 they waited not for his counsel.
\q1 \v 14 And they lusted exceedingly in the wilderness, and tempted God in the dry \add land\add*.
\q1 \v 15 And he gave them their request,
\q2 and sent fulness into their souls.
\b
\q1 \v 16 They provoked Moses also in the camp,
\q2 and Aaron the holy one of the Lord.
\q1 \v 17 The earth opened and swallowed up Dathan,
\q2 and closed upon the congregation of Abiram.
\q1 \v 18 And a fire was kindled in their congregation,
\q2 and a flame burnt up the sinners.
\b
\q1 \v 19 And they made a calf in Choreb,
\q2 and worshipped the carved image,
\q1 \v 20 and they changed their glory
\q2 into the similitude of a calf that feeds on grass.
\q1 \v 21 They forgot God that saved them,
\q2 who had worked great deeds in Egypt;
\q1 \v 22 wondrous \add works\add* in the land of Ham,
\q2 and terrible things at the Red Sea.
\q1 \v 23 So he said that he would have destroyed them,
\q2 had not Moses his chosen
\q1 stood before him in the breach, to turn \add him\add* away from the fierceness of his anger, so that he should not destroy them.
\b
\q1 \v 24 Moreover they set at nothing the desirable land,
\q2 and believed not his word.
\q1 \v 25 And they murmured in their tents:
\q2 they hearkened not to the voice of the Lord.
\q1 \v 26 So he lifted up his hand against them,
\q2 to cast them down in the wilderness;
\q1 \v 27 and to cast down their seed among the nations,
\q2 and to scatter them in the countries.
\b
\q1 \v 28 They were joined also to Beelphegor,
\q2 and ate the sacrifices of the dead. And they provoked him with their devices;
\q1 \v 29 and destruction was multiplied among them.
\q1 \v 30 Then Phinehas stood up, and made atonement:
\q2 and the plague ceased.
\q1 \v 31 And it was counted to him for righteousness,
\q2 to all generations for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 32 They provoked him also at the water of Strife,
\q2 and Moses was hurt for their sakes;
\q1 \v 33 for they provoked his spirit, and he \f + \fr 105:33 \fqa Or, \ft gave commandment.\f*spoke \add unadvisedly\add* with his lips.
\b
\q1 \v 34 They destroyed not the nations which the Lord told them \add to destroy\add*;
\q1 \v 35 but were mingled with the heathen,
\q2 and learned their works.
\q1 \v 36 And they served their carved images;
\q2 and it became an offence to them.
\q1 \v 37 And they sacrificed their sons
\q2 and their daughters to devils,
\q1 \v 38 and shed innocent blood,
\q2 the blood of their sons and daughters,
\q1 whom they sacrificed to the idols of Canaan;
\q2 and the land was defiled with blood,\f + \fr 105:38 \fqa Or, \ft murderously defiled.\f*
\q1 \v 39 and was polluted with their works;
\q2 and they went a whoring with their own devices.
\b
\q1 \v 40 So the Lord was very angry with his people,
\q2 and he abhorred his inheritance.
\q1 \v 41 And he delivered them into the hands of \add their\add* enemies;
\q2 and they who hated them ruled over them.
\q1 \v 42 And their enemies oppressed them,
\q2 and they were brought down under their hands.
\q1 \v 43 Many a time he delivered them;
\q2 but they provoked him by their counsel,
\q2 and they were brought low by their iniquities.
\b
\q1 \v 44 Yet the Lord looked upon their affliction,
\q2 when he heard their petition.
\q1 \v 45 And he remembered his covenant,
\q2 and repented according to the multitude of his mercy.
\q1 \v 46 And he caused them to be pitied in the sight of all who carried them captive.
\b
\q1 \v 47 Save us, O Lord our God,
\q2 and gather us from among the heathen,
\q1 that we may give thanks to your holy name,
\q2 that we may glory in your praise.
\b
\q1 \v 48 Blessed be the Lord God of Israel
\q2 from everlasting and to everlasting;
\q1 and all the people shall say, “Amen, Amen.”
\c 106
\ms BOOK V
\s1 Thanksgiving for Deliverance
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 Give thanks to the Lord, for he is good; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.
\q1 \v 2 Let them say \add so\add* who have been redeemed by the Lord,
\q2 whom he has redeemed from the hand of the enemy;
\q1 \v 3 and gathered them out of the countries,
\q2 from the east, and west, and north, and south.\f + \fr 106:3 \fqa Gr. \ft sea.\f*
\b
\q1 \v 4 They wandered in the wilderness in a dry land;
\q2 they found no way to a city of habitation.
\q1 \v 5 Hungry and thirsty,
\q2 their soul fainted in them.
\q1 \v 6 Then they cried to the Lord in their affliction,
\q2 and he delivered them out of their distresses.
\q1 \v 7 And he guided them into a straight path,
\q2 that they might go to a city of habitation.
\q1 \v 8 Let them acknowledge to the Lord his mercies,
\q2 and his wonderful works to the children of men.
\q1 \v 9 For he satisfies the empty soul,
\q2 and fills the hungry \add soul\add* with good things,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \add even\add* them who sit in darkness and the shadow of death,
\q2 fettered in poverty and iron;
\q1 \v 11 because they rebelled against the words\f + \fr 106:11 \fqa Gr. \ft embittered the oracles.\f* of God,
\q2 and provoked the counsel of the Most High.
\q1 \v 12 So their heart was brought low with troubles;
\q2 they were weak, and there was no helper.
\q1 \v 13 Then they cried to the Lord in their affliction,
\q2 and he saved them out of their distresses.
\q1 \v 14 And he brought them out of darkness and the shadow of death,
\q2 and broke their bonds apart.
\q1 \v 15 Let them acknowledge to the Lord his mercies,
\q2 and his wonders to the children of men.
\q1 \v 16 For he broke to pieces the brazen gates,
\q2 and crushed the iron bars.
\b
\q1 \v 17 He helped them out\f + \fr 106:17 \ft see the Hebrew.\f* of the way of their iniquity;
\q2 for they were brought low because of their iniquities.
\q1 \v 18 Their soul abhorred all meat;
\q2 and they drew near to the gates of death.
\q1 \v 19 Then they cried to the Lord in their affliction,
\q2 and he saved them out of their distresses.
\q1 \v 20 He sent his word, and healed them,
\q2 and delivered them out of their destructions.
\q1 \v 21 Let them acknowledge to the Lord his mercies,
\q2 and his wonderful works to the children of men.
\q1 \v 22 And let them offer to him the sacrifice of praise,
\q2 and proclaim his works with exultation.
\b
\q1 \v 23 They who go down to the sea in ships,
\q2 doing business in many waters;
\q1 \v 24 these \add men\add* have seen the works of the Lord,
\q2 and his wonders in the deep.
\q1 \v 25 He speaks, and the stormy wind arises,
\q2 and its waves are lifted up.
\q1 \v 26 They go up to the heavens, and go down to the depths;
\q2 their soul melts because of troubles.
\q1 \v 27 They are troubled, they stagger as a drunkard,
\q2 and all their wisdom is swallowed up.
\b
\q1 \v 28 Then they cry to the Lord in their affliction,
\q2 and he brings them out of their distresses.
\q1 \v 29 And he commands the storm, and it is calmed into a gentle breeze,
\q2 and its waves are still.
\q1 \v 30 And they are glad, because they are quiet;
\q2 and he guides them to their desired haven.
\q1 \v 31 Let them acknowledge to the Lord his mercies,
\q2 and his wonderful works to the children of men.
\q1 \v 32 Let them exalt him in the congregation of the people,
\q2 and praise him in the seat of the elders.
\b
\q1 \v 33 He turns rivers into a desert,
\q2 and streams of water into a dry land;\f + \fr 106:33 \fqa Gr. \ft thirst.\f*\f + \fr 106:33 \fqa Gr. \ft waters.\f*
\q1 \v 34 a fruitful land into saltness,
\q2 for the wickedness of them who dwell in it.
\q1 \v 35 He turns a wilderness into pools of water,
\q2 and a dry land into streams of water.
\q1 \v 36 And there he causes the hungry to dwell,
\q2 and they establish for themselves cities of habitation.
\q1 \v 37 And they sow fields, and plant vineyards,
\q2 and they yield fruit of increase.
\q1 \v 38 And he blesses them, and they multiply exceedingly,
\q2 and he diminishes not the number of their cattle.
\b
\q1 \v 39 Again they become few, and are brought low,
\q2 by the pressure of evils and pain.
\q1 \v 40 Contempt is poured upon their princes,
\q2 and he causes them to wander in a desert and trackless land.
\q1 \v 41 But he helps the poor out of poverty, and makes \add him\add* families as a flock.
\q1 \v 42 The upright shall see and rejoice;
\q2 and all iniquity shall stop her mouth.
\b
\q1 \v 43 Who is wise, and will observe these things,
\q2 and understand the mercies of the Lord?
\c 107
\d \v 1 Song of a Psalm by David.
\b
\q1 \v 2 O God, my heart is ready, my heart is ready;
\q2 I will sing and sing psalms with my glory.
\q1 \v 3 Awake, psaltery and harp;
\q2 I will awake early.
\q1 \v 4 I will give thanks to you, O Lord, among the people;
\q2 I will sing praise to you among the Gentiles.
\q1 \v 5 For your mercy is great above the heavens, and your truth \add reaches\add* to the clouds.
\q1 \v 6 Be you exalted, O God, above the heavens;
\q2 and your glory above all the earth.
\q1 \v 7 That your beloved \add ones\add* may be delivered,
\q2 save with your right hand, and hear me.
\q1 God has spoken in his sanctuary;
\q2 \v 8 “I will be exalted,
\q1 and will divide Sicima,
\q2 and will measure out the valley of tents.
\q1 \v 9 Gilead is mine; and Manasseh is mine; and
\q2 Ephraim is the help of my head; Judas is my king;
\q1 \v 10 Moab is the caldron of my hope;
\q2 over Idumea will I cast my sandal;
\q2 the Philistines are made subject to me.”
\b
\q1 \v 11 Who will bring me into the fortified city?
\q2 or who will guide me to Idumea?
\q1 \v 12 Will not you, O God, who have rejected us?
\q2 and will not you, O God, go forth with our hosts?
\q1 \v 13 Give us help from tribulation:
\q2 for vain is the help of man.
\q1 \v 14 Through God we shall do valiantly;\f + \fr 107:14 \fqa Gr. \ft work power.\f*
\q2 and he will bring to nothing our enemies.
\c 108
\d \v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.  
\p
\q1 O God, pass not over
\q2 my praise in silence;
\q1 \v 2 for the mouth of the sinner and the mouth of the crafty \add man\add* have been opened against me:
\q2 they have spoken against me with a crafty tongue.
\q1 \v 3 And they have surrounded me with words of hatred;
\q2 and fought against me without a cause.
\q1 \v 4 Instead of loving me,\f + \fr 108:4 \fqa Or, \ft in return for my love.\f* they falsely accused me:
\q2 but I continued to pray.
\q1 \v 5 And they rewarded me evil for good,
\q2 and hatred for my love.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Set you a sinner against him;
\q2 and let the devil stand\f + \fr 108:6 \fqa Or, \ft the accuser.\f* at his right hand.
\q1 \v 7 When he is judged, let him go forth condemned:
\q2 and let his prayer become sin.
\q1 \v 8 Let his days be few: and \x + \xo 108:8 \xt Acts 1. 20.\x*
\q2 let another take his office of overseer.
\q1 \v 9 Let his children be orphans,
\q2 and his wife a widow.
\q1 \v 10 Let his children wander without a dwelling-place, and beg:
\q2 let them be cast out of their habitations.
\q1 \v 11 Let \add his\add* creditor exact all that belongs to him:
\q2 and let strangers spoil his labours.
\q1 \v 12 Let him have no helper;
\q2 neither let there be anyone to have compassion on his fatherless children.
\q1 \v 13 Let his children be \add given up\add* to utter destruction:
\q2 in one generation let his name be blotted out.
\q1 \v 14 Let the iniquity of his fathers be remembered before the Lord;
\q2 and let not the sin of his mother be blotted out.
\q1 \v 15 Let them be before the Lord continually;
\q2 and let their memorial be blotted out from the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Because he remembered not to show mercy, but persecuted the needy and poor man, and \add that\add* to slay him that was pricked in the heart.
\q1 \v 17 He loved cursing also, and it shall come upon him;
\q2 and he took not pleasure in blessing, so it shall be removed far from him.
\q1 \v 18 Yes, he put on cursing as a garment,
\q2 and it is come as water into his bowels,
\q2 and as oil into his bones.
\q1 \v 19 Let it be to him as a garment which he puts on,
\q2 and as a belt with which he girds himself continually.
\q1 \v 20 This is the dealing of the Lord with those who falsely accuse me,
\q2 and of them who speak evil against my soul.
\b
\q1 \v 21 But you, O Lord, Lord, deal \add mercifully\add* with me,
\q2 for your name's sake:
\q2 for your mercy is good.
\q1 \v 22 Deliver me, for I am poor and needy;
\q2 and my heart is troubled within me.
\q1 \v 23 I am removed as a shadow in its going down:
\q2 I am tossed up and down like locusts.
\q1 \v 24 My knees are weakened through fasting,
\q2 and my flesh is changed because of \add the want of\add* oil.
\q1 \v 25 I became also a reproach to them: \add when\add* they saw me they shook their heads.
\b
\q1 \v 26 Help me, O Lord my God;
\q2 and save me according to your mercy.
\q1 \v 27 And let them know that this is your hand; and \add that\add* you, Lord, have worked it.
\q1 \v 28 Let them curse, but you shall bless:
\q2 let them who rise up against me be ashamed,
\q2 but let your servant rejoice.
\q1 \v 29 Let those that falsely accuse me be clothed with shame,
\q2 and let them cover themselves with their shame as with a mantle.
\q1 \v 30 I will give thanks to the Lord abundantly with my mouth;
\q2 and in the midst of many I will praise him.
\q1 \v 31 For he stood on the right hand of the poor, to save \add me\add* from them who persecute my soul.
\c 109
\d \v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p \x + \xo 109:1 \xt Mat. 22. 44.\x*
\q1 The Lord said to my Lord,
\q2 “Sit you on my right hand,
\q1 until I make yours enemies
\q2 your footstool.”
\b
\q1 \v 2 The Lord shall send out a rod of power for you out of Zion:
\q2 “rule you in the midst of yours enemies.”
\q1 \v 3 With you is dominion in the day of your power,
\q2 in the splendours of your saints:\f + \fr 109:3 \fqa Or, \ft holiness, \fqa i. e. \ft holy things.\f*
\q2 I have begotten you from the womb before the morning.
\b
\q1 \v 4 The Lord swore,
\q2 and will not repent,
\q1 “You are a priest for ever,
\q2 after the order of Melchizedek.”
\b
\q1 \v 5 The Lord at your right hand
\q2 has dashed in pieces kings in the day of his wrath.
\q1 \v 6 He shall judge among the nations, he shall fill up \add the number of\add* corpses,
\q2 he shall crush the heads of many on the earth.
\q1 \v 7 He shall drink of the brook in the way;
\q2 therefore shall he lift up the head.
\c 110
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 I will give you thanks, O Lord, with my whole heart,
\q2 in the council of the upright, and \add in\add* the congregation.
\b
\q1 \v 2 The works of the Lord are great,
\q2 sought out according to all his will.\f + \fr 110:2 \ft Comp. \fqa Gr. \ft with \fqa Heb.\f*
\q1 \v 3 His work is \add worthy of\add* thanksgiving and honour:
\q2 and his righteousness endures for ever and ever.
\q1 \v 4 He has caused his wonderful works to be remembered:
\q2 the Lord is merciful and compassionate.
\q1 \v 5 He has given food to them who fear him:
\q2 he will remember his covenant for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 6 He has declared to his people the power of his works,
\q2 to give them the inheritance of the heathen.
\q1 \v 7 The works of his hands are truth and judgment:
\q2 all his commandments are sure:
\q1 \v 8 established for ever and ever,
\q2 done in truth and uprightness.
\q1 \v 9 He sent redemption to his people:
\q2 he commanded his covenant for ever:
\q2 holy and fearful is his name.
\b
\q1 \v 10 The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom,\f + \fr 110:10 \fqa Or, \ft sum.\f*
\q2 and all that act accordingly have a good understanding;
\q1 his praise endures for ever and ever.
\c 111
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 Blessed is the man who fears the Lord:
\q2 he will delight greatly in his commandments.
\q1 \v 2 His seed shall be mighty in the earth:
\q2 the generation of the upright shall be blessed.
\q1 \v 3 Glory and riches shall be in his house;
\q2 and his righteousness endures for evermore.
\q1 \v 4 To the upright light has sprung up in darkness:
\q2 he is pitiful, and merciful, and righteous.
\b
\q1 \v 5 The good man is He who pities and lends:
\q2 he will direct his affairs with judgment.
\q1 \v 6 For he shall not be moved for ever;
\q2 the righteous shall be in everlasting remembrance.
\q1 \v 7 He shall not be afraid of \add any\add* evil report:
\q2 his heart is ready to trust in the Lord.
\q1 \v 8 His heart is established, he shall not fear, till he shall see \add his desire\add* upon his enemies.
\q1 \v 9 \x + \xo 111:9 \xt 2 Cor. 9. 9.\x* He has dispersed abroad; he has given to the poor;
\q2 his righteousness endures for evermore:
\q2 his horn shall be exalted with honour.
\b
\q1 \v 10 The sinner shall see and be angry,
\q2 he shall gnash his teeth, and consume away:
\q2 the desire of the sinner shall perish.
\c 112
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 Praise the Lord, you servants \add of his\add*,
\q2 praise the name of the Lord.
\q1 \v 2 Let the name of the Lord be blessed, from this present time and for ever.
\q1 \v 3 From the rising of the sun to his setting,
\q2 the name of the Lord is to be praised.
\b
\q1 \v 4 The Lord is high above all the nations;
\q2 his glory is above the heavens.
\q1 \v 5 Who is as the Lord our God?
\q2 who dwells in the high places,
\q1 \v 6 and \add yet\add* looks upon the low things in heaven,
\q2 and on the earth:
\b
\q1 \v 7 who lifts up the poor from the earth,
\q2 and raises up the needy from the dunghill;
\q1 \v 8 to set him with princes, \add even\add* with the princes of his people:
\q1 \v 9 who settles the barren \add woman\add* in a house, \add as\add* a mother rejoicing over children.
\c 113
\s1 A Psalm of Exodus
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p At the going forth of Israel from Egypt,
\q2 of the house of Jacob from a barbarous people,
\q1 \v 2 Judea became his sanctuary,\f + \fr 113:2 \fqa Or, \ft consecrated thing.\f* \add and\add* Israel his dominion.
\b
\q1 \v 3 The sea saw and fled:
\q2 Jordan was turned back.
\q1 \v 4 The mountains skipped like rams,
\q2 and the hills like lambs.
\q1 \v 5 What \add ailed\add* you, O sea, that you fled?
\q2 and you Jordan, that you wast turned back?
\q1 \v 6 \add You\add* mountains, that you skipped like rams, and \add you\add* hills, like lambs?
\b
\q1 \v 7 The earth trembled at the presence of the Lord,
\q2 at the presence of the God of Jacob;
\q1 \v 8 who turned the rock into pools of water, and the flint into fountains\f + \fr 113:8 \fqa Gr. \ft sharp rock.\f* of water.
\s1 To Your Name Be the Glory
\q1 \v 9 Not to us, O Lord, not to us,
\q2 but to your name give glory, because of your mercy
\q2 and your truth;
\b
\q1 \v 10 lest at any time the nations should say,
\q2 “Where is their God?”
\b
\q1 \v 11 But our God has done in heaven and on earth,
\q2 whatever he has pleased.
\b
\q1 \v 12 The idols of the nations are silver and gold,
\q2 the works of men's hands.
\q1 \v 13 They have a mouth, but they cannot speak;\f + \fr 113:13 \fqa Gr. \ft will not.\f*
\q2 they have eyes, but they cannot see:
\q1 \v 14 they have ears, but they cannot hear;
\q2 they have noses, but they cannot smell;
\q1 \v 15 they have hands, but they cannot handle;
\q2 they have feet, but they cannot walk:
\q2 they cannot speak through their throat.
\q1 \v 16 Let those that make them become like to them,
\q2 and all who trust in them.
\b
\q1 \v 17 The house of Israel trusts in the Lord:
\q2 he is their helper and defender.
\q1 \v 18 The house of Aaron trusts in the Lord:
\q2 he is their helper and defender.
\q1 \v 19 They who fear the Lord trust in the Lord:
\q2 he is their helper and defender.
\q1 \v 20 The Lord has remembered us, and blessed us:
\q2 he has blessed the house of Israel,
\q2 he has blessed the house of Aaron.
\q1 \v 21 He has blessed them who fear the Lord,
\q2 both small and great.
\b
\q1 \v 22 The Lord \f + \fr 113:22 \fqa Or, \ft increase you.\f*add \add blessings\add* to you
\q2 and to your children.
\q1 \v 23 Blessed are you of the Lord,
\q2 who made the heaven and the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 24 The heaven of \f + \fr 113:24 \fqa Gr. \ft sing.\f*heavens \add belongs\add* to the Lord:
\q2 but he has given the earth to the sons of men.
\q1 \v 25 The dead shall not praise you, O Lord,
\q2 nor any that go down to Hades.
\q1 \v 26 But we, the living, will bless the Lord,
\q2 from henceforth and for ever.
\c 114
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 I am well pleased, because the Lord will listen
\q2 to the voice of my supplication.
\q1 \v 2 Because he has inclined his ear to me,
\q2 therefore will I call upon him while I live.
\b
\q1 \v 3 The pangs of death surrounded me;
\q2 the dangers of hell found me:
\q2 I found affliction and sorrow.
\q1 \v 4 Then I called on the name of the Lord:
\q2 “O Lord, deliver my soul.”
\b
\q1 \v 5 The Lord is merciful and righteous;
\q2 yes, our God has pity.
\q1 \v 6 The Lord preserves the simple:\f + \fr 114:6 \fqa Gr. \ft infants.\f*
\q2 I was brought low, and he delivered me.
\q1 \v 7 Return to your rest, O my soul;
\q2 for the Lord has dealt bountifully with you.
\q1 \v 8 For he has delivered my soul from death,
\q2 my eyes from tears, and my feet from falling.
\b
\q1 \v 9 I shall be well-pleasing before the Lord
\q2 in the land of the living.
\c 115
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p \x + \xo 115:1 \xt 2 Cor. 4. 13.\x*
\q1 I believed, wherefore I have spoken:
\q2 but I was greatly afflicted.”
\q1 \v 2 And I said in my amazement,
\q2 “Every man is a liar.”
\b
\q1 \v 3 What shall I render to the Lord for all the things
\q2 wherein he has rewarded me?
\q1 \v 4 I will take the cup of salvation,
\q2 and call upon the name of the Lord.
\v 4a I will pay my vows to the Lord, in the presence of all his people.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Precious in the sight of the Lord
\q2 is the death of his saints.
\q1 \v 7 O Lord, I am your servant;
\q2 I am your servant, and the son of yours handmaid:
\q2 you have burst my bonds apart.
\b
\q1 \v 8 I will offer to you the sacrifice of praise,
\q2 and will call upon the name of the Lord.
\q1 \v 9 I will pay my vows to the Lord,
\q2 in the presence of all his people,
\q1 \v 10 in the courts of the Lord's house,
\q2 in the midst of you, Jerusalem.
\c 116
\s1 Extol Him, All You Peoples
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p \x + \xo 116:1 \xt Rom. 15. 11.\x*
\q1 Praise the Lord, all you nations:
\q2 praise him, all you peoples.
\q1 \v 2 For his mercy has been abundant toward us:
\q2 and the truth of the Lord endures for ever.
\c 117
\s1 The LORD Is on My Side
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 Give thanks to the Lord; for \add he is\add* good: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.
\q1 \v 2 Let now the house of Israel say, that \add he is\add* good: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.
\q1 \v 3 Let now the house of Aaron say, that \add he is\add* good: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.
\q1 \v 4 Let now all that fear the Lord say, that \add he is\add* good: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.
\b
\q1 \v 5 I called on the Lord out of affliction: and he hearkened to me, \add so as to bring me\add*
\q2 into a wide place.
\q1 \v 6 \x + \xo 117:6 \xt Heb. 13. 6.\x* The Lord is my helper; and I will not fear
\q2 what man shall do to me.
\q1 \v 7 The Lord is my helper; and I shall see \add my desire\add*
\q2 upon my enemies.
\q1 \v 8 \add It is\add* better to trust in the Lord
\q2 than to trust in man.
\q1 \v 9 \add It is\add* better to hope in the Lord,
\q2 than to hope in princes.
\b
\q1 \v 10 All nations surrounded me about:
\q2 but in the name of the Lord I repulsed them.
\q1 \v 11 They completely surrounded me about:
\q2 but in the name of the Lord I repulsed them.
\q1 \v 12 They surrounded me about as bees \add do\add* a honeycomb,
\q2 and they burst into flame as fire among thorns:
\q2 but in the name of the Lord I repulsed them.
\q1 \v 13 I was thrust, and sorely shaken, that I might fall:
\q2 but the Lord helped me.
\q1 \v 14 The Lord is my strength and my song,
\q2 and is become my salvation.
\b
\q1 \v 15 The voice of exultation and salvation is in the tabernacles of the righteous:
\q2 the right hand of the Lord has worked mightily.
\q1 \v 16 The right hand of the Lord has exalted me:
\q2 the right hand of the Lord has worked powerfully.”
\q1 \v 17 I shall not die, but live,
\q2 and recount the works of the Lord.
\q1 \v 18 The Lord has chastened me sore:
\q2 but he has not given me up to death.
\b
\q1 \v 19 Open to me the gates of righteousness:
\q2 I will go into them, and give praise to the Lord.
\q1 \v 20 This is the gate of the Lord:
\q2 the righteous shall enter by it.
\q1 \v 21 I will give thanks to you; because you have heard me,
\q2 and art become my salvation.
\q1 \v 22 \x + \xo 117:22 \xt Mat. 21. 42.\x* The stone which the builders rejected,
\q2 the Shuham is become the head of the corner.
\q1 \v 23 This has been done of the Lord;
\q2 and it is wonderful in our eyes.
\q1 \v 24 This is the day which the Lord has made:
\q2 let us exult and rejoice in it.
\b
\q1 \v 25 O Lord, save now:
\q2 O Lord, send now prosperity.
\b
\q1 \v 26 \x + \xo 117:26 \xt Mat. 21. 9.\x* Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord:
\q2 we have blessed you out of the house of the Lord.
\q1 \v 27 God is the Lord, and he has shined upon us:
\q2 celebrate the feast with \f + \fr 117:27 \ft Possibly, the multitude, \fqa q. d. \ft cœtu frequenti.\f*thick \add branches, binding the victims\add*
\q1 even to the horns of the altar.
\q1 \v 28 You are my God, and I will give you thanks:
\q2 you are my God, and I will exalt you. I will give thanks to you, for you have heard me, and art become my salvation.
\q1 \v 29 Give thanks to the Lord; for he is good: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.
\c 118
\s1 Your Word Is a Lamp to My Feet
\qa א
\qa ALEPH
\d \v 1 Alleluia.  
\p
\q1 Blessed are the blameless in the way,
\q2 who walk in the law of the Lord.
\q1 \v 2 Blessed are they who search out his testimonies:
\q2 they will diligently seek him with the whole heart.
\q1 \v 3 For they who work iniquity
\q2 have not walked in his ways.
\q1 \v 4 You have commanded \add us\add*
\q2 diligently to keep your precepts.
\q1 \v 5 O that my ways were directed
\q2 to keep yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 6 Then shall I not be ashamed,
\q2 when I have respect to all your commandments.
\q1 \v 7 I will give you thanks with uprightness of heart,
\q2 when I have learnt the judgments of your righteousness.
\q1 \v 8 I will keep yours ordinances:
\q2 O forsake me not greatly.
\qa ב
\qa BETH
\b
\q1 \v 9 Wherewith shall a young man direct his way?
\q2 by keeping your words.
\q1 \v 10 With my whole heart have I diligently sought you:
\q2 cast me not away from your commandments.
\q1 \v 11 I have hidden yours oracles in my heart,
\q2 that I might not sin against you.
\q1 \v 12 Blessed are you, O Lord:
\q2 teach me yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 13 With my lips have I declared
\q2 all the judgments of your mouth.
\q1 \v 14 I have delighted in the way of your testimonies, \add as much\add* as in all riches.
\q1 \v 15 I will meditate on your commandments,
\q2 and consider your ways.
\q1 \v 16 I will meditate on yours ordinances:
\q2 I will not forget your words.
\qa ג
\qa GIMEL
\b
\q1 \v 17 Render a repayment to your servant: \add so\add* shall I live, and keep your words.
\q1 \v 18 Unveil you my eyes,
\q2 and I shall perceive wondrous things out of your law.
\q1 \v 19 I am a stranger in the earth:
\q2 hide not your commandments from me.
\q1 \v 20 My soul has longed exceedingly
\q2 for your judgments at all times.
\q1 \v 21 You have rebuked the proud:
\q2 cursed are they who turn aside from your commandments.
\q1 \v 22 Remove from me reproach and contempt;
\q2 for I have sought out your testimonies.
\q1 \v 23 For princes sat and spoke against me:
\q2 but your servant was meditating on yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 24 For your testimonies are my meditation,
\q2 and yours ordinances are my counsellors.
\qa ד
\qa DALETH
\b
\q1 \v 25 My soul has cleaved to the ground;
\q2 quicken you me according to your word.
\q1 \v 26 I declared my ways, and you did hear me;
\q2 teach me yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 27 Instruct me in the way of yours ordinances;
\q2 and I will meditate on your wondrous works.
\q1 \v 28 My soul has slumbered for sorrow;
\q2 strengthen you me with your words.
\q1 \v 29 Remove from me the way of iniquity;
\q2 and be merciful to me by your law.\f + \fr 118:29 \fqa q. d. \ft in teaching me your law.\f*
\q1 \v 30 I have chosen the way of truth;
\q2 and have not forgotten your judgments.
\q1 \v 31 I have cleaved to your testimonies, O Lord;
\q2 put me not to shame.
\q1 \v 32 I ran the way of your commandments,
\q2 when you did enlarge my heart.
\qa ה
\qa HE
\b
\q1 \v 33 \f + \fr 118:33 \fqa sc. \ft as a lawgiver.\f*Teach me, O Lord, the way of yours ordinances,
\q2 and I will seek it out continually.
\q1 \v 34 Instruct me, and I will search out your law,
\q2 and will keep it with my whole heart.
\q1 \v 35 Guide me in the path of your commandments;
\q2 for I have delighted in it.
\q1 \v 36 Incline my heart to your testimonies,
\q2 and not to covetousness.
\q1 \v 37 Turn away my eyes that I may not behold vanity:
\q2 quicken you me in your way.
\q1 \v 38 Confirm yours oracle to your servant,
\q2 that he may fear you.
\q1 \v 39 Take away my reproach which I have feared:
\q2 for your judgments are good.
\q1 \v 40 Behold, I have desired your commandments:
\q2 quicken me in your righteousness.
\qa ו
\qa WAW
\b
\q1 \v 41 And let your mercy come upon me, O Lord; \add even\add* your salvation, according to your word.
\q1 \v 42 And \add so\add* I shall render an answer to them who reproach me:
\q2 for I have trusted in your words.
\q1 \v 43 And take not the word of truth utterly out of my mouth;
\q2 for I have hoped in your judgments.
\q1 \v 44 So shall I keep your law continually,
\q2 for ever and ever.
\q1 \v 45 I walked also at large:
\q2 for I sought out your commandments.
\q1 \v 46 And I spoke of your testimonies before kings,
\q2 and was not ashamed.
\q1 \v 47 And I meditated on your commandments,\f + \fr 118:47 \fqa Or, \ft exercised myself in.\f*
\q2 which I loved exceedingly.
\q1 \v 48 And I lifted up my hands to your commandments which I loved;
\q2 and I meditated in yours ordinances.
\qa ז
\qa ZAYIN
\b
\q1 \v 49 Remember your words to your servant,
\q2 wherein you have made me hope.
\q1 \v 50 This has comforted me in my affliction:
\q2 for yours oracle has quickened me.
\q1 \v 51 The proud have transgressed exceedingly;
\q2 but I swerved not from your law.
\q1 \v 52 I remembered your judgments of old, O Lord;
\q2 and was comforted.
\q1 \v 53 Despair took hold upon me,
\q2 because of the sinners who forsake your law.
\q1 \v 54 Yours ordinances were my songs
\q2 in the place of my sojourning.
\q1 \v 55 I remembered your name, O Lord, in the night,
\q2 and kept your law.
\q1 \v 56 This I had,
\q2 because I diligently sought yours ordinances.
\qa ח
\qa HETH
\q1 \v 57 You are my portion, O Lord:
\q2 I said that I would keep your law.
\q1 \v 58 I begged your favour with my whole heart:\f + \fr 118:58 \fqa Gr. \ft presence, \fqa or, \ft countenance.\f*
\q2 have mercy upon me according to your word.
\q1 \v 59 I thought on your ways,
\q2 and turned my feet to your testimonies.
\q1 \v 60 I prepared myself, (and was not terrified,)
\q2 to keep your commandments.
\q1 \v 61 The snares of sinners entangled me:
\q2 but I forgot not your law.
\q1 \v 62 At midnight I arose, to give thanks to you
\q2 for the judgments of your righteousness.
\q1 \v 63 I am a companion of all those who fear you,
\q2 and of them who keep your commandments.
\q1 \v 64 O Lord, the earth is full of your mercy:
\q2 teach me yours ordinances.
\qa ט
\qa TETH
\b
\q1 \v 65 You have worked kindly with your servant, O Lord,
\q2 according to your word.
\q1 \v 66 Teach me kindness, and instruction, and knowledge:
\q2 for I have believed your commandments.
\q1 \v 67 Before I was afflicted, I transgressed;
\q2 therefore have I kept your word.
\q1 \v 68 Good are you, O Lord;
\q2 therefore in your goodness teach me yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 69 The injustice of the proud has been multiplied against me:
\q2 but I will search out your commandments with all my heart.
\q1 \v 70 Their heart has been curdled like milk;
\q2 but I have meditated on your law.
\q1 \v 71 \add It is\add* good for me that you have afflicted me;
\q2 that I might learn yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 72 The law of your mouth is better to me
\q2 than thousands of gold and silver.
\qa י
\qa YODH
\b
\q1 \v 73 Your hands have made me, and fashioned me:
\q2 instruct me, that I may learn your commandments.
\q1 \v 74 They who fear you will see me and rejoice:
\q2 for I have hoped in your words.
\q1 \v 75 I know, O Lord, that your judgments are righteousness, and \add that\add* you in truthfulness have afflicted me.
\q1 \v 76 Let, I pray you, your mercy be to comfort me,
\q2 according to your word to your servant.
\q1 \v 77 Let your compassions come to me, that I may live:
\q2 for your law is my meditation.
\q1 \v 78 Let the proud be ashamed; for they transgressed against me unjustly:
\q2 but I will meditate in your commandments.
\q1 \v 79 Let those that fear you,
\q2 and those that know your testimonies, turn to me.
\q1 \v 80 Let my heart be blameless in yours ordinances,
\q2 that I may not be ashamed.
\qa כ
\qa KAPH
\q1 \v 81 My soul faints for your salvation:
\q2 I have hoped in your words.
\q1 \v 82 My eyes failed \add in waiting\add* for your word, saying,
\q2 “When will you comfort me?”
\q1 \v 83 For I am become as a bottle in the frost: \add yet\add* I have not forgotten yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 84 How many are the days of your servant?
\q2 when will you execute judgment for me on them who persecute me?
\q1 \v 85 Transgressors told me \add idle tales\add*;
\q2 but not according to your law, O Lord.
\q1 \v 86 All your commandments are truth;
\q2 they persecuted me unjustly; help you me.
\q1 \v 87 They nearly made an end of me in the earth;
\q2 but I forsook not your commandments.
\q1 \v 88 Quicken me according to your mercy;
\q2 so shall I keep the testimonies of your mouth.
\qa ל
\qa LAMEDH
\q1 \v 89 Your word, O Lord,
\q2 abides in heaven for ever.
\q1 \v 90 Your truth \add endures\add* to all generations;
\q2 you have founded the earth, and it abides.
\q1 \v 91 The day continues by your arrangement;
\q2 for all things are your servants.
\q1 \v 92 Were it not that your law is my meditation,
\q2 then I should have perished in my affliction.
\q1 \v 93 I will never forget yours ordinances;
\q2 for with them you have quickened me.
\q1 \v 94 I am yours, save me; for I have sought out yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 95 Sinners laid wait for me to destroy me; \add but\add* I understood your testimonies.
\q1 \v 96 I have seen an end of all perfection; \add but\add* your commandment is very broad.
\qa מ
\qa MEM
\b
\q1 \v 97 How I have loved your law, O Lord!
\q2 it is my meditation all the day.
\q1 \v 98 You have made me wiser than my enemies \add in\add* your commandment;
\q2 for it is mine for ever.
\q1 \v 99 I have more understanding than all my teachers;
\q2 for your testimonies are my meditation.
\q1 \v 100 I understand more than the aged;
\q2 because I have sought out your commandments.
\q1 \v 101 I have kept back my feet from every evil way,
\q2 that I might keep your words.
\q1 \v 102 I have not declined from your judgments;
\q2 for you have instructed me.\f + \fr 118:102 \ft That is, as a lawgiver.\f*
\q1 \v 103 How sweet are yours oracles to my throat!
\q2 more so than honey to my mouth!
\q1 \v 104 I gain understanding by your commandments:
\q2 therefore I have hated every way of unrighteousness.
\qa נ
\qa NUN
\b
\q1 \v 105 Your law is a lamp to my feet,
\q2 and a light to my paths.
\q1 \v 106 I have sworn and determined
\q2 to keep the judgments of your righteousness.
\q1 \v 107 I have been very greatly afflicted, O Lord:
\q2 quicken me, according to your word.
\q1 \v 108 Accept, I pray you, O Lord, the freewill-offerings of my mouth,
\q2 and teach me your judgments.
\q1 \v 109 My soul is continually in yours hands;\f + \fr 118:109 \fqa Heb. \ft and \fqa Alex. \ft my.\f*
\q2 and I have not forgotten your law.
\q1 \v 110 Sinners spread a snare for me;
\q2 but I erred not from your commandments.
\q1 \v 111 I have inherited your testimonies for ever;
\q2 for they are the joy of my heart.
\q1 \v 112 I have inclined my heart to perform yours ordinances for ever, \f + \fr 118:112 \ft See \fqa Heb.\f*in return \add for your mercies\add*.
\qa ס
\qa SAMEKH
\b
\q1 \v 113 I have hated transgressors;
\q2 but I have loved your law.
\q1 \v 114 You are my helper and my supporter;
\q2 I have hoped in your words.
\q1 \v 115 Depart from me, you evil-doers;
\q2 for I will search out the commandments of my God.
\q1 \v 116 Uphold me according to your word, and quicken me;
\q2 and make me not ashamed of my expectation.
\q1 \v 117 Help me, and I shall be saved;
\q2 and I will meditate in yours ordinances continually.
\q1 \v 118 You have brought to nothing all that depart from yours ordinances;
\q2 for their inward thought is unrighteous.
\q1 \v 119 I have reckoned all the sinners of the earth as transgressors;
\q2 therefore have I loved your testimonies.
\q1 \v 120 Penetrate my flesh with your fear;
\q2 for I am afraid of your judgments.
\qa ע
\qa AYIN
\b
\q1 \v 121 I have done judgment and justice;
\q2 deliver me not up to them who injure me.
\q1 \v 122 Receive your servant for good:
\q2 let not the proud accuse me falsely.
\q1 \v 123 My eyes have failed for your salvation,
\q2 and for the word of your righteousness.
\q1 \v 124 Deal with your servant according to your mercy,
\q2 and teach me yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 125 I am your servant; instruct me,
\q2 and I shall know your testimonies.
\q1 \v 126 \add It is\add* time for the Lord to work:
\q2 they have utterly broken your law.
\q1 \v 127 Therefore have I loved your commandments more than gold,
\q2 or the topaz.
\q1 \v 128 Therefore I directed myself \add according\add* to all your commandments:
\q2 I have hated every unjust way.
\qa פ
\qa PE
\b
\q1 \v 129 Your testimonies are wonderful:
\q2 therefore my soul has sought them out.
\q1 \v 130 The manifestation of your words will enlighten,
\q2 and instruct the simple.
\q1 \v 131 I opened my mouth, and drew breath:
\q2 for I earnestly longed after your commandments.
\q1 \v 132 Look upon me and have mercy upon me,
\q2 after the manner of them who love your name.
\q1 \v 133 Order my steps according to your word:
\q2 and let not any iniquity have dominion over me.
\q1 \v 134 Deliver me from the false accusation of men:
\q2 so will I keep your commandments.
\q1 \v 135 Cause your face to shine upon your servant:
\q2 and teach me yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 136 My eyes have been bathed in streams of water,
\q2 because I kept not your law.
\qa צ
\qa TZADE
\b
\q1 \v 137 Righteous are you, O Lord,
\q2 and upright are your judgments.
\q1 \v 138 You have commanded righteousness
\q2 and perfect truth, \add as\add* your testimonies.
\q1 \v 139 Your zeal has quite wasted me:
\q2 because my enemies have forgotten your words.
\q1 \v 140 Your word \add has been\add* very fully tried;
\q2 and your servant loves it.
\q1 \v 141 I am young and despised: \add yet\add* I have not forgotten yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 142 Your righteousness is an everlasting righteousness,
\q2 and your law is truth.
\q1 \v 143 Afflictions and distresses found me: \add but\add* your commandments \add were\add* my meditation.
\q1 \v 144 Your testimonies \add are\add* an everlasting righteousness:
\q2 instruct me, and I shall live.
\qa ק
\qa KOPH
\b
\q1 \v 145 I cried with my whole heart; hear me, O Lord:
\q2 I will search out yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 146 I cried to you; save me,
\q2 and I will keep your testimonies.
\q1 \v 147 I arose before the dawn, and cried:
\q2 I hoped in your words.
\q1 \v 148 My eyes prevented the dawn,
\q2 that I might meditate on yours oracles.
\q1 \v 149 Hear my voice, O Lord, according to your mercy;
\q2 quicken me according to your judgment.
\q1 \v 150 They have drawn near who persecuted me unlawfully;
\q2 and they are far removed from your law.
\q1 \v 151 You are near, O Lord;
\q2 and all your ways are truth.\f + \fr 118:151 \fqa Alex. \ft ἐντολαί, commands.\f*
\q1 \v 152 I have known of old concerning your testimonies,\f + \fr 118:152 \fqa Gr. \ft of.\f*
\q2 that you have founded them for ever.
\qa ר
\qa RESH
\b
\q1 \v 153 Look upon my affliction, and rescue me;
\q2 for I have not forgotten your law.
\q1 \v 154 Plead my cause, and ransom me:
\q2 quicken me because of your word.
\q1 \v 155 Salvation is far from sinners:
\q2 for they have not searched out yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 156 Your mercies, O Lord, are many:
\q2 quicken me according to your judgment.
\q1 \v 157 Many are they who persecute me and oppress me: \add but\add* I have not declined from your testimonies.
\q1 \v 158 I beheld men acting foolishly, and I pined away;
\q2 for they kept not yours oracles.
\q1 \v 159 Behold, I have loved your commandments, O Lord:
\q2 quicken me in your mercy.
\q1 \v 160 The beginning of your words is truth;
\q2 and all the judgments of your righteousness \add endure\add* for ever.
\qa ש
\qa SIN and SHIN
\b
\q1 \v 161 Princes persecuted me without a cause,
\q2 but my heart feared because of your words.
\q1 \v 162 I will exult because of yours oracles,
\q2 as one that finds much spoil.
\q1 \v 163 I hate and abhor unrighteousness;
\q2 but I love your law.
\q1 \v 164 Seven times in a day have I praised you
\q2 because of the judgments of your righteousness.
\q1 \v 165 Great peace have they who love your law:
\q2 and there is no stumbling-block to them.
\q1 \v 166 I waited for your salvation, O Lord,
\q2 and have loved your commandments.
\q1 \v 167 My soul has kept your testimonies,
\q2 and loved them exceedingly.
\q1 \v 168 I have kept your commandments and your testimonies;
\q2 for all my ways are before you, O Lord.
\qa ת
\qa TAW
\b
\q1 \v 169 Let my supplication come near before you, O Lord;
\q2 instruct me according to yours oracle.
\q1 \v 170 Let my petition come in before you, O Lord;
\q2 deliver me according to yours oracle.
\q1 \v 171 Let my lips utter a hymn,
\q2 when you shall have taught me yours ordinances.
\q1 \v 172 Let my tongue utter yours oracles;
\q2 for all your commandments are righteous.
\q1 \v 173 Let yours hand be \add prompt\add* to save me;
\q2 for I have chosen your commandments.
\q1 \v 174 I have longed after your salvation, O Lord;
\q2 and your law is my meditation.
\q1 \v 175 My soul shall live, and shall praise you;
\q2 and your judgments shall help me.
\q1 \v 176 I have gone astray like a lost sheep;
\q2 seek your servant; for I have not forgotten your commandments.
\c 119
\d \v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p
\q1 In my affliction I cried to the Lord,
\q2 and he hearkened to me.
\q1 \v 2 Deliver my soul, O Lord,
\q2 from unjust lips, and from a deceitful tongue.
\b
\q1 \v 3 What should be given to you,
\q2 and what should be added to you, for \add your\add* crafty tongue?
\q1 \v 4 Sharpened weapons of the mighty,
\q2 with coals of the desert.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged;\f + \fr 119:5 \fqa Heb. \ft משך.\f*
\q2 I have tabernacled among the tents of Kedar.
\q1 \v 6 My soul has long
\q2 been a sojourner;
\q1 \v 7 I was peaceable among them who hated peace;
\q2 when I spoke to them, they warred against me without a cause.
\c 120
\d \v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p
\q1 I lifted up my eyes to the mountains,
\q2 from where my help shall come.
\q1 \v 2 My help \add shall come\add* from the Lord,
\q2 who made the heaven and the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Let not your foot be moved;
\q2 and let not your keeper slumber.
\q1 \v 4 Behold, He who keeps Israel
\q2 shall not slumber nor sleep.
\b
\q1 \v 5 The Lord shall keep you:
\q2 the Lord is your shelter upon your right hand.
\q1 \v 6 The sun shall not burn you by day,
\q2 neither the moon by night.
\b
\q1 \v 7 May the Lord preserve you from all evil:
\q2 the Lord shall keep your soul.
\q1 \v 8 The Lord shall keep your coming in,
\q2 and your going out, from henceforth and even for ever.
\c 121  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p I was glad when they said to me, Let us go into the house of the Lord.  
\v 2 Our feet stood in your courts, O Jerusalem.  
\v 3 Jerusalem is built as a city whose fellowship is complete.  
\v 4 For there the tribes went up, the tribes of the Lord, as a testimony for Israel, to give thanks to the name of the Lord.  
\v 5 For there are set thrones for judgment, \add even\add* thrones for the house of David.   
\p
\v 6 \f + \fr 121:6 \fqa Or, \ft ask Jerusalem how it is with her.\f*Pray now for the peace of Jerusalem: and \add let there be\add* prosperity to them who love you.  
\v 7 Let peace, I pray, be within yours host, and prosperity in your palaces.  
\v 8 For the sake of my brothers and my neighbours, I have indeed spoken peace concerning you.  
\v 9 Because of the house of the Lord our God, I have diligently sought your good.   
\c 122  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p To you who dwell in heaven have I lifted up my eyes.  
\v 2 Behold, as the eyes of servants \add are directed\add* to the hands of their masters, \add and\add* as the eyes of a maid-servant to the hands of her mistress; so our eyes \add are directed\add* to the Lord our God, until he have mercy upon us.  
\v 3 Have pity upon us, O Lord, have pity upon us: for we are exceedingly filled with contempt.  
\v 4 \add Yes\add*, our soul has been exceedingly filled \add with it\add*: \f + \fr 122:4 \fqa Or, \ft we are the reproach of them who are at ease.\f*\add let\add* the reproach \add be\add* to them who are at ease, and contempt to the proud.   
\c 123  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p If it had not been that the Lord was among us, let Israel now say;  
\v 2 if it had not been that the Lord was among us, when men rose up against us;  
\v 3 truly they would have swallowed us up alive, when their wrath was kindled against us:  
\v 4 truly the water would have drowned us, our soul would have gone under the torrent.  
\v 5 Yes, our soul would have gone under the overwhelming water.   
\p
\v 6 Blessed be the Lord, who has not given us for a prey to their teeth.  
\v 7 Our soul has been delivered as a sparrow from the snare of the fowlers: the snare is broken, and we are delivered.  
\v 8 Our help is in the name of the Lord, who made heaven and earth.   
\c 124  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p They who trust in the Lord \add shall be\add* as mount Zion: He who dwells in Jerusalem shall never be moved.  
\v 2 The mountains are round about her, and \add so\add* the Lord is round about his people, from henceforth and even for ever.  
\v 3 For the Lord will not allow the rod of sinners to be upon the lot of the righteous; lest the righteous should stretch forth their hands to iniquity.   
\p
\v 4 Do good, O Lord, to them \add that are\add* good, and to them \add that are\add* upright in heart.  
\v 5 But them who turn aside to crooked ways the Lord will lead away with the workers of iniquity: \f + \fr 124:5 \fqa Or, \ft let peace be.\f*\add but\add* peace \add shall be\add* upon Israel.   
\c 125  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p When the Lord turned the captivity of Zion, we became as comforted ones.  
\v 2 Then was our mouth filled with joy, and our tongue with exultation: then would they say among the Gentiles,  
\v 3 The Lord has done great things among them. The Lord has done great things for us, we became joyful.   
\p
\v 4 Turn, O Lord, our captivity, as the streams in the south.  
\v 5 They who sow in tears shall reap in joy.  
\v 6 They went on and wept as they cast their seeds; but they shall surely come with exultation, bringing their sheaves \add with them\add*.   
\c 126  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p Except the Lord build the house, they who build labour in vain: except the Lord keep the city, the watchman watches in vain.  
\v 2 It is vain for you to rise early: you rise up after resting, you that eat the bread of grief; while he gives sleep to his beloved.   
\p
\v 3 Behold, the inheritance of the Lord, children, the reward of the fruit of the womb.  
\v 4 As arrows in the hand of a mighty man; so are the children of those who were outcasts.  
\v 5 Blessed is the man who shall satisfy his desire with them: they shall not be ashamed when they shall speak to their enemies in the gates.   
\c 127  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p Blessed are all they who fear the Lord; who walk in his ways.  
\v 2 You shall eat the \f + \fr 127:2 \fqa Alex. \ft fruits of your labours.\f*labours of your hands: blessed are you, and it shall be well with you.  
\v 3 Your wife shall be as a fruitful vine on the sides of your house: your children as young olive-plants round about your table.   
\p
\v 4 Behold, thus shall the man be blessed that fears the Lord.  
\v 5 May the Lord bless you out of Zion; and may you see the prosperity of Jerusalem all the days of your life.  
\v 6 And may you see your children's children. Peace be upon Israel.   
\c 128  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p Many a time have they warred against me from my youth, let Israel now say:  
\v 2 Many a time have they warred against me from my youth: and yet they prevailed not against me.  
\v 3 The sinners worked upon my back: they prolonged their \f + \fr 128:3 \ft שיך iniquity, easily read for שוכה a furrow.\f*iniquity.  
\v 4 The righteous Lord has cut apart the necks of sinners.   
\p
\v 5 Let all that hate Zion be put to shame and turned back.  
\v 6 Let them be as the grass of the house-tops, which withers before it is plucked up.  
\v 7 Wherewith the reaper fills not his hand, nor He who makes up the sheaves, his bosom.  
\v 8 Neither do they who go by say, The blessing of the Lord be upon you: we have blessed you in the name of the Lord.   
\c 129  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p Out of the depths have I cried to you, O Lord.  
\v 2 O Lord, listen to my voice; let yours ears be attentive to the voice of my supplication.  
\v 3 If you, O Lord, should mark iniquities, O Lord, who shall stand?  
\v 4 For with you is \f + \fr 129:4 \fqa Or, \ft propitiation.\f*forgiveness: for your \f + \fr 129:4 \ft See \xt Gen. 31. 53.\f*name's sake  
\v 5 have I waited for you, O Lord, my soul has waited for your word.  
\v 6 My soul has hoped in the Lord; from the morning watch till night.  
\v 7 Let Israel hope in the Lord: for with the Lord is mercy, and with him is plenteous redemption.  
\v 8 And he shall redeem Israel from all his iniquities.   
\c 130  
\d
\v 1 \f + \fr 130:1 \fqa Alex. \ft + 'for David.'\f*A Song of Degrees.  
\p O Lord, my heart is not exalted, neither have my eyes been \add haughtily\add* raised: neither have I exercised myself in great \add matters\add*, nor in things too wonderful for me.  
\v 2 \add I shall have sinned\add* if I have not been humble, but have exalted my soul: according to \add the relation of\add* a weaned child to his mother, so will you recompense my soul.  
\v 3 Let Israel hope in the Lord, from henceforth and for ever.   
\c 131  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p Lord, remember David, and all his meekness:  
\v 2 how he swore to the Lord, \add and\add* vowed to the God of Jacob, \add saying\add*,  
\v 3 \f + \fr 131:3 \fqa Gr. \ft if I shall, etc. \fqa Hebraism.\f*I will not go into the tabernacle of my house; I will not go up to the couch of my bed;  
\v 4 I will not give sleep to my eyes, nor slumber to my eyelids, nor rest to my temples,  
\v 5 until I find a place for the Lord, a tabernacle for the God of Jacob.  
\v 6 Behold, we heard of it in Ephratha; we found it in the fields of the wood.  
\v 7 Let us enter into his tabernacles: let us worship at the place where his feet stood.   
\p
\v 8 Arise, O Lord, into your rest; you, and the ark of yours holiness.  
\v 9 Your priests shall clothe themselves with righteousness; and your saints shall exult.  
\v 10 For the sake of your servant David turn not away the face of yours anointed.   
\p
\v 11 The Lord swore \add in\add* truth to David, and he will not annul it, \add saying\add*, Of the fruit of your body will I set \add a king\add* upon your throne.  
\v 12 If your children will keep my covenant, and these my testimonies which I shall teach them, their children also shall sit upon your throne for ever.  
\v 13 For the Lord has elected Zion, he has chosen her for a habitation for himself, \add saying\add*,  
\v 14 This is my rest for ever: here will I dwell; for I have chosen it.  
\v 15 I will surely bless her provision: I will satisfy her poor with bread.  
\v 16 I will clothe her priests with salvation; and her saints shall greatly exult.  
\v 17 There will I cause to spring up a horn to David: I have prepared a lamp for my anointed.  
\v 18 His enemies will I clothe with shame; but upon himself shall my \f + \fr 131:18 \ft See \xt Ps. 88. 39.\f*holiness flourish.   
\c 132  
\d
\v 1 \f + \fr 132:1 \fqa Alex. \ft + 'for David.'\f*A Song of Degrees.  
\p See now! what is so good, or what so pleasant, as for brothers to dwell together?  
\v 2 \add It is\add* as ointment on the head, that ran down to the beard, \add even\add* the beard of Aaron; that ran down to the fringe of his clothing.  
\v 3 As the dew of Aermon, that comes down on the mountains of Zion: for there the Lord commanded the blessing, \add even\add* life for ever.   
\c 133  
\d
\v 1 A Song of Degrees.  
\p Behold now, bless you the Lord, all the servants of the Lord, who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God.  
\v 2 Lift up your hands by night \f + \fr 133:2 \fqa Or, \ft to the holy places.\f*in the sanctuaries, and bless the Lord.  
\v 3 May the Lord, who made heaven and earth, bless you out of Zion.   
\c 134  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia.  
\p Praise you the name of the Lord; praise the Lord, \add you his\add* servants,  
\v 2 who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God.  
\v 3 Praise you the Lord; for the Lord is good: sing praises to his name; for \add it is\add* good.   
\p
\v 4 For the Lord has chosen Jacob for himself, \add and\add* Israel for his peculiar treasure.  
\v 5 For I know that the Lord is great, and our Lord is above all gods;  
\v 6 all that the Lord willed, he did in heaven, and on the earth, in the sea, and in all deeps.  
\v 7 Who brings up clouds from the extremity of the earth: he has made lightnings for the rain: he brings winds out of his treasures.  
\v 8 Who struck the first-born of Egypt, both of man and beast.  
\v 9 He sent signs and wonders into the midst of you, O Egypt, on Pharaoh, and on all his servants.  
\v 10 Who struck many nations, and slew mighty kings;  
\v 11 Seon king of the Amorites, and Og king of Bashan, and all the kingdoms of Canaan:  
\v 12 and gave their land \add for\add* an inheritance, an inheritance to Israel his people.   
\p
\v 13 O Lord, your name \add endures\add* for ever, and your memorial to all generations.  
\v 14 For the Lord shall judge his people, and comfort himself concerning his servants.  
\v 15 The idols of the heathen are silver and gold, the works of men's hands.  
\v 16 They have a mouth, but they cannot speak; they have eyes, but they cannot see;  
\v 17 they have ears, but they cannot hear; for there is no breath in their mouth.  
\v 18 Let those who make them be made like to them; and all those who trust in them.   
\p
\v 19 O house of Israel, bless you the Lord: O house of Aaron, bless you the Lord:  
\v 20 O house of Levi, bless you the Lord: you that fear the Lord, bless the Lord.  
\v 21 Blessed in Zion be the Lord, who dwells in Jerusalem.   
\c 135  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia.  
\p Give thanks to the Lord: for he is good: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 2 Give thanks to the God of gods: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 3 Give thanks to the Lord of lords: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.   
\p
\v 4 To him who alone has worked great wonders: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 5 To him who made the heavens by understanding; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 6 To him who established the earth on the waters; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 7 To him who alone made great lights; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 8 The sun to rule the day; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 9 The moon and the stars to rule the night; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.   
\p
\v 10 To him who struck Egypt with their first-born; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 11 And brought Israel out of the midst of them; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 12 with a strong hand, and a high arm: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 13 To him who divided the Red Sea into parts: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 14 and brought Israel through the midst of it: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 15 and overthrew Pharaoh and his host in the Red Sea: for his mercy endures for ever.  
\v 16 To him who led his people through the wilderness: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.   
\p
\v 17 To him who struck great kings: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 18 and slew mighty kings; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 19 Seon king of the Amorites: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 20 and Og king of Bashan: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 21 and gave their land \add for\add* an inheritance: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 22 even an inheritance to Israel his servant: for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.   
\p
\v 23 For the Lord remembered us in our low estate; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever:  
\v 24 and redeemed us from our enemies; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 25 Who gives food to all flesh; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.  
\v 26 Give thanks to the God of heaven; for his mercy \add endures\add* for ever.   
\c 136  
\d
\v 1 \f + \fr 136:1 \fqa Alex. \ft omits.\f*For David, \add a Psalm\add* of Jeremiah.  
\p By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat; and wept when we remembered Zion.  
\v 2 We hung our \f + \fr 136:2 \fqa Gr. \ft organs, \fqa or, \ft instruments.\f*harps on the willows in the midst of it.  
\v 3 For there they who had taken us captive asked of us the words of a song; and they who had carried us away \add asked\add* a hymn, \add saying\add*, Sing us \add one\add* of the songs of Zion.   
\p
\v 4 How should we sing the Lord's song \f + \fr 136:4 \fqa Gr. \ft on.\f*in a strange land?  
\v 5 If I forget you, O Jerusalem, let my right hand forget \add its skill\add*.  
\v 6 May my tongue cleave to my throat, if I do not remember you; if I do not prefer Jerusalem as the chief of my joy.   
\p
\v 7 Remember, O Lord, the children of Edom in the day of Jerusalem; who said, Rase \add it\add*, \f + \fr 136:7 \fqa Gr. \ft empty, empty.\f*rase \add it\add*, even to its foundations.  
\v 8 Wretched daughter of Babylon! blessed \add shall he be\add* who shall reward you as you have rewarded us.  
\v 9 Blessed \add shall he be\add* who shall seize and dash yours infants against the rock.   
\c 137  
\d
\v 1 A Psalm for David, of Aggæus and Zacharias.  
\p I will give you thanks, O Lord, with my whole heart; and I will sing psalms to you before the angels; for you have heard all the words of my mouth.  
\v 2 I will worship toward your holy temple, and give thanks to your name, on account of your mercy and your truth; for you have magnified your holy name above every thing.  
\v 3 In whatever day I shall call upon you, hear me quickly; you shall abundantly provide me with your power in my soul.  
\v 4 Let all the kings of the earth, O Lord, give thanks to you; for they have heard all the words of your mouth.  
\v 5 And let them sing in the ways of the Lord; for great is the glory of the Lord.   
\p
\v 6 For the Lord is high, and \add yet\add* regards the lowly; and he knows high things from afar off.  
\v 7 Though I should walk in the midst of affliction, you will quicken me; you have stretched forth yours hands against the wrath of my enemies, and your right hand has saved me.  
\v 8 O Lord, you shall recompense \add them\add* on my behalf: your mercy, O Lord, \add endures\add* for ever: overlook not the works of yours hands.   
\c 138  
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.\f + \fr 138:1 \fqa Alex. \ft + of Zacharias in the dispersion.\f*  
\p O Lord, you have proved me, and known me.  
\v 2 You know my down-sitting and my up-rising: you understand my thoughts \f + \fr 138:2 \fqa Gr. \ft from afar.\f*long before.  
\v 3 You have traced my path and my \f + \fr 138:3 \fqa q. d. \ft of rushes, \fqa lit. \ft rush.\f*bed, and have foreseen all my ways.  
\v 4 For there is no unrighteous word in my tongue: behold, O Lord, you have known all things,  
\v 5 the last and the first: you have fashioned me, and laid yours hand upon me.   
\p
\v 6 The knowledge of you is too wonderful for me; it is very difficult, I cannot \add attain\add* to it.  
\v 7 Where shall I go from your Spirit? and where shall I flee from your presence?  
\v 8 If I should go up to heaven, you are there: if I should go down to hell, you are present.  
\v 9 If I should spread my wings \add to fly\add* \f + \fr 138:9 \fqa Alex. \ft κατ᾿ ὄρθον, toward the dawn.\f*straight forward, and stay temporarily at the extremity of the sea, \add it would be vain\add*,  
\v 10 for even there your hand would guide me, and your right hand would hold me.  
\v 11 When I said, Surely the darkness will cover me; even the night \add was\add* light in my \f + \fr 138:11 \ft Comp. \fqa Heb.\f*luxury.  
\v 12 For darkness will not be darkness with you; but night will be light as day: as its darkness, so shall its light \add be to you\add*.  
\v 13 For you, O Lord, have possessed my reins; you have helped me from my mother's womb.  
\v 14 I will give you thanks; for you are fearfully wondrous; wondrous are your works; and my soul knows \add it\add* well.  
\v 15 My bones which you made in secret were not hidden from you, nor my substance, in the lowest parts of the earth.  
\v 16 Your eyes saw my unwrought \add substance\add*, and all \add men\add* shall be written in your book; they shall be formed by day, though \add there should for a time\add* be no one among them.   
\p
\v 17 But your friends, O God, have been greatly honoured by me; their rule has been greatly strengthened.  
\v 18 I will number them, and they shall be multiplied beyond the sand; I awake, and am still with you.   
\p
\v 19 Oh that you would slay the wicked, O God; depart from me, you men of blood.  
\v 20 For you will say concerning \add their\add* thought, \add that\add* they shall take your cities in vain.  
\v 21 Have I not hated them, O Lord, that hate you? and wasted away because of yours enemies?  
\v 22 I have hated them with perfect hatred; they were counted my enemies.  
\v 23 Prove me, O God, and know my heart; examine me, and know my paths;  
\v 24 and see if \add there is any\add* way of iniquity in me, and lead me in an everlasting way.   
\c 139  
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.   
\p
\v 2 Rescue me, O Lord, from the evil man; deliver me from the unjust man.  
\v 3 Who have devised injustice in their hearts; all the day they prepared war.  
\v 4 They have sharpened their tongue as \add the tongue\add* of a serpent; \x + \xo 139:4 \xt Rom. 3. 13.\x* the poison of asps is under their lips. Pause.  
\v 5 Keep me, O Lord, from the hand of the sinner; rescue me from unjust men; who have purposed to overthrow my goings.  
\v 6 The proud have hid a snare for me, and have stretched out ropes \add for\add* snares for my feet; they set a stumbling-block for me near the path. Pause.   
\p
\v 7 I said to the Lord, You are my God; listen, O Lord, to the voice of my supplication.  
\v 8 O Lord God, the strength of my salvation; you have screened my head in the day of battle.  
\v 9 Deliver me not, O Lord, to the sinner, according to my desire: they have devised \add mischief\add* against me; forsake me not, lest they should be exalted. Pause.   
\p
\v 10 \add As for\add* the head of them who compass me, the mischief of their lips shall cover them.  
\v 11 Coals of fire shall fall upon them on the earth; and you shall cast them down in afflictions: they shall not bear up \add under them\add*.  
\v 12 A talkative man shall not prosper on the earth: evils shall hunt the unrighteous man to destruction.  
\v 13 I know that the Lord will maintain the cause of the \f + \fr 139:13 \fqa Gr. \ft singular.\f*poor, and the right of the needy ones.  
\v 14 Surely the righteous shall give thanks to your name: the upright shall dwell in your presence.   
\c 140  
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David.  
\p O Lord, I have cried to you; hear me: attend to the voice of my supplication, when I cry to you.  
\v 2 Let my prayer be set forth before you as incense; the lifting up of my hands \add as\add* an evening sacrifice.  
\v 3 Set a watch, O Lord, on my mouth, and a \f + \fr 140:3 \fqa Lit. \ft a door of fortification.\f*strong door about my lips.  
\v 4 Incline not my heart to evil things, to \f + \fr 140:4 \fqa Gr. \ft pretend pretences.\f*employ pretexts \f + \fr 140:4 \fqa Gr. \ft in.\f*for sins, with men who work iniquity: and \f + \fr 140:4 \fqa Gr. \ft I will not.\f*let me not unite with their choice ones.  
\v 5 The righteous shall chasten me with mercy, and reprove me: but let not the oil of the sinner anoint my head: for yet shall my prayer also be in their \f + \fr 140:5 \fqa q. d. \ft lest I be injured by them.\f*pleasures.   
\p
\v 6 Their mighty ones have been swallowed up near the rock: they shall hear my words, for they are sweet.  
\v 7 As a lump of earth is crushed upon the ground, our bones have been scattered by the \add mouth of\add* the grave.  
\v 8 For my eyes are to you, O Lord God: I have hoped in you; take not away my life.  
\v 9 Keep me from the snare which they have set for me, and from the stumbling-blocks of them who work iniquity.  
\v 10 Sinners shall fall by their own net: I am alone until I shall escape.   
\c 141  
\d
\v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of instruction for David, when he was in the cave,—a Prayer.   
\p
\v 2 I cried to the Lord with my voice; with my voice I made supplication to the Lord.  
\v 3 I will pour out before him my supplication: I will declare before him my affliction.  
\v 4 When my spirit was fainting within me, then you knew my paths; in the very way wherein I was walking, they hid a snare for me.  
\v 5 I looked on \add my\add* right hand, and behold, for there was none that noticed me; refuge failed me; and there was none that cared for my soul.  
\v 6 I cried to you, O Lord, and said, You are my hope, my portion in the land of the living.  
\v 7 Attend to my supplication, for I am brought very low; deliver me from them who persecute me; for they are stronger than I.  
\v 8 Bring my soul out of prison, that I may give thanks to your name, O Lord; the righteous shall wait for me, until you recompense me.   
\c 142  
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David, when his son pursued him.  
\p O Lord, attend to my prayer: listen to my supplication in your truth; hear me in your righteousness.  
\v 2 And enter not into judgment with your servant, for in your sight shall no \add man\add* living be justified.   
\p
\v 3 For the enemy has persecuted my soul; he has brought my life down to the ground; he has made me to dwell in a dark \add place\add*, as those that have been long dead.  
\v 4 Therefore my spirit was grieved in me; my heart was troubled within me.  
\v 5 I remembered the days of old; and I meditated on all your doings: \add yes\add*, I meditated on the works of yours hands.  
\v 6 I spread forth my hands to you; my soul \add thirsts\add* for you, as a dry land. Pause.   
\p
\v 7 Hear me quickly, O Lord; my spirit has failed; turn not away your face from me, else I shall be like to them who go down to the pit.  
\v 8 Cause me to hear your mercy in the morning; for I have hoped in you; make known to me, O Lord, the way wherein I should walk; for I have lifted up my soul to you.  
\v 9 Deliver me from my enemies, O Lord; for I have fled to you for refuge.  
\v 10 Teach me to do your will; for you are my God; your good Spirit shall guide me in the \f + \fr 142:10 \fqa Alex. \ft as \fqa Heb. \ft 'land of uprightness.'\f*straight \add way\add*.  
\v 11 You shall quicken me, O Lord, for your name's sake; in your righteousness you shall bring my soul out of affliction.  
\v 12 And in your mercy you will destroy my enemies, and will destroy all those that afflict my soul; for I am your servant.   
\c 143  
\d
\v 1 \add A Psalm\add* of David concerning Goliad.  
\p Blessed \add be\add* the Lord my God, who instructs my hands for battle, \add and\add* my fingers for war.  
\v 2 My mercy, and my refuge; my helper, and my deliverer; my protector, in whom I have trusted; who subdues my people under me.   
\p
\v 3 Lord, what is man, that you are made known to him? or the son of man, that you take account of him?  
\v 4 Man is like to vanity: his days pass as a shadow.   
\p
\v 5 O Lord, bow your heavens, and come down: touch the mountains, and they shall smoke.  
\v 6 Send lightning, and you shall scatter them: send forth yours arrows, and you shall discomfit them.  
\v 7 Send forth yours hand from on high; rescue me, and deliver me out of great \f + \fr 143:7 \fqa Gr. \ft many.\f*waters, out of the hand of strange children;  
\v 8 whose mouth has spoken vanity, and their right hand is a right hand of iniquity.   
\p
\v 9 O God, I will sing a new song to you: I will play to you on a psaltery of ten strings.  
\v 10 \add Even\add* to him who gives \f + \fr 143:10 \fqa Or, \ft victory.\f*salvation to kings: who redeems his servant David from the hurtful sword.  
\v 11 Deliver me, and rescue me from the hand of strange children, whose mouth has spoken vanity, and their right hand is a right hand of iniquity;  
\v 12 whose children are as plants, strengthened in their youth: their daughters are beautiful, sumptuously adorned after the similitude of a temple.  
\v 13 Their garners are full, and bursting with one kind of store after another; their sheep are prolific, multiplying in their streets.  
\v 14 Their oxen are fat: there is no falling down of a hedge, nor going out, nor cry in their \f + \fr 143:14 \fqa Or, \ft habitations.\f*folds.  
\v 15 Men bless the people to whom this lot belongs, \add but\add* blessed is the people whose God is the Lord.   
\c 144  
\d
\v 1 David's \add Psalm of\add* praise.  
\p I will exalt you, my God, my king; and I will bless your name \f + \fr 144:1 \fqa Lit. \ft to the age, and to the age of the age.\f*for ever and ever.  
\v 2 Every day will I bless you, and I will praise your name \f + \fr 144:2 \fqa Lit. \ft to the age, and to the age of the age.\f*for ever and ever.  
\v 3 The Lord is great, and greatly to be praised; and there is no end of his greatness.  
\v 4 Generation after generation shall praise your works, and tell of your power.  
\v 5 And they shall speak of the glorious majesty of your holiness, and recount your wonders.  
\v 6 And they shall speak of the power of your terrible \add acts\add*; and recount your greatness.  
\v 7 They shall utter the memory of the abundance of your goodness, and shall exult in your righteousness.   
\p
\v 8 The Lord is compassionate, and merciful; long-suffering, and abundant in mercy.  
\v 9 The Lord is good to those that wait \add on him\add*; and his compassions are over all his works.  
\v 10 Let all your works, O Lord, give thanks to you; and let your saints bless you.  
\v 11 They shall speak of the glory of your kingdom, and talk of your dominion;  
\v 12 to make known to the sons of men your power, and the glorious majesty of your kingdom.  
\v 13 Your kingdom is \f + \fr 144:13 \fqa Gr. \ft a kingdom of all ages.\f*an everlasting kingdom, and your dominion \add endures\add* \f + \fr 144:13 \fqa Gr. \ft in every generation and generation.\f*through all generations.  
\v 13a The Lord is faithful in his words, and holy in all his works.   
\p
\v 14 The Lord supports all that are falling, and sets up all that are broken down.  
\v 15 The eyes of all wait upon you; and you give \add them\add* their food in due season.  
\v 16 You open yours hands, and fill every living thing with pleasure.  
\v 17 The Lord is righteous in all his ways, and holy in all his works.   
\p
\v 18 The Lord is near to all that call upon him, to all that call upon him in truth.  
\v 19 He will perform the desire of them who fear him: and he will hear their supplication, and save them.  
\v 20 The Lord preserves all that love him: but all sinners he will utterly destroy.  
\v 21 My mouth shall speak the praise of the Lord: and let all flesh bless his holy name for ever and ever.   
\c 145  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia, \add a Psalm\add* of Aggæus and Zacharias.  
\p My soul, praise the Lord.  
\v 2 While I live will I praise the Lord: I will sing praises to my God as long as I exist.  
\v 3 Trust not in princes, nor in the children of men, in whom there is no safety.  
\v 4 His breath shall go forth, and he shall return to his earth; in that day all his thoughts shall perish.   
\p
\v 5 Blessed is he whose helper is the God of Jacob, whose hope is in the Lord his God:  
\v 6 who made heaven, and earth, the sea, and all things in them: who keeps truth for ever:  
\v 7 who executes judgment for the wronged: who gives food to the hungry. The Lord looses the fettered ones:  
\v 8 the Lord gives wisdom to the blind:  
\p The Lord sets up the broken down: the Lord loves the righteous: the Lord preserves the strangers;  
\v 9 he will relieve the orphan and widow: but will utterly remove the way of sinners.  
\v 10 The Lord shall reign for ever, \add even\add* your God, O Zion, to all generations.   
\c 146  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia, \add a Psalm\add* of Aggæus and Zacharias.  
\p Praise you the Lord: for psalmody is a good thing; let praise be sweetly sung to our God.  
\v 2 The Lord builds up Jerusalem; and he will gather together the dispersed of Israel.  
\v 3 He heals the broken in heart, and binds up their wounds.  
\v 4 He numbers the multitudes of stars; and calls them all by names.  
\v 5 Great is our Lord, and great is his strength; and his understanding is infinite.  
\v 6 The Lord lifts up the meek; but brings sinners down to the ground.   
\p
\v 7 Begin \add the song\add* with thanksgiving to the Lord; sing praises on the harp to our God:  
\v 8 who covers the heaven with clouds, who prepares rain for the earth, who causes grass to spring up on the mountains, [\f + \fr 146:8 \ft See Ps \xt 103. 14.\f*and green herb for the service of men;]  
\v 9 and gives to cattle their food, and to the young ravens that call upon him.  
\v 10 He will not take pleasure in the strength of a horse; nor is he well-pleased with the legs of a man.  
\v 11 The Lord takes pleasure in them who fear him, and in all that hope in his mercy.   
\c 147  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia, \add a Psalm\add* of Aggæus and Zacharias.\f + \fr 147:1 \ft In the original text, the following verses are numbered 12 to 20.\f*  
\p Praise the Lord, O Jerusalem; praise your God, O Zion.  
\v 2 For he has strengthened the bars of your gates; he has blessed your children within you.  
\v 3 He makes your borders peaceful, and fills you with the \f + \fr 147:3 \fqa Lit. \ft fat of wheat.\f*flour of wheat.  
\v 4 He sends his oracle to the earth: his word will run swiftly.  
\v 5 He gives snow like wool: he scatters the mist like ashes.  
\v 6 Casting \add forth\add* his ice like morsels: who shall stand before his cold?  
\v 7 He shall send out his word, and melt them: he shall blow \add with\add* his wind, and the waters shall flow.  
\v 8 He sends his word to Jacob, his ordinances and judgments to Israel.  
\v 9 He has not done so to \f + \fr 147:9 \fqa Gr. \ft every.\f*any \add other\add* nation; and he has not shewn them his judgments.   
\c 148  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia, \add a Psalm\add* of Aggæus and Zacharias.  
\p Praise you the Lord from the heavens: praise him in the highest.  
\v 2 Praise you him, all his angels: praise you him, all his hosts.  
\v 3 Praise him, sun and moon: praise him, all you stars and light.  
\v 4 Praise him, you heavens of heavens, and the water that is above the heavens.  
\v 5 Let them praise the name of the Lord: for he spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created.  
\v 6 He has established them for ever, even for ever and ever: he has made an ordinance, and it shall not pass away.   
\p
\v 7 Praise the Lord from the earth, you serpents, and all deeps.  
\v 8 Fire, hail, snow, ice, stormy wind; the things that perform his word.  
\v 9 Mountains, and all hills; fruitful trees, and all cedars:  
\v 10 wild beasts, and all cattle; reptiles, and winged birds:  
\v 11 kings of the earth, and all peoples; princes, and all judges of the earth:  
\v 12 young men and virgins, old men with youths:  
\v 13 let them praise the name of the Lord: for his name only is exalted; his praise is above the earth and heaven,  
\v 14 and he shall exalt the horn of his people, \f + \fr 148:14 \fqa Or, \ft who is the praise of.\f*\add there is\add* a hymn for all his saints, \add even\add* of the children of Israel, a people who draw near to him.   
\c 149  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia.  
\p Sing to the Lord a new song: his praise is in the assembly of the saints.  
\v 2 Let Israel rejoice in him that made him; and let the children of Zion exult in their king.  
\v 3 Let them praise his name in the dance: let them sing praises to him with timbrel and psaltery.  
\v 4 For the Lord takes pleasure in his people; and will exalt the meek with salvation.   
\p
\v 5 The saints shall rejoice in glory; and shall exult on their beds.  
\v 6 The \f + \fr 149:6 \fqa Gr. \ft exaltations, \fqa q. d. \ft extollings.\f*high praises of God shall be in their throat, and two-edged swords in their hands;  
\v 7 to execute vengeance on the nations, \add and\add* punishments among the peoples;  
\v 8 to bind their kings with fetters, and their nobles with manacles of iron;  
\v 9 to execute on them the judgment \f + \fr 149:9 \fqa Gr. \ft inscribed, \fqa sc. \ft in the scripture.\f*written: this honour have all his saints.   
\c 150  
\d
\v 1 Alleluia.  
\p Praise God in his holy places: praise him in the firmament of his power.  
\v 2 Praise him on \add account of\add* his mighty acts: praise him according to his abundant greatness.  
\v 3 Praise him with the sound of a trumpet: praise him with psaltery and harp.  
\v 4 Praise him with timbrel and dance: praise him with stringed instruments and the organ.  
\v 5 Praise him with melodious cymbals: praise him with loud cymbals.  
\v 6 Let every thing that has breath praise the Lord.   
\c 151  
\d
\v 1 This Psalm is a genuine one of David, though supernumerary, \add composed\add* when he fought in single combat with \f + \fr 151:1 \fqa Alex. \ft Goliath.\f*Goliad.  
\p I was small among my brothers, and youngest in my father's house: I tended my father's sheep.  
\v 2 My hands formed a musical instrument, and my fingers tuned a psaltery.  
\v 3 And who shall tell my Lord? the Lord himself, he himself hears.  
\v 4 He sent forth his angel, and took me from my father's sheep, and he anointed me with the oil of his anointing.  
\v 5 My brothers were handsome and tall; but the Lord did not take pleasure in them.  
\v 6 I went forth to meet the Philistine; and he cursed me by his idols.  
\v 7 But I drew his own sword, and beheaded him, and removed reproach from the children of Israel.   